      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>McDOUGAWS PATENT SELF—RAISING FLOUR mAILITTOsTny. Tfq EXAMINER, FRIDAY, AUGUST 10 1900</head>
        <p>McDOUGAWS PATENT SELF—RAISING FLOUR mAILITTOsTny. Tfq EXAMINER, FRIDAY, AUGUST 10 1900</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>HY DEAR NELLIE, I am informed that the newest travelling hats are those of stitched linen, simply trimmed with a twist of ribbon and a quill, but the old round shape is still very fashionable, and others are of light felt or straw, boat-shaped. Anything in the nature of tulle, chiffon, and perishable stuffs should be avoided. A good supply of gloves, fresh veils, ribbons, cravats, and lace, and a few fresh flowers to furbish up a hat which has been robbed of its colouring by the sun are all trifles which take up 'very little room, and are a godsend very often /when extra calls are made upon one's limited sartorial resources. Odds and ends of this sort should certainly be noted down in the traveller's v:tde mecum.</p>
        <p>I really believe that the chiffon ruffle was never more popular than it is at present, and the reason undoubtedly is that such pretty and becoming models have been designed for the season, that no one can resist buying at least one of them. One I saw yesterday was made of white accordion- pleated chiffon, edged with quillings of narrow white gauze ribbon, with a fine black line at either side. The long boa ends are formed of accordion-pleated frills of chiffon, edged with quillings of ribbon, and arranged spirally, so that they taper off towards the end. This pretty ruffle can also be had in plain black, plain white, or in black with a white line at the edge of the quillings. I may here remark that lace insertion is utilised on every gown, entire yokes and sleeves built of nought but finely-stitched bands of delicate fabric.</p>
        <p>Many extremes are shown in fashionable neck arrangements. Many thin walking dresses are made without neck-bands at all, being cut round at the base of the throat and finished with a nar- row lace edging without fulness, but if there is a collar-band at all it is tight and high, and some kind of a ruffle or boa is generally worn out of doors. Cravats of white silk-muslin with edgings of cream or yellow lace, bows of white tulle, and scarves of black lace tied in sailor knots or passed through gold or enamelled rings, are all fashion- able, besides the Pierrot ruffs of black or white muslin and the chiffon boas with many long streamers of satin or velvet ribbon. Throatlets of black velvet with gold or jewelled clasps are fre- quently worn with the low-cut collars, and are very becoming.</p>
        <p>In the matter of skirts, a decided reaction against their inconvenient length appears to have already set in, and it is not at all unusual to see well- dressed women in drill or alpaca skirts that are well off the ground all round. A dust-coloured alpaca, made in this way, looked very well with brown shoes and open-worked stockings to match, For a few days' holiday a white alpaca is prepared, also short enough to clear the ground all round. The quality of the stuff is of the finest and silkiest. The skirt is arranged in pleats all round, the ful- ness from these flowing out just below the knees. The bodice is unlined, for coolness, and is gathered into a yoke of finely tucked muslin and insertion, also unlined and semi-transparent. The alpaca sleeves only reach an inch or two below the elbows, very fine ones of muslin and insertion supplement- ing them from there. Many of the fashionable bell-shaped sleeves are ornamented with appliques of creamy lace, and from inside them falls a puff sleeve of fine creamy lace; the sleeves are lined with pink, which shows slightly when the arm is lifted. The ruffle which is worn with this is composed of loose, pink roses, and it has long ends of accordion-pleated cream chiffon, with rose-petals at the ends. The parasol, generally carried, is also of cream chiffon, edged and trimmed with roses to match the boa. The hat is often made of black tulle, with ehoice black ostrich tips, large rosettes of tulle, and a long jet buckle. This model could be carried out equally well in voile or eolienne, with a silk sash, and it would look quite as pretty in pay) grey, or any other fashionable colour, or in white with a tur- quoise-blue sash.</p>
        <p>In many dresses the fichu is the most striking item, and is composed of palest Wedgwood blue rauslin, gathered into the waistband and bordered with frills. The fichu is one mass of infinitesimally tiny frocks, the fulness of the frill being added on separately. The yoke, also tucked, is cut square and headed with gathered lace. A band of velvet surrounds the slender neck. Pearls are cooler, but not always more becoming. The hat is finest black crinoline straw turned up at the left side With many plumes. It will be noticed that this gown is long all round. This is a way that girls have who lack height. It certainly adds apparent inches to their stature, but it is a most inconvenient fashion, and the front of the hem is apt to get badly used.</p>
        <p>The demi-train still reigns triumphant, except for cycling and yachting dresses, and the severely tailor-made mountaineering suit. Seaside gowns, too, have immunity from the trained skirt. The one a friend has is made of flax-blue Irish linen, a. lovely shade, paler than cornflower, and most becoming to pink cheeks, white brows, and sunny hair. _ .</p>
        <p>Your loving JENNIE.</p>
        <p>A GOOD BROOM-HOLDER may be made by putting two long screws or nails into the wall about 6 feet from the ground. Drop the broom between them handle downwards.</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITiOUS.</p>
        <p>WILL DELIVER FREE AT YOUR DOOR,</p>
