        <p>REAKFAST AND SUPPER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>The late Duke of Saxe-Coburg-Gotha, was not the only :member of the Royal Family whose health has caused considerable anxiety of late. The Empress Frederick is far from strong, Princess Victoria of Wales too, is anything but robust,, and the Dueness of Fife is again ordered complete rest, by her phyaicians, owing to nerve trouble. The young Duke ot Albany, now Doke of Saxe Coburg, is, happily, growing much stronger and sturdier as time goes on, and theugh greatly fatigued by the sad duties imposed upon him at Coburg, bore him- self bravely to the end. The command for general mourning, has been observed on all sides. At this season of the year such a command is rendered easy by the fact that the majority of 119 not in mourning, have at least one white gown in our wardrobe. With the addition of a touch of black, we are enabled to pay that respect to the memory of the late Duke, her Majesty desires, without expense. Few indeed among us b iast not a black hat. The black hat, like the ruffle has been almost a uniform this sum- mer. A point to congratulate ourselves upon. Such a form of headgear is economical, for it can be worn With any toilette, it is also most becoming to tee generality of women. The question of packing hats is one that is now exer- cising the feminine mind considerably. Of course where the travelling impedimenta includes a properly fitted hat box there is no trouole, but it is not everyone who possesses such a convenience and makeshift has to be made with something else. A good plan is to reserve one trunk or basket, and line the same wish canvas. Hats and toques can then be secured by means of lone hatpins, to tne sides, top and bottom, of the trunkoe hen we may defy the steutest porter to inflict the slightest damaee upon our " creations" csnteined therein:. If there are any ribbon bows these should he pecked with soft tissue paper, to keep them in position- Plain skirts. a little gathered on the hips, are being turned out for autumn wear by all the best hoeses. Much has been said concerning their unbecomingness to all save the most slender, but unquestionably the sliehtly gathered skirt is much more tiecomine to the most " generously built "than the "eel" variety proved. Ths much gathered skirt, for day wear, will vanish with the summer, but for svenieg wear it promises to remain much in evidence during the coming autumn and winter. From an artist's point of view the Academy season has been a disappointing one, as regards the sale of pictures which shows a considerable falling off as compared with former year.. Amongst the more notable sales are Mr Sidney Cooper's four pictures representing the four seasons, which together realised just on 22.000. There is always a keen contest for the works of Mr. Cooper who in many respects is the most remarkable of the Academicians. At the great age of 97—for he I was born in 1803, he is almost as hearty as when he first began to paint pictures, more than 80 years ace. He neither drinks nor smokes, to which fact he mostly ; attributes his good health,and though he is snowy white, he scarcely stocps at all, while his hand is as steady as when he berean to do his best work, chiefly the painting of cattle. Mr. Cooper lives most of the yesr at Canter- bury, and finds many of the" mcdels " on the farm, near his house. Miss Maud Earl, whose " Dees of Death " in this year's Academy received so much notice, is at the Present time in France, painting more Canine portraits. Miss Earl is devoted to dogs, entering thoroughly into their likes and dislikes, and she can quiet the most ex- cited and nervous among them. Her sitters are gener- ally pedigree animals, and she usually requires their attendance at her studio for two days, a day consisting of about six hours. Collies she says, are among her quietest sitters. They seem to know quite well that they are being watched and admired, and they are so accustomed to adulation that they simply stand still and wait for it. Miss Earl has painted several of the Prince and Princess of Wales' dogs at Sandringham, among them the Princess's famous Berzei "Alex." Hithertn, we have only read about that time to come. when, it is said, we shall all live upon concentrated food compressed into dainty little tabloids, convenient for the purse or waistcoat pocket. That there is more in the scientific development of artificial food than a mere interesting theory. is however to be proved by an enter- prising Freech chef, who is about to open a restaurant in Paris to supply these preparations. The menu will consist of an assortment of tabloids, of which a few will comprise the entire meal. It will be interesting to learn whether such a meal is really as sustaining as an ordinary dinner. If this is possible it opens up the prospect of a new life tor women, seeing that it would dispense with all cooking, meal preparinr, and clearing away. It would in fact turn the world upside down, and we are told that not only shall we buy our food at the chemists, but that drinks. cmnprising all the constituents of a glass at claret or whisky and soda, can be provided in the same con- centrated form. If all this is possible, it would revolu- tioniae our whole methods of life, and it will be deeply interesting to learn whether the experiment of the neW Parinian restaurant,will bear out all the promises of these scientifc theories. In spite of all the fascinating possibilities in the development of artificial food however, it is satisfactory to find that we are now paying gre-iter attention to Schools of Domestic Economy. This subject was dis- cussed at on of the Conferences in connection with the Paris Exhibition the other day, and it is a branch of education which is undoubtedly much better understood in France than by oar people. Amongst working men, and the poorer of the middle classes, there is a general complaint that girls know little or nothing of household management. This is one of the inevitable results of the universal tendency among girls to do anything ratter than go into domestic service. As a rule those who seek outside occuration have little or no practical knowledge of household affairs, and up to quite recently very little attention has been paid to these matters in our schools and institutions. The French people attach much more importance to household man- agement than has been the rule in this country, though we are at least, doing much more than formerly in ex- tendirtf, this useful branch of education. In America, the revival of the fashionable fortune teller has disclosed an amount of ignorance and super- stition among the public which is equal to anything we have ever read of in stories from the East. This much was revealed in the recent prosecution of the lady who presided over the" house of the healer" in New York. Her books were seized, and they showed all items paid to her cad what they were for. One man paid 5 dole. for. hie wife to die, as he c-old get an heiress. Others paid various sums for the Sibyl's influence in obtaining position, riches and assistance in love affairs. Score, of married women paid fees "to get husband to die." There were in fact thousands of such entries in the ledger of varying degrees of criminality, and the profit; of the establishment amounted altogether to something like £l.OOO a year. Unfortunately for the proprietress o the piice interfered and she is now enjoying the. seclusion of the gad.</p>
        <p>" MADGE'"</p>
      </div>
