      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUSICAL</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSICAL</p>
        <p>SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Anrual Meeting.</p>
        <p>In the Trinity Schoolroom, Warrington, last Thursday eveninr7, the trwmbers of the Warrington Musical S)ciety as,cmbied to transact the annual business of the society. Amongst thosn present were :—Mr John White. J.P.. Dr White, Mesars R T H Bond, R Eccles, J Nainuombe, P C Price, G F F White, W H Wallington, J Bnowies, H Wnitfi,A, F J Lord. J G Critchley, W Critchley with F Crossley (conductor), W E Atkin (lion. secretary), L W Reynolds (bon. treasurer.) ABSENCE OF THE PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>Mr W E Atkin, the hon. secretary, at the outset, explainpd that he had received a letter from Mrs Parr re4retting the inability of Mr Parr to attend the meeting Mr Parr was not very well and it was a disappointment to him that he wa;t; not able to be present. It was unanimcusly decided that Mr John White J.P. Should preside over the proceedinas. THE ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Bon. SECILETAaIo read his annual report whic h statel that the first full rehearse, of, the work of the fir it concert of the season 1899.1900 was held in the Trinity Schoolroom on S-ptember I.oth,a.nd the choir awl bane had in all, met for practice 37 times, exclusive of fina rehearsals in the Parr Hall. On Tuesday, July 25. lase year, the committee met and draw up s prospectus of the work of the season which had been carried out almost exactly and in its entirety. Four concerts were decided upon—three subscription concerts and the usual extra performance of the "Messiah "at popular prices in December. The Secre- tary proceeded to refer to the concerts in detail and in Commenting upon the miscellaneous concert, said as far as he could judge, the committee might congratulate themselves on the wisdom of the plan of giving at least one such concert each season. He believed a programme of the kind usually adopted was one which appealed very strongly to the public and at the same time afforded an Opportunity to chorus, and band alike of trying some works (necessarily short as a rule) of a different class to the round of oratorio or cantata which excellent as it is Might possibly be heavy food for some digestions if taken four times a year. It was not his place to criticise in any way the work of the season, but he ventured to remark that it was not by any means an easy task to accomplish in the time and that nothing hut the hearty good-will and keenness of the members themselves working under the able and untiring and painstaking conductor, it was their good fortune to possess carried through such a prospectus. He could not let the consideration of the season's work pass without just slight notice of one or two points. Two works by Geunc-ni were given, one as an opening and one as a closing concert, and it seemed to him te have been a happy selection. The performance ot "Faust " was a new thing, he thoueht, for Warrington, and the performance of " The Redemption "in April last, corn: leg as it did at the end of the Lenten season, was most teneularly appropriate. It was too, the first performance of this magnificent work in the town and he did not hesitate to predict that it would not be long before the Musiell Society was called upon to repeat the work. During the past season the numerical strength of the society's action farce had been well kept up. The members on the ho iks were : 57 sopranoe,63 contraltos. 27 tenors and 41 b is% a total chorus of 188 Members and a band ec 34 members, mak- ing a gross total of 222. This force had at all the concerts been increased to upwards of 250 by the added professional help the society was obliged to secure in the orchestra. He only wished it were possible to have a full amateur band, hut this was, he imaginecloomething that could hardly ever be hoped for. Mr. Newcombe had faithfully attend 'd to the work of the registration of aetendances at the rehearsals. The committee had not been called upon to naeet in a formal way more than twice in all since the last annual general meeting, and this, he thought, he might be allowed to say, spoke volumes for the excellent organisation of the business of the society. In a 'alto society like their's it was impos- sible to avoid the falling out of some members and they had occesionally to lose old friends—ladies got married and left the town or the inevitable removale from War- rington took place, and they regretted to have lost Several in these ways—but he was glad to say he did not think through any fault in their system or through any unfortunate quarrels had they lost any members during the past season. Their forces had very largely been added to by new members. He could not close that report without slightly alluding to the sad loss sustained by their conductor at the close of 1899. They had already placed on the minutes a recoid of their deep sympathy with him, but he felt that the society could not let its annual meeting close without allusion to that reselutien and a vote of con- dolence, and if anything were needed to increase the good feeling and sympathy already existing between Mr. Crossley and his chorus and orchestra, possibly the sad Christmas time of 1899 for him, did so. Tneir thanks were due to the officers of the society and he felt certain would be given heartily, bat light he be allowel to offer his own very sincere thanks '4)&amp;11—to Mr. Reynolds, Mr. G. F. F. White, Mr. New- e°tebe, Mr. Pearson, Mr. Caldwell and Mr. Crossley. He W'es obliged in a very high degree to Mr. Parsons too, who f,r some years had been of infinite service in most Voluntarily helpine him with the purely clerical part of the work and he (Mr. Atkin) would like to express his thanks to him, regretting that he nad now ceased to live near enough to Warrington to be any longer his right hand man. He hoped they might have a more peaceable time ahead and with a steady quiet time in political and national affairs he wished the society a better fieencial result teen, he feared, could be hoped for at the end of the otherwise suecessful season it had been his duty to report on now. (Applause.) FINANCIAL STATEMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. L. W. REYNOLDS presented the annual financial statement. He stated that at the last annual meeting it Was estimated that the adverse balance against the gociety on the year's working was £3 is 9i but since then he had received 13s 6.1 for some tickets sold which reduced the adverse balance to £2 8s 3d. Another item Which he ought also to mention ki connection with last year's working was the gift of music cases by their re sPected librarian Mr. G. F. F. White. (Applause.) Coming to the past season's work he said the total receipts were £3BO 133 8d and the exoenditureamounted to £4lO 3s 9(1 leaving a deficit Of £29 lOs id. On the lira; concert there was a gain of £6 33 6d; on the second a loss of £8 183 id ; on the third a gain of £4 03 ld, and on the" Msssiali " c,ncert a loss of £3O 153 ; making a total loss on the four concert 3 of £29 94 6d, which was alansc the amount of loss they had sustained during the Pes.nll. The total indebtedness of the society was now 51. 93 10d. The CELkIRMAN formally moved the adoption of the reports. He very mush regretted as he was sure they all did, the absence of Mr Parr, the president, and also that his 'absence was due to the state of his health. He trusted that their President's health might improve and that they might soon see him amongst them again. (Ilear, hear.) Although the bat ance sheet which had just been present d was not very encouraging they had every reason to con- grw ulate themselves upon the past year's work. The society's report was very interesting, So far as he ceuld judge he thought the past year had been the most uccess!ul in the history of the society from a musical point of view. (Hear, hear.) It was especially gratify- trig to notice the reference in the report to the perfect harmony which prevailed throughout the society, not Only at the concerts but also in all the arrange- tnente of the society, which was largely due to the tact and ability of the respected conductor. (Hear, hear.) As regarded the adverse bal- ance he did not think they need worry themselves yery much about it. The Musical Society always was ' In debt and always had been. (Laughter.) It had always been in debt since he knots it and and he had known it now for somethiag like fifty years. (Laughter.) It was satisfactory to some extent that the deficit on the Oast year's working was not larger than £29. They were met with the fact that the debt was accumulating year by year and unless they had a very successful fin- ancial time in coming years stops would have to be tahen to reduce the debt. However, he did not think they need worry themselves very much about the fin ancial position and he would now move the adoption of the report and balance sheet. Mr. R. T. Fainctocas seconded. He thought they might he encouraged by the last season's work. Although they were in debt and likely to remain in stlebt still he thought they had had a very successful Feltqon on the whole and had every reason to be en- ccuraged for that. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr; NEWCOMBE suggested that they might increase the charges for the" Messiah" concert. He thought the members would supp)rt the idea. It was a very serious matter that they slaiuld lose £3O at oi:e concert. Rising again, Mr. NEwcomnE said he had jAst been told it had been &amp;cided not to have a " Messiah " con- cert in the forthcoming season, so the point he bad raisld stopped at this. The report was unanimously adopted. ELECTION OF PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>Dr. WinTE Proposed the re-election of Mr. J. COariton Parr as president of the society for the ensuing year. He referred to Mr. Parr's gift of the Parr Hall and said that unless they had had the hall the society would have been almost extinct. The least they could do, therefore, was to acknowledge the debt they owed Mr. Parr by electing him as president. Moreover. Mr. Parr was also very greatly interested in the society', and always endeavoured to be present at their concerts. If everyone supported the society in the same manner the scciet7 would be in a prosperous condition. Mr NEWCOMBE seconded and said Mr Parr had been President for a good many years and he hoped he would continue to hold the position for some time to come. The motion was unanimously carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
