      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>TUE VICE-PRESIDENTS.</head>
        <p>TUE VICE-PRESIDENTS.</p>
        <p>The fo lowing gentlemen were elected vice-presidents of the Society Th Mayor (Alderman Robetts), Messrs Z Armitage J Crostield, A fl Crodield, J A alelsten, James Faircleugh,John Fairclouzh, R T Fairclouzh, F A Frost, R Garnett, Dr Gornall, Henry Greenall. F Monks, William Owen, R C Parr, R Pierpoint, M.P., Cilone Ridgway, and John White.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>COXIBIITTEE.</head>
        <p>THE COXIBIITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mr R T FAlRcLouou in prop,ising the re-election of the committee, said they had done their work remarkably well and they could not do better than re-elect theui.The committee consists of Messrs H Bond, It Eccles D Fanshaw, J Newcombe, P C Price, W Scotson, R H Silcock, W II Wallington, and R Whitfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>OFFICERS THANKED FOR THEIR SERVICES.</head>
        <p>THE OFFICERS THANKED FOR THEIR SERVICES.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said the next business was the election of officers, out he thought the least they could do before proceeding to re-elect them was to thank them for their past services. (Rear, hear.) He begged most cordially to propose a vote of thanks to them. No one knew better than he did the amount of work which devolved upon them. The proposition was unanimously carried. THE TREASURER.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. ECCLES proposed the re-election of Mr. L. W. Reynolds as hon. treasurer. Mr. PRICE seconded, and it was agreed to. Mr. REYNOLDS said it was in the year 1867 that he first became hon. secretary of the society. Some years afterwards he became hon. treasurer and he had held that position ever since. He thought it was about time that the society appointed someone to take his place. He knew it was a difficult matter to get anyone to be treasurer of a society that was always indebt—(laughter) —but if he cpu!d only see the society out of debt he should make a decided stand and say that he must be allowed to retire. He was afraid he would not be able to see the society out of debt. They had done something within recent years to reduce it, but unfortunately during the past season they had had to make an addition to it. He hoped they might be more succes.ful in the forthcoming season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>SECRETARIAL WORK.</head>
        <p>THE SECRETARIAL WORK.</p>
        <p>Mr. NEWCOMBE proposed the re-electien of Mr. W. E. Atkin as bon. secretary. .</p>
        <p>Mr. CROSSLEY seconded, and explained that owing to business changes, Mr. Atkin would not be in a position to devote the same amount of time to the duties as he had done in the past. Re would thereforo suggest that Mr. F. J. Lord be appointed joint secretlry with Mr. Atkin. The suggestion was approved, and Mr Atkin thanked the meeting for appointing such a gentleman to assist him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>OTHER OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>OTHER OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Mr GF F WHITE was re-elected librarian ; Mr Johnson' assistant, Mr Newcombe, registrar, Mr J B Caldwell organist and Mal H Pearson.leader of orchestra.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>PERCENTAGE OF ATTENDANCE.</head>
        <p>THE PERCENTAGE OF ATTENDANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr NEWCOMBE submitted a statement showing the percentage of attendanct.s at the rehearsals and the ecncerts. It Appears that at the first concert- the percen two was 68i, " Messiah" concert 731. second subQcription concert 61, third, 571, showing an average ot 65i per cent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>SUGGESTION.</head>
        <p>A SUGGESTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. ECCLES suggested that in view of the committee's decision not to hold a " Messiah" concert during the ensuing season, a miscellaneous concert might be sub- stituted, to be given by the society without any outside assistance. He would suggest that it consist of vocal and instrumental selections of a miscellaneous descrip- tion. He thought the society could give a presentable kind of concert. _ _ .</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN suggested that the committee should consider the matter at the next meeting.</p>
        <p>THE CONDUCTOR.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said the society was exceedingly for- tunate in having Mr. Cr 4ssley as the conductor. It was to the co,,ductor mainly, if not entirely, that the success of thu society was due. He (the chairman) therefore wished to thank him publicly for what he had done. Mr. NEWCOMBE seconded, and the vote was carried with acclamation. Mr. CROSSLEY in acknowlecling the c)mpliount spoke in warm terms of the general organisation of the society. It was a matter for regret that the "Messiah" concert turned out so badly from a financial standpoint. If it had not been for that concert they would have Lad no deficit to record. Various causes had been assigned,but he thought it was owing to the general feeling of depression last December on the turn of affairs in South Africa. Nearly all the musical societies in the country had lost money during the past season. He had not heard of one society that had made money. The season began well, but political matters got 80 bad that there was a great falling off in the attendance. Theatrical companies had suffered in like manner. Referring to Mr Eccle3's proposal he said it was an excellent one, but there was not a great deal ta be said against engaging outiide assistance, for at the "Messiah "concert, the cost was comparatively small. Be thought the decision of the committee not to have a performance this year was a wise one. The society would avoid all risk of losing money by it, and it would be rather better for one year's rest. It would also give the members more time to get up the work for the other concsrts. He would suggest that one of the works to be performed be" The Golden Legend." (Hear, hear.) The meeting terminated with an acknowledgment of the Chairman's services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>SHOCKING RAILWAY FATALITY. ---- A Warrington Platelayer Cut to Pieces.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING RAILWAY FATALITY. ---- A Warrington Platelayer Cut to Pieces.</p>
        <p>Shortly before two o'clock on Saturday afternoon a fatal accident occurred on the London and North Western Railway, near Froghall Bridge, Warring- ton. It appears that Thomas Gilbert, 43 years of age, a foreman platelayer, was engaged in tighten- ing some bolts on the line when he was caught by an express train from Leicester to Blackpool and cut to pieces. His remains were gathered together and removed to the mortuary. The driver of the train blew his whistle loudly, but deceased failed to take any notice of the warning. The deceased was very well known and respected in Warrington. He had been in the service of the company at Warring- ton for over twenty years, and was well acquainted with the local railway sy.4em. The Inquest</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Railway Company not Represented.</head>
        <p>The Railway Company not Represented.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse conducted an inquiry at the Police Station on Monday afternoon. At the outset. the Coroner asked it the London and North-Western Rail- way Company were represented, and Police-Irpinecter Taylor repliea that Permanent-way Invector Woston was in attendance. The C)roner (to Weston) : Have you any plans of the place? Weston : No sir. The Coroner: Why ? big company like the Lon- don and North-Western Railway Company ought t ) pro- vide every information for the jury. There is no solicitor no stationmaster here ; nobody. The evidence was tben called. William Gilbert sen of deceased, stated that his father was 48 years of age last birthday. Mr Sudlow (a juror) said ho had known the deceased very well fer many years. Be was a very steady and sober man, and thoroughly interested in his work. Inspector Weston said the deceased had been 20 yaars , in his present place. The Coroner : Can you explain where the accident happened. Inspector Weston : Near Frnehail Bridge. About eight yards on the north side oi the bride. The train was coming from Bank Quay Station, on its way !run Leicester to Blackpool. Samuel Brown, driver of the train which killed the deceased, said the train left Crewe at 12.35. It was a fast train and should not have stopped lo,tween Crewe land Poniton. When he first saw the deceased he was I about 80 yards away from the engine, and they were I Proceeding at the rate of from 45 r:, 48 miles an hour. The deeeased seemed to be kneeling as if working with a spanner inside the "four-foot " with his back turned towards the approaching train. Witt CPS blew his whistle when he saw the deceased and tried to pull up but he could not do K.) in time. The Coroner : Is it a train that runs cvery clay ? Witness : Yes sir.</p>
        <p>ih.;-6—o;Onei: Were you late ? Witness : Thirty-one minutes late from Crewe. The Coroner: He must have bEen under the im- pression von had gone past? Witness : Yes sir. The Coroner: Were the signals in frost of him ? Witness • He was on the north side of the stop signal. Inspector Weston said the deceased had been in the</p>
        <p>service of the company for 25 years, and about 20:years he had been engaged on that particular part of the line. He was a caretul, steady man. The Coron-r : What is your idea of the accident?</p>
        <p>Witnrss : I eotild not say unless he WAS sc.rewing up in thy" four-foot." It was the deceas,d's duty to go over the line after the platelayers had finished.</p>
        <p>The Coroner, in briefly summing up the evidence,said the facts appesred to be very simple, his experience to'd him that men like the deceased examined the work which had been done by his men after they had gone away. That course was adopted on the Cheshire Lines. A similar accident happened on that line. A man was killed whilst walking on the Cheshire,Lines with his back to the approach;ng train,and he was a very steady man, and he had known him for a considerable number of years. A verdict of "Accidental death " was returnod, the Coroner observing that these men became so accustomed to their work that they did not think of danger. Deceased's son said his father was very much inter- ested in his work.</p>
        <p>At the close of the inquiry. Mr. Brighouse said the company ought to have supplied the jury with plans of the spot where the accident occurred. (To Inspector Weston) : You hive nlans in the cabin showing each section, the signal boxes and the approaches. Inswctor Weston • I did not think of that. The Coroner : I don't say it is your fault, but if I did not say these things about the Railway Company it would be said that I wa3 afraid to do so. lam not afraid of thin any more than anyone else. The com- pany ought to be here with the plane if only as a mark of respect to the deceased. A juryman thought that some intimation ought to have been given to the workmen that the train was 31 minutes late.</p>
        <p>A few drops on the toothbrush every morning</p>
        <p>'11H: FXAMINI4II, FR) DAY, AUGUST 109 1900 QUOTATIONS FROM THE GLASGOW'S YEARLY MAGAZINES. EXODUS. _— Cromwell the First British Imperialist. It must las taken as a plain truth of history that Cromwell is the first consisteet and systematic architect of British Imperialism As such he has been, and he will be, prised or blamed by thoso weo glory in or those woo cendemn the huge structure which bas been built up on those foundations. But those who deolore that such barbarous excrescences on the glorious roll of English history should be linked with the memory of so pure a nam not forget that the Prottc:or of the middle of the 17th century must not be judged by the canons o any echool in the end of the 19th cen,ury ; that the standard we use baust be relative,not absolute; that Cromwell, however wise and just, could not rise above the best ideals of his aee, beeone the only religion conceivable to a Bible Christian.—Fr eel " Mr. Firth's Cremwell," in the " Correll!! :idea zinc"- 'or August. How the Guns were lost at Colenso. Saortly aftsr we had returnee to camp, however, there came sad and solemn confirmation of all that had been told to us, for the ambulances I had sent to Coleuses returned, one w:th tw wounded B ,ers, the other with Lieutenant-Colonel Hunt of the Artillery shot through both thighs. Thou we learned that what we had been told was too true. Cul mel Long had h nuelf selected the position for the batteries, and as they dashed up the plain they had suddenly been swept by shrapnel and rifle fire at a range of eleven or twelve hundred yards. The guns had been fought for about half an hour, and were getting the Boers ere under, when the ammunition ran short. Just ab not then Colonel Hunt had been wounded as he walked from gun to'gun, and had been carried tort donga wh-re he lay all day. Its the evening when the British ambulances c ime up, the stretchers had run short, ad he had waited till the last, when a Fi-ld Cornet seeing he was an officer, ordered him to be taken prisoner insteed of allowing him to be takett back—t quite ju,tifi able proceeding of c •u-se, but herd luck.— From " With aB oer Amnu aucceiu Na' al,' la the" Corn- hill Magazine" for August. Steel Plates. " Feilden's Magazine," quoting the remark recently made by a gentemad that " one of thee-) days we will see Canadian steel-plates coming up the Clyde and under- selling the Scotch article in the h ome market," thinks that this prophecy is not altogether such a wild natio, as it at first sight appeaes, and acids "The moral, if there is a moral, seem o to he this, that British ste,q manufacturers can by no M 03.113 afford to rest on their oars or neglect any practicable moans of improving their methods and cheapening their prodnct. Incidentally it may be noted that the boom in the British iron and steel trade, which is now at the tag end, may not un- likely give place to a long period of at least quiet business. The output of steel in tbe Unite I States will again overtake consumption before long, with the usual result upon prices." The Riches of Morocco. "From time to time one hoses reports of the vast wealth of Morocco, and people who never s -t foot in the country hasten to say it has no existence save in the imaginatiou of story-tellers. Yet the few who have travelled through the interior tell of a mineral wealth that bids fair to rival the Transvaal, undeveloped and uuezploited—first, because the Moors believe it is against the will of Allah to break into the bowels of the earth; secondly, because the Sultans and their advisers have been quick to realise that with tho advent of the mining industry the downfall of the native Government; can no longer be delayed. The fate of Major Spilsbury's eadeavour on behalf of the notorious Globe Venture Syndicate, formed to trade with the Sus, will not be for- gotten in this connection,and other attempts on a smaller scale have been made, with less reclame, but no better result. The restrictions that hamper this branch of in- dustry find counterpart in other directions. Morocco is reckoned throughout vast tracks of land to be one of the most fertile countries of the world. It has corn-growing districts even richer than our val- ley of the Dee at home; foe here the land must be served by the sweat of man's brow, while in Morocco it needs but to be tckled with a hoe in order to laugh with a harvest. There, azain, a most pernicious system of restriction comes in. The Seereefian Govern- ment will not permit grain to be sent from one part of the country to another, and consequently for one year a district may bese rich in grain that the harvest rots for lack of adequate labour to gather it, and the next year there may be starvation in the same quarter. Tnere is little or no storage in any of the inland towns ; for, so soot as a man shows even latent signs of wealth, the Governor comes upon him for a heavy subsidy, and if he do's not pay he is thrown into prison and left to starve. Iniustry is paralysed. In order to realise h.w such a state of things becomes possible, it should be noted that every governor of a Moroccan town pays the Sultan heavily for his post. It is clearly underatool that he wilt get his capital back, together with a very large interest, from the unhappy people he is called upon to govern ! Morocco is roughly parcelled out among cer- tain trib,s, all owing fealty to the Sultan. In some cases the fealty is merely nominal, in others the fealty might almo,t b said to exist on the other side, for the Sultan has to subsidise certain tribes to keep them quiet."— S. L. Bensusan, in the August number of the" Windsor Magazine." Manchester Dock Extension. " A great scheme of dock exteesien," says " Feilden's Magazine," " has ben prepared in connection with the Manchester Ship Canal. No fewer than 142 acres of land will be added to tho dock estate. . • Those who have followed the returns know how greedy the traffie has increased on the Snip Canal. Sp are land has gradu- ally been absorbed, spaces allotted to open-air cargoes have been cavered by transit shed.; for general cargoes brought by huge vessels from Canada and the United States and last summer even the railway sidings were covered with timber stacks. Since the appropriation of the Liverpool timber docks for the use of regular Atlantic liners, there has been a gradual transference of the tim- ber trade to Manchester, and this circumstance alene is sufficient justification for the works now proposed. . . Consequent upon the increased cost of labour and fuel, the railway companies have exhibited a very natural disposition to raise the rates for transport, as in Ssotland and to enforce charges for waggon hire, demurrage, weighing, and other items which have been more or less overlooked in the past. Ttaders do net appear to be particularly pleased at alterations of this kind, and deny the right of the cempinies to advance their rates. In view of the hostile attitude in which the mercantile community and the rail ways sand to w !el one another, and of the probability that further friction is likely to take place in the future, it is worth consider- ing whether the question of the purchase of the British railway system by the State ought not to be revived. . . . On the other hand, the public e-ite7tains a most welcome distrust of Government metnodi and if the railways of the kingdom were c,ontrolied by a department constituted on the m odol of the War Office. it is not clear that much advantage would result. Railway servants, from the general manager to the humble porter, only retain their appointments so long as they are found to be dischareine their geveral duties In a satisfactory manner. This condition app,ies in a very limited degre• to civil servants, each of whom is in a sense independent of his eeniore, and cannot he missed save for dishonesty. Again the laws of promotion recognise age and length of service rather than ability, and no one could expect railways to be properly ad- ministered on lines such as these. When the question of purchase comes to be discussed in a practical manner we hope the trading community will not lose sight of this essential consideration."</p>
        <p>[By "QuILP 'N.:I</p>
        <p>At ordinary times Glasgow is one of the busiest of cities. On every hand there are evidences of the great industrial activity which has gained for the city its present proud position as a commercial and manufacturing centre. Everyone is on business bent. With intellects sharpened by the keen struggle for exi,tence in this c3mpetitive age, these men of business as they hasten to and fro seem to pursue their way with a fixed determination to maintain their commercial supremacy: Trade is the mainspring of their action. It is trade which has made Glasgow suet a busy hive of industry. The streets resound with the clatter of horses feet and the rumbling of the constant; stream of vehicular traffic. Hundreds of chimneys belch forth columns of smoke which hangs over the city like a pall. Amid the constant bustle and stir nearly a million people live in densely populated areas. A progras- , eive and enlightened Corporation endeavours, with some success, to make the conditions under which these hard-working citizens live, as healthy and comfortable as possible. Much, however, remains to be done before an ideal state can be reached. A little over one hundrel and fifty years ago Defoe penned in his journal: "Glasgow is the beauti- fullest little city I have seen in Britain." Great industrial changes have been witnessed in Glasgow since then and it is probable that even Defoe woald use different adjectives in describing the Glasgow of to-clay.</p>
        <p>Once a- year a great change comes over the busy scene. In July there is a general exodus of the workers of Glasgow for a few brief days' respite from toil The event is known throughout Scotland as Glas- gow Fair Holidays.A few weeks ago I was privileged to see something of this great annual exodus from Glasgow. In Lancashire we have nothing to com- pare with it in vastness and extent. Oldham's great annual wakes or Manchester's Whit-week ar6 but small affairs when compared with the remarkable exodus from the commercial capital of Scocland during the Fair Holidays. Amongst the workers of Glasgow, holidays are even more generally re- cognised than in English cities. The very poor of Glasgow succeed by some means in getting to the coast for a few days. Special facilities are offered by railway and steamboat and the pleasure- seekers are conveyei in thousands to almost every part of ::cotland. The coast towns are largely re- sorted to, but some of the trippers venture across the Irih Sea to Ireland whilst others make their presence felt in the Isle of Ilan. Very few find their way into England and the inference may be drawn tnat the:Scotsman is of opinion that we have nothing better to offer him in the way of holiday resorts than he is able to find nearer" hame,” In this conclusion be is probably right,for as a pleasant place to spend a holiday, Scotland is hard to beat.</p>
        <p>- To witness the exodus from Glasgow during the Fair Holidays one needs to stand at one of the chief railway stations or near the Broomelaw, from whence the ste imers depart for innumerable places on the Clyde and beyond it. As the holiday- makers gather at the various places of departure they form a motley crowd. Every class of artisan and unskilled labourer is to be seen accompanied by his wife and generally a large family. It is a crowd such as one might see departing for a day's excursion in connection with local works. But the Glasgvw citizen does not think of a single day's excursion, Hcwever p3or he may be, be spends at least a few days' holiday at the seaside. To witness the departure is a sight indeed. Igen and women accompanied by excited youngsters bustle amid piles of luggage. There are boxes, bicycles, "prams," spades and buckets, umbrellas and sticks enfolded in strapped wraps and mackintoshes, and it almost looks as if they were departing, never to return again. The gentle rain may descend as persistently as ever, it makes no difference in the holiday arrangements. All Glasgow people seem determined to reach the coast towns. The bairns distinguish themselves in causing their parents endless trouble by their perverse conduct and the little "Wullies" and "Jeans," if not punished on the spot, are threatened with numerous pains and penalties. The greatest rush is witnessed on the first and second days of the holidays which commence on a Friday. The various places of departure are crowded with thousands of holiday seekers who come from all parts of the city. All thoughts of Glasgow are left behind in the pleasurable anticipation of the holiday which has now c3mmenced. At the earliest possible moment they fIEH from the city as though it were a scourge-smitten place. Te procession of excursionists to the railway statioas Continues with- out interruption hour after hour and the railway and steamboat resources are strained to the uttermost to meet the heavy deminds made upon them. The people congregate b Jore the barriers at the rail- way stations in their hundreds and immediately a train is signalled there is a mad rush to obtain seats in the carriages. In a short space of time all the compartments are crowded to excess, and some of the women and children are roughly used. Taken as a whole, however, the crowds are well-behaved and self possessed, and to these good qualities are due the few casualties reported. 'train after train departs, and still the crowd outside the barriers seems to increase rather than diminish. It is well ma in the night time before there is an appreciable diminution, and the following morning has only just dawned when more trippers for the early trains put in an appearance.</p>
        <p>- Glasgow never presents a more deserted appear- ance than on the Sunday in Fair week. Some of the churches are closed, an I others which remain open are so scantily attended that they might almsst as well have been shut. Very few people remain in town. The police force and newspaper-men appear to he the only men in possession. The roar of traffic has ceased and as the huge chimneys no longer emit black smoke it is possible to see the sky under favourable conditions at least once a year. The railway stations which have been the centres of such busy scenes are now as quiet as a country churchyard, and the officials and employes are tak- ing a well-deserved rest.</p>
        <p>At the coast towns, the large influx of visitors makes things " hum " as the Americans would say. Fair week is the busiest of the year. Every apart- ment is occupied and the Glasgowian proceeds to spend a right merry time. If no music is provided he supplies it himself on the concertina, accordian, or some less expensive instrument. Perhaps the liveliest groups are those occupying tents by the sea shore. In order to distinguish the tents a name is usually given to them end it is quite possible to find "The Innocents" aide by side with " Pretoria " or some other distinctive title. Many of the private parks and grounds are closed to the public during the Fair holidays owing to the wanton damage which has been done at previous Fa'rs It is stated by these in a position to judge that the conduct of the Glasgow holiday-maker has greatly improved within recent years. From my own observations I saw little to complain of. Some were certainly inclined co be a little rowdy, whilst other showed a weakness for too many" wee-draps." Now and again one cams across an individual under the influence of drink, who showed a disposition to throw his crip on the ground and make himself loll( silly by his foolish ways or expres-ions, hut these were the exceptions and were in striking contrast with well-behaved citizens who were rationally enjoying their brief holiday. To presume that little drinking takes place during the holidays would be a mistake. Whisky is largely in demand. This may be judged by the following statement of a writer of Clydeside echoes who remarks" It may interest the gentlemen of the black Qquad to know that there were over a hundred million gallons of wnisky in Scotland just before the opening of the Gla.gow Fair festivities."</p>
        <p>Th-e Fair holidays are generally marked by one or two noteable event. This year there was a dread- ful collision in Belfast Lough, known as the Dravoodary disaster, in which a number of pleasure seekers lost their lives whilst returning home. Then there was a litqe trouble at Dijon of a less serious nature. Some-fifty passengers went down the pier for the purpose of joining a cruise steamer, which failed to put in an appearance. For the privilege of entering the pier, the passengers had paid the customary toll of one penny, but on returning to the pierbead they objected to pay the usual penny charged to passengers landing. As they had failed through no fault of their own, to embark, the demand for an additional; toll was tantamount to adding insult to injury. They strongly objected to what they considered an imposition. The pier authorities were equally obdurate and the result was that the unfortunate passengers were kept imprisoned on the pier for nearly two hours, until, acting on the advice of the police, they were permitted to pass the turnstiles without the pay- ment ef a second penny. This was a triumph for the holiday-makers indeed, but at a great cost of time and mach inconvenience.</p>
        <p>Dunoon has gained notoriety in connection with its pier. This is due to the Sunday sailings on the</p>
        <p>1 Cylde against which the Danoon pier authorities lare strongly opposed. To rightly appreciate the difficulty it is necessary to state teat the majority of Scotch people are rigid tSabbatarians. There is no railway service on a sunday and the postman does not even make a delivery of letters on the Sabbath day. Sunday closing of public-houses is also strictly enforced. It is only within recent years that a steamship company has had the tern- erity to inaugurate Sunday sailings on the Clyde. The innovation was strongly resented in certain quarters and even now the Sunday steamer is known as the" Sabbath breaker." At Dunoon, the pier authorities at first refused to let the Sunday travellers land. Some rowdy scenes were witnessed and much feeling aroused. During this year's Fair, six passengers were graciously allowed to land on the Sunday but as they found the exit gates closed against them they were perforce obliged to climb over the spiked railings in order to get off the pier. The company included a lady who negotiated the railings with the best grace possible under the cir- cumstances.</p>
        <p>It is not until the close of Fair week that the ex- cursionists return, and Glasgow re-awakens to its usually busy life. Reinvigorated by a brief rest, the citizens turn LO the work of another year with fresh enthusiasrc, and in most cases, with pleasant reminiscences of the Fair.</p>
        <p>re. _.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>DRAW DRAW FOR THE NORTHERN UNION CUP.</head>
        <p>THE DRAW DRAW FOR THE NORTHERN UNION CUP.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night the first monthly meeting of the newly elected Committee of the Union was held at the George Hotel, Huddersfield, Mr. J. H. Smith, Widnes,presiding. The election of officers was proceeded with first, and re- sulted as follows :—President, Mr El Hutchinson, Wakr... field Trinity ; vice presidents, Mr J H Houghton, St. Helens; hon. treasurer, Mr J Nichol, Halifax ; hon secretary, Mr J Platt, Oldham. The draw for the Northern Union Cup resulted as follows : First Round, March 2 : Hull Kingston Rovers v Sal- ford, Rochdale Hornets Flramley, M inningham v Castleford, Leeds v Warrington, Runcorn v Wigan, Leigh v Alverthorpe, Wakefield Trinity v Eastin oor, Leeds Parish Church v Radcliffe • Barrow v Brighouse Rangers, Huddersfield v Hull, dunslet v Wath Brow, Aspatria v Altrinham, Tyldesley v Broughton Rangers, Lancaster v Maryport, Outwood Church v Holbeck, Liversedge v Normanton, Todmorden v Sowerby Bridge, Bradford v Swinton, Morecambe v Pontefract, Birken- head v Millom, Dewsbury v Featherstone, Stockport v Shipley, Hebden Bridge v Whitworth,Goole v St. Helens, Workington v Werneth, York v Halifax, Ocley v Old- ham, Keighley, v Kinsley, Windhill v 03sett,Beckmond- wike v S:aton, Batley v Elland, Whitehaven Rezreation v Widnes.</p>
        <p>Second round, March 9 isA,Dowsbury or Featherstone v Morecambe or Pontefract ; B, Manningham or Castle- ford v Workington or Werneth ; C, Keighley or Minsley v York or Halifax; D, Runcorn or Wigan v Leigh or Alverthorpe • E, Aspatria or Altrincham v Wakefield Trinity or iastnaoor ; F, Liversedge or Normanton v Leeds Parish Cnurch or Radcliffe; :G, Todmorden or &amp;meshy Bridge v Barrow or Brighouse Rangers; H Whicehaven Recreation or Widnes v Hunslet or Wath Brow; I, Outwood Church or Holbeck v Bradford or Swinton; J Lancaster or Maryport v Hull Kings- ton Rovers or Salford ; K. Rochdale Hornets or Bramley v Otley or Oldham •, L, Goole or St Helens v Stockport or Shipley; M, Hebden B-ide or Whitworth v Tyldesley or Broughton Rangers; N, Wind- hill or Ossett v Birkenhead Wanderers or:Millem; o,L,eds or Warrington v Heckmondwike or Seaton ; P,Batley or Elland v Huddersfield or Hull. Third round, March 23 : Q. winner of 0 v winner of F; R, winner of L v winner of P; 5, winner of E v winner of I T, winner of N v winner of H; U, winner, of G v win.aer of J; V winner of M v winner of K ; winner of A v winner of B ;X, winner of D v winner of C. Fourth round, March 30: AA, winner of U v winner of W ; 88, winner of T v winner of V; CO. winner of Q v winner of S ; DD. winner of R v winner of X Semi-final April 23 : EE, winner of CC v winner of AA; FF, winner of BB v winner of DD. Final : April 21. It was resolved that Messrs J Shel ion (Leeds), P Marsh ( fyldesley). and T Reddish (Leigh) be elected members of the Committee, in place of Messrs H Sewell. R Shaw, and CRa wle. It was els" resolved that the protest made by the Eastern division of the Yorkshire second competition against the formation of the League be referred back to the s-crind competition ; and that the clubs complaining be informed that the matter is one that the second division competition should settle ; but this Committee strongly recommend that the divisions shoeld be made as near Neal as possible. The alterations of by laws of Cumberland county and Lancashire and Yorkshire senior competitions were con- firmed. The Haverigg Football Club wee elected to membership of the Union. The rules of the Westmorland and North Lancashire League was confirmett Leeds Parish Church asked • "If a senior club pays in the close season a new player a given sum for signing a competition form, but he does not sign A and C forms until September 1. is this a violation of Rule 4, page 14, Official Guide ' "P —Answer . No. The Professional Sib-committee will consist of Messrs H H Waller, Brighouse Rangers; H Hutchinson, Wake- field Trinity ; J Clifford, Huddersfield ; J H Flongh,on, St. Helens; E Gresty, Broughton Rangers; J Oldham ; and J Welsh, SooLskport. The follawing is the alteration of the by-laws of the Cumberland County Union, which was confirmed • Na. 16. No club match shall be played in Cumberland on the dates of home County matches except by special per- mission of the Committee. In the Lancashire Senior Competition by-law 19 was deleted, and bv-law 21. altered to read thus :—Each club to register the names of all playera.who shall not at any time exceed the num- ber of 75, such names to be handed to the hon. secretary by September lin each year. Any new playing member must before playing get the sanction of this Committee, and have his name duly entered on the register. A fee of 2s 61 must be forwarded with the application for registration of each new player. This rule will also apply to the Yorkshire Senior Competition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>WARRINGTON. --- Licensing Committee.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON. --- Licensing Committee.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of he magiitrates on Tuesday morning the following gewlemen were appointed to form the Licensing Committee : —The Mayor (Alderman Roberts), Colonel Ridgway. Niessrs. F. Monks, E J Guest, T Birch all, W Pierpoint, and J Geddes.</p>
        <p>EXCELSIOR CYCLES at £lO 10s. Sole Agent, J. S. GIBIAND,S2. 53. and 55, Bridge-street,Warrington. Advt. Manchester Ship Canal Company.—A Profit</p>
        <p>of £82,230.</p>
        <p>The directors of the Ship Canal Company,in their report for the half-year ending June 30, states that the receipts amounted to £141,316, as against £124,183 in the corresponding period of last year; • but as the expenditure has risen to the extent of £8,588, the net revenue shows an increase of only £8.575. The weight of toll-paying merchandise has increased from 1,325,821 tons at this time last year to 1,487,841 tons. The whole of this increase, and more, is due to sea-going craft, for there is a decline of about 70,000 tons in barge traffic. The total prat on the half-year's working is £62.230. The directors have paid the interest due on the first and second mortgage debentures and the mortgage on surplus lands The balance of £16,488 they have handed to the Corporation A Miserablel32:nk Holiday.</p>
        <p>Monday was one of the most miserable August bank holidays which have been experienced for a considerable number of years. Shortly after nine o'clock rain commenced to fall and continue I almost incessantly until fire o'clock in the evening. Then the sun made its appearance, but it was only for a very short time. and a steady downpour of rain soon commenced again. being accompanied by thunder and vivid flas es of lightning. Not many people ventured out of town but notwithstanding all the adverse circumstances a large number passed through the town in waggonettes on their way from such places as Leigh and Widnes to Lymm, Frodsham and other well-known Cheshire holiday resorts, The Ruby Football Club Sports which should have been held at Warrington in the afternoon had to be abandoned and it is the intention of the committee to hold them on Saturday week. The Dahlia Queen Fe•tival which should have taken place at Burtonwood, was also postponed.</p>
      </div>
