      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>THEFT OF A BICYCLE BY A YOUT H</head>
        <p>THEFT OF A BICYCLE BY A YOUT H</p>
        <p>A Case from Warrington.</p>
        <p>On Saturday at the Wallasey Police Court, before Messrs. Gravenson and Stead, Lionel William George Marbert, a youth of 15 years of age, residing at 11, Ampthill-square, London, was charged on remand with stealing on the 25th ult. a bicycle, the property of Edward Warren,who carries on business as a cycle agent at Seacombe. Mr. Thomas S. Steel, solicitor, Warring- ton, appeared for the prisoner, who pleaded guilty. It will be remembered that at about ten o'clock on the evening of the dare mentioned the prisoner was seen in Bridge-street. Warrington, by P.C. Carter with a bicycle, rug, mackintosh, whip and a pair of gaiters. El, had heard that Marbert haa attempted to pawn the bicycle for 153., which was nothing like the real value. In answer to questions the youth made conflicting state- ments with which the constable was not satisfied, and he was locked up. The following nacrning he was brought before the magistrates at VVarringt.sn and re- manded for a week for iuquiries. It was found that the bicycle had been hired from a shop at Seaeombe, and he was eventually handed over to the Liscard police. The prosecutor stated that the prisoner came to his shop on the 27th ult. and hired the bicycle (produced), which belonged to him. Its value was £3 103. He paid a shilling on account, and should have returned the bicycle at one o'clock the same day. He. however, did not return it, and he saw the machine next in that court on Friday when the prisoner was in custody charged with stealinz it. Cross examined by Mr. Steel witness said the prisoner told him his name was Fred Williams and he gave the address of a New Brighton Hotel, which was the largest in the district. Asked if he did not consider it a great temptation to let a boy like the prisoner have a bicycle on such information, the witness said it had to be done in his bnsiness.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel said he was instructed by the broken- hearted mother of this boy to bring a few facts under the notice of the magistrates in thebope that they might see their way to deal leniently with her son. The prisoner was only 15 years of age. He was educated at the Mapin-street school, and leanly at a Collegiate School in Fitzroy-,quare. After leaving school he was engaged in a hosier and clothier's shop, where he re- mained for 15 months. He was afterwards apprenticed to an accountaot,but not liking this kind of employment he ran away from home and got into trouble. On going back home he entered into conversation with some ot his former school companions with the result that one of them arranged with the prisoner to run away again, They were to come to Liverpool, and with boyish ideas, they hoped to get on a South African liner and sail right away to a land where their fortunes would be insured. At the last moment the prisoner left home alone, with £25 of his mother's money, but for this she was willing to forgive him. He first went to Sheffield, and after spending the bulk of the money be came to Liverpool. Be visited the docks but was unable to secure a ship, and then decided to try bis fortune at Liscard. He walked 'for nine miles along the shore, and still being unsuccessful he returned to Seacombs. He was desirou s of getting back to Sheffield to take a situation be had had promised him. He had not sufficient money to pay his railway fare, and he devised the idea of hiring a bicycle and riding ta Sheffield. He rode to Warrington and eight coming on another boyish idea entered his fertile brain. He thought that if he could pawn the machine he would have sufficient money to take him to Sheffield that night; he woula take the situation wait- ing for him, and when he had saved the money he would redeem the bicycle and let the prosecutor have it back. That might be very childish, but the Court would remember that the prisoner was a boy with boyish ideas. The boy had no father to control his conduct,he having been dead eleven years, and the prisoner having Leon brought up in a large boarding hou.e, which his mother conducted he hid pretty much his own way and there had peen no check put on his money spend- ing. Having regard to these circumstacces he , (Mr Steel) submitted that the prisoner bad no felor SUS intent. To a certain extent he had acted in an irresponsible manner, and he was instructed that the position of the prisoner was the result of bad companionship. Mr. Taylor, the Warrington Police Court Missionary, had interested himself in the case and was prepared to do all he could for the youth, to endeavour to redeem him. He (Mr. Steel) would suggest that the prisoner should be committed for say seven or 14 days and at the end of that time Mr. Taylor would have endeavoured to have secured a shin for him. The boy was quite willing to go to sea. Be knew they would have no desire to make a criminal of the prisoner, and by sending him to prison for a long term they would no doubt do so. Be hoped they would sea their way to act on his suggestion.</p>
        <p>Superiiitendent Macdonald said the prisoner was con- victed at Worthing a few weeks-ago of ctealing a horse and trap and was fined £7 103. He had told him that he had stolen the £25 from his mothfr.</p>
        <p>In answer to tto Chairman, Mr. Taylor slid he would endeavour to get the prisoner a ship. The Chairman said they were sorry to see a boy of such tender years in ths position of the prisoner. They had, however, decided to act upon the suggestion ef his solicitor and he would have to go to nriFoi for 14 days. When he come out a situation would be found for him on a ship, and he hoped he would make a fresh start in life and turn out a good man. •</p>
        <p>Warrington Markets.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Corn Market on Wednesday : wheat fetched 3i 103 to 4s (701 b to the bushel) ; oats, 2R 10d ; flour (best seconds) per 2401 b., 21s ; bran, 10,41 per 20Ib. At the Provision Market prices were as follows :—Fresh butter, is 2d to is 31 per lb ; cheese, 7d and 8d per lb; fresh eags, 10 and 11 for Is; fowls, 45 Gd to 5s 6fl par couple; English beef, 6.0 to 101; mutton 74 to 104 ; lamb, 91 to 11d ; veal, 7d to 10d; pork, 61 to 80.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL '/AT.BllB.—Advt</p>
        <p>HOW TO INCREASE YOUR INCOME (problem solved) twenty additional agents required, ,xcellent terms to ener g,tic men of good eh .racter (whole or spare time).—Apply W Taylor. Superintendent Wesleyan General Assurance dociety 14. Cairo-street, Warrington.—Advt, Oood Templars' Picnic and Open-air Meeting A party of Good Templars 3onnecte4 with lodges in the area of No. 1 Convention, S.W. Lancashire had an enjoyable picnic on Saturday. Amongst those present were the folllowing Convention Offi- cials, Bro George Clarke, C.C.T., Bro J Speakman P.C.C.T., Bro E Newman, C.Trea., Bro J Curwen, C.Cb., and Bro James Sheath, C.M. The afternoon, was passed away very pleasantly, at Stockton Heath. The weather being bright and sunny at this time, a gentle stroll was taken through the village,ancl on to the Cantilever Bridge at Latchford. After a short time spent in admiring the simplicity with which the low level bridge was turned severs times for the passage of steamers along the Ship Canal, the party returned by 'bus to Warrington, and adjourned to the " Winmarleigh " Cafe in Sankey-street, where a further contingent of members from Ashton joined the party, who were refreshed in a manner which gpve every satisfac- tion. After tea, the members proceeded to Brick- street where an open air meeting was held, con- ducted by Bro George Clarke,D.E.S , of Earlestown, who gave an earnest address. An address was also given by Bro E Newman, D.S.J.T. Sister Mrs Sheath and Bro George Clarke sang melodies; and Sister Miss Walls, of Leigh,and Bro Joseph Sheath, of Warrington, contributed appropriate recitations. Suitable literature was distributed, and a hearty in- vitation to join the " TemPerance Mission Lodge meeting in the Brick—treet Mi,sion Room, was given. Seventeen names of children were given willing to join the Juvenile Teulple just about to be formed. The proceedings which had passed off very satisfactorily were brought to a close about 9 p.m. by the singing of the Poxology.</p>
      </div>
