      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <p>RESULT OF</p>
        <p>SPRING CLEANING CObIPETITIQN.</p>
        <p>We have received such a number of really good parodies on Kipling's celebrated verses that we should like very much to give the prize to several dozens of them. As that, how- ever, is impossible. we have had to make a choice and have decided upon which we think is really the most appropriate. We publish this one, and also a selection of the others, not adjudging them tht, best of those which h..ve been sent in but as a representative selection. We desire to thank all those who have taken the trouble to send us contributions towards this competition.</p>
        <p>X3was AWARDED to EDWARD H. DAVIS, Harborne, for the following verses :—BOOTS ADVICE to HOUSEWIVES.</p>
        <p>When you're started with Spring Cleaning—and you want</p>
        <p>to do it clean - (And you know you cannot do it with your mouth) Will you kindly buy a bottle ot our • k'urnitewrer Cream., . .</p>
        <p>Wliich is used in 'East and West and North and South. It's a powerful kind of compound, and it's very good to And you and Jane can take it as you find it. When it's used in active service, cleaning stains from " sticks," You will find It wont leave many marks behind it.</p>
        <p>Boots shop, Boots shop, shop of a hundred things An enterprising Company, it's all the rage to-day Everything sold for the lowest cash (as y,.us can see for yourselves). Then step in ladies, take your choice, and pay I pay pay There are wives who do it secret—asking no permission to— For their husbands wouldn't give it if they did; But suppose you use oar "polish"—it will make things look like new— And you need not stop the work " cos " there's a kid. You could do your cleaning casual—without worry—when he's gone, For if the beggar's absent never mind him. You might use our "Wills' Ammonia," which would take away the smell Of tobacco—which he always leaves behind him. Boots shop, Boots shop, etc.</p>
        <p>You can manage so as later, when you look him in the face, You can tell him what he'll think is only fair, How that while he earned the wages you had tidied up the place And had polished up his special old arm chair. He's an absent-minded begg.r, and he may forget it all, But you do not want his kiddies to remind him That you locked them in the coal house while you cleaned his house and hall, And were hright'ning up the home he'd left behind him. Boots shop, Boots shop, etc.</p>
        <p>When you've started on your cleaning, when you're scour. ing all your rooms, Use" Ammonia, Cloudy Cleansing," made by Wills, You can buy it for a shilling, and you'll find its pleasant fumes All the dirt and noxious germs quite quickly kills, Boots' an advertising beggar, but his stores are very great. And I and you must take him as we find him, He'd sooner have your cash than put it en a slate, And he's leaving all the other shops behind him. Cooks Boots, dukes Boots, Boots of a hundred kings. Boots of a market public fit them all.we say; With Varnish, or Cream. and Paint, and thousands of Other things, Ask for them all for your cleaning's sake and pay. Pay. pay. There are girls who make no seeret,'cos they'veino occasion to Boots Cream to polish furniture is good, That Gold Paint as made by Morgan is the finest thing that you Can gild upon old ormolu or wood. There are girls you talk to casual, will assure you that they love To polish up their brasses, and their tins, With Paste th,t's sold at Boots, so much better, and above All others, covering multitudes of sins.</p>
        <p>Cooks' sins, dukes' sins, sins of a belted Aar], Fifty thousand soldiers' sins, gone from them astray, Buttons and buckles and gun, will shine our khaki girl, Buy it quick for your polishing's sake, to-day, day, day. There are families by thousands using Boobies Oaken Stain To furbish up bare spots around their thors, It saves them yards ot carpet, for it will wash again When varished well to close the open pores. You can polish up the chairs with the Cream they sell Boots— You need not go to Africa to find him. He has so many branches, from scores of good old roots, He is mostly st the frJnt, nor looks behind him 1 Dukes' Cream, cooks' Stain, Polish for metals, Gol Paint, These are the things you will ask for going to Boots to-day, With Ammonia, Cloudy Cleansing, to save you from a faint, Take yoar cash in your pocket and pay, pay,pay. Nom de plume JACOB WILSON, Nottingham.</p>
        <p>If your slavey goes and leaves you when spring cleaning's coming on, And you cannot get another maid that suite. I will tell you how to manage with little extra toil, Just walk off to the neare.t shop of LIOOTS. They are civil and obliging, you will very quickly find, Their goods are cheap and fresh and up to date. And you'll buy for say one shilling what most firms would sell for three, As they trade for cash and do not keep a slate. Pure Drugs, Gold Paint, Furniture Cream and Pills, Stuff for saving elbow grease. powders and various roots, Stains and Polish of every kind, things that will cure all ills, So cease to fret, but put on your shoes and just try BOOTS. If you think your chairs and tables get a little worse for wear, And the sideboard is not what it used to seem, Don't bresk your cups and astners and say you wish you're dead. For 4d. only need be spent on Cream. With a rag apply it gently, any child can put it Ott, It's as easy if you want to stain the floor, Spend another elid. on a pot of Varnish 8, am, Then your home will look just as it did of yore. Soft Soap, Brunswick Black, Paste for a hundred tins, Oils and Paints of every kind, Cloudy Ammonia, too, Each of them certain todo its work, just as you're told it will, So keep home clean for your credit's sake and you'll not rue. When you've finished in the bedroom, and the parlour and the hall. , Although you're well repaid doa't think you're done, ' There's the pits and pans and kettles want a little clean- • lug out, , So again to BOOTS you'll have to run; Get a tin of Metal Polish, just one penny does the triak, No stains or scratches you will ever tr Ice, Then you'll sing about your housework and be glad' r arA alive, And in everything you'll see a smiling face, Cash Chemists, Good Chemists, things at the lowest rate, Everything superior for a price th,t always suits, 50,000 housewives sing praise ot one accord, And never deal with another firm but Boon,. BOOTS, BOOTS. F. JOHNSON, 4, Victoria-rd., Penketh, near Warrington.</p>
        <p>When the Springtime surreptitiously steals on us unawares With a sudden light of pale and sickly sun, Then the prudent British matron lakes to polishing the stairs, And there'd lots of other house-work to be done. So in order that the mansien may be thoroughly made bright. BOOTS VARNISH STAINS and other things are bought. And the hubby's consolation is in thoughts of speedy flight, For he doesn't understand things as he ought. Rub, rub. scrnb, scrub, ain't it awful work. So the hubby thinketh, though he really doesn't know One little bit about it when he fancies terrors lurk, And the beet thing he can do is to go, go, go. Oh, the saving in the coal bills with the corning cf the Spring, When the black bacteria wobble in their flight, And the income tax collectors and the birds begin to sing, And the dirty smoky csiliugs turn to white. If you view it as von ought to it's a most congenial time, With UNRIVALLED METAL PLISH to the fore. In burnishing door handles and removing all the grime From the metals while the women scrub the floor. Rub, rub, scrub, sr rub, making things look like new Fuars.Tuits: CREAM and BRUNSWICK BLACK are ;ts articles tnat pay. Buy all you want at Boots, you'll keep that web in view? And do your work when hubby's out of the way, way, way. When the work is nearly over there's a transformation scene, And you don't regret you've tackled it in time, As OR realize the tact that now your house is nice and clean, have left it dirty would have been a crime. Oh 1 the glory of the carpets which now look as good as new, Oh 1 the brightness or the fenders and the chairs, surprising to the hubby as he takes a startled view, And the wife enjoys his wonder as he stares. What, what I whist, what 1 done the whole lot in a day? (Heaven seems to open to the guilty man who acca4s) Wonderful how did you do it? Tell me your secret pray, " I (id it all with things I bought from Boots, Boots, Boots."</p>
        <p>C. P. JONRS, Solicitor, 6. St. Andrews-street, Cambridge.</p>
