      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>TELE EX t„ MIN Ell,</head>
        <p>TELE EX t„ MIN Ell,</p>
        <p>AUGUST 10TH 1900.</p>
        <p>The session has ended The Session. without any regrets. From</p>
        <p>beginning to close it has been over-shadowed by the war in South Africaits legislation has been rendered scanty and its finance is weak and procrastinating from the same cause. There have been no great debates, and neither Ministers nor the opposition can look back upon its record with pride and elation of soul. It was the seventh session of the present Parliament, but that does not necessarily make it the last, because tha Septennial Act prescribes years and not sessions When the session opened matters were not pro- ceeding satisfactorily in South Africa, and the shortcomings of the War Department were so obvious that a Committee of Inquiry was promised which is to begin its operations woen the war is over. Of the measures referred to in the Queen's I Speech of February 9th all but three—the Second- ary Education Bill, the Factory Bill, and the Lunacy Bill—have been passed. This is a pretty good record, but it must be admitted that the measures have been small in scope and in size. The Companies Bill and the Money-lending Bill were remnants from previous sessions, and the Bill for the Prevention of Railway Accidents was also a survival. The Agricultural Holdings Bill was new and unsatisfactory, and the Lords' amendments were ignominiously accepted at the last moment, in order, the Government protested, to save the bill. The Housing Bill is a measure of limited scope for acquiring land outside municipal areas The Secondary Educa- tion Bill never got through even the Lords. The Elementary Education Bill for England was a very small effort. The Irish Tithes Bill will make land- lords, who pay the tithes in Ireland, a handsome present. The Volunteers Bill had to be deprived of the section by which Volunteers could accept responsibility for serving out of the United Kingdom before the House of Commons would have it. The Government has used the Grand Com- mittees to hasten forward some contentious bills. Reference ought to be made to the Burial Grounds Bill and the Census Bill. The Australian Com- monwealth Bill was the result of political work in the Colonies, but there were long negotiations over Clause 74, and it was three times changed before being finally accepted. A sequential measure, to add four judges to the Judicial Com- mittee of the Privy Council, was promised, but never came to anything. It has been a session of Committees. There was a Committee on the Sea Fisheries Bill, and it recommended more inquiry I before legislation. There was a Joint Committee on Municipal Trading, but it made no conclusive report. There was a Committee on Army Con- tracts, which discovered no marked corruption, but suggested useful changes. A Hospitals Com- mittee of outsiders was appointed and is now on its way to South Africa. A Boilers Committee also of outsiders, is to sit upon water-tube boilers especially Bellevilles. The finance of the year was knotted up early, so as to be ready for a dissolu- tion if good news came from the war; but the war went on, and so did expenditure. The Budget was introdueed, March sth, and for several days prior to its introduction goods were drawn out of bond with panic impetuosity, about £4,000,000 being paid iii dztio. Toa Income-tax was in- creased 4d. in the 2, is. a barrel extra was put on beer, 4d. a p mnd on tobacco, 6d. a gallon on spirits, and 21. a pound on tea. The proposal to tax brokers' contracts stirred up so much opposi- tion that it had to be abandooed. Later on, a Bill to reduce the interest on Savings Bank de- posits was also dropped. As finally rectified, the Budget will Come out a good deal over £150,000,000 in expenditure, and there will be a big deficit. The war is estimated to cost about £70,000,000, but there are a good many charges to be added next year. Consols have dropped, and the financial and industrial outlook is by no means I bright. The only important private member'sl bill passed is to extend the Compensation Act to agricultural labourers, under restrictions. The China trouble has increased our general difficulties. I The MinisterialistA have held together better than the Liberals have,and if Sir H. CAMPBELL •BANNER- lidtsases leadership has often been uncmccessful, Mr. BALFOUR has hardly 'men as capable as usual, and over the Hospital Committee he has displayed bad I taste and bad temper. Mr. WYNnium is about the I only important mern")er who has signally improved his position during the session.</p>
        <p>NEw regulations in con- The Aged and Poor. nection with the aged and Deserving deserving poor have been drawn up by the Local Government Board, but a bill would be necessary to carry them out, as they require aid from the Exchequer. Hence, remarks Mr. Chaplin, in a circular he has sent outto the Boards of Guardians, referring to the Chancellor of the Exchequer, "having regard to the enormous expense which he has to meet at the present time, I have come to the conclusion, although very re- luctantly, that this is not a fitting moment to make further demands upon him." We fear that this means the indofinite postponement of the re- forms recommended by the Select Committee, as the demands on the Exchequer will not dimin- ish for some time to come. However, Mr. Chaplin makes one or two useful suggestions, such as the removal of imbeciles from workhouses and the boarding out of children. The aged and deserving poor "should not be urged to enter the workhouse at all," unless there are special reasons for it. They should be granted outdo* relief, but the Board</p>
        <p>"are afraid that too frequently such relief is not adequate in amount." Upon these points comments are invited, and we may say that few imbeciles are now classed with ordinary inmates. They have been removed to lunatic asylums, in order to obtain the grant for such per- sons which was given in the 'seventies. Only as imbeciles are so treated can they be used as a means of obtaining the 4s. grant. The re- moval of the children from workhouses is a more serious matter, and we admire the zeal and. humanity of the Guardians who have entered upon the experiment of boarding out. It has answered well in nearly every case, and now that provision is being made for sending the children to ordinary elementary schools a wise change can be completed. With regard to the outdoor relief for the aged and deserving poor, the Guardians will hardly be so ready to change customs and principles. For many decades the policy of Poor Law inspectors has been to urge upon Guardians to offer" the House." It has been regarded as a cure for habitual pauperism, and it has been enforced by all the machinery known to the law. Out- door relief has been considered more costly than indoor, and more demoralising. Hence outdoor relief has been kept low on purpose to force the poor into the House. If all this is to be changed, the ratepayers may feel that they are being called upon to make good the broken promises of tke Government as to old-ago pensions and other humane forms, and they will not like it. Little good can be expected from the paternal advice of Mr. CHAPLIN for some time to come. When in the House, it may be possible to improve the lot of the deserving poor by the changes Mr. CHAPLIN suggests. Separate classifi- cation at sixty-five, day-rooms, separate cubicles, liberty to see friends, a locker, and allowances of tea, sugar, and tobacco, ate all useful points to submit to the Guardians, if they can carry them out. In many cases it will be impossible to give day and sleeping rooms without costly enlarge- ments and alterations. Where the children can be boarded out there may be room available, and gradual improvements may be made. The small luxuries Mr. CHAPIAN mentions can, and we are glad to know that so far as Warrington is con- cerned, are being given, and are gratefully re- ceived by many aged inmates. We should hope that Mr. CHAPLIN'S circular may have some moral, though it can have no legal effect. Oar treatment of the aged and deserving poor has been allowed to fall behind that of several other countries. But the blame cannot be put upon the Guardians, who have been lectured and brow- beaten by Government inspectors and auditors until they have been forced to be hard and of tea inhuman.</p>
        <p>Pleasure seekers on Bank Holiday were in a real sense seekers after pleasure, for there wall certainly much more of the seeking than the find- ing of pleasure on Moliday last. Warrington bas rarely, if ever, experienced such a wet and un- pleasant August Bank Holiday. Rain, wind and cold are not usually associated with August weather but on Monday we had all three and theY were reminiscent rather of March than of August. There were some who ventured lurther afield in the hope of meeting with more favourable climatic conditions but they returned with woeful tales of unpleasant experiences. In town, the sports 00 the Footoall Field had to be abandoned ow „ing to the ihcessant downpour of rain, and all oure-44°Z enjoyment was banned, and the only persons W, seemed to profit by the bad weather were We publicans and theatre-proprietors. They were amongst the few people who had no cause t° gromble at the weather on Monday.</p>
        <p>, There is reason to fear that the work of tbi,/, Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Chilare" will suffer from lack of funds. The enthusissl which greeted its formation when the Act el Parliament happily called the children's charter was passed has been dwindling in the last few yearse and lately there has been a large transfer'? sympathy to the suffe_ers from the war. It Is characteristic of the kindness of Lord Roberts theb he should announce from Pretoria the willingees! of Lady Roberts and himself to become patrons ei the society, in the desire that one good work rosY not suffer from another. The trut`i is that the efficiency of such a society depends upon its beleg kept up to a certain scale. If the districts of clic, inspectors are not kept within moderate limits the duties of inquiry throughout those districts must suffer. Again, it is useless to have inspectors unless in gross cases of cruelty, prosecutions can be properly supported in courts of lajw, and the legal expenses are necessarily heavy. lbe prosecutions form but a small portion of the society's work, but without the means of follow- big up warnings the remonstrances would be 01 little avail. It is a work, too, which must be done with peculiar discretion, as it runs counter to .e cherished belief that an Englishman's home is 1110 castle, and prescribes an outside interpreter as to the responsibilities of parents. The inhumanity ef some fathers and mothers, however, demands ell interp,,sition of this sort, and the usefulness of the society is pr ,ved by its record. Cruelty, starv*'. tion and filth have alike been cleared away from many a drunkard's home by its influence.</p>
        <p>There can be no doubt that, in spite of the present wave of renewed prosperity in the iron and steel industry, due for the most part to a rise in prices which may not be mainOined,rnsn,ll- , facturers have cause to view the future with anxiety. The matter is one of Rational concern,so intimately is the production of steel bound 11P with shipbuilding, and, the provision of a nal, strong enough to defend not only home waters SOL' the lines of commerce, but also distant colonies; which would be singularly open to raids in time °I war. Even at present, it is said, steel plates oil" not he producbd at a sufficiently rapid rate t° meet the demands of the shipbuilding programme of the Admiralty, and perilous indeed would be. the situation if by a collapse of the industry Britain Governments should be solely dependeou upon America for the supply. Yet year by Yell the competition from the States grows more ail' ,more severe, and there is now a threat, and no_„re than a threat,of a great output from Cauada,wileiv iron and steel production is fostered by means of bounty. As usual when industries are threatened: it appears that our manufacturers are sornewhiw behindhand in their processes and appliances' clinging with a devotion worthy of a better catir to methods which have been superseded 10 America. Possibly the attention which has beet now directed to the matter will lead to impNvea ment in this respect. There is, moreover, fo' in the manufacturers' complaint with regard to the cost of coal carriage. It has become almost sicl axiom that iron ore can only be worked in Engls'l . where it occurs in close proximity to coal layerw! i while n America coal is brought into the iron cell tres from a great distance at a low rate.</p>
        <p>-- When the Warrington Corporation Bill of last year was under discussion there were certain raft)" payers who were inclined to regard as mere alarni. ists those who told us that in order to obtain SP_ adequate water supply for the town, considerat": extensions were necessary at Winwick. There sore those who thought that the wells already sunk scsii quite sufficient to meet the demand and that , the Corporation need do wa3 to keep on purePieli: The condition of the wells during the last ,7,0 months has shown the fallacy of that presunW° irr,, and proved beyond doubt the wisdom of the for poration in taking immediate steps to provide di, the increasing wants-of-the-town by sinking tional wells and erecting reservoirs. 91. work is now in hand and by next 7?„1, the Corporation hope tn, be in a better positi-</p>
        <p>ed be or</p>
        <p>re." &gt;pe 1130</p>
        <p>or the )1q-</p>
        <p>as regards the water supply than they have b, een this year. Indeed on certain days the water oelcame so low in the wells at Winwick as to give cause for a restriction of the supply in the event of the hot weather continuing. Fortunately, that coridition of affairs has passed for a time, and when the extensions now being made are completed no suich fears will haunt us.</p>
        <p>'The report of the directors of the Manchester ip Canal Company which was submitted to the tnebting of shareholders on Thursday, was of a inore promising character than any which has pre- ceded it. There were no dividend warrants to distribute but as none were expected, it can hardly ba said that anyone was greatly disappointed. The directors, however, have succeeded in doing the laext best thing and that is to make a profit. The total profit on the half-year's working is £62,230 and this not only enables the directors to pay the ilaterest due upon the first and second mortgage dEjbentures, and on the mortgage of surplus lands, but also places them in the position of being able to hand over a sum of £16,488 to the A.%orporation of Manchester on account of the interest due on the debentures they hold. The traffic continues to steadily increase and con- -Bequently the receipts are advancing. The weight ? f toll-paying merchandise is still under one and a ztalf million tons fo7_. the half-year but with the co-operation of traders and shippers this should easily be exceeded.</p>
        <p>Wartington is now a County Borough. The Royal Assent to the Bill authorising it has been duly given in the House of Lords, so that we may consider ourselves in possession of this new dignity and source of additional power, although the Act does not come into operation until September text. There are good grounds for believing that Warrington has become a County Borough just in: the nick of time. By the new education scheme, County Boroughs will be the authorities for the purposes of secondary educa- tion, and will have absolute control of this most important matter. The non-county boroughs will be under the control of the County Council, and if their experience is like unto that of Warrington in regard to Technical Education their position is riot an enviable one. The Corporation are to be congratulated upon the result of their applica- tion. The Town Clerk, in particular, upon whom has devolved most of the work, is deserving of a full meed of praise, on the successful outcome of his labours. The Corporation has been very suc- ,ceesful with recent bills before Parliament and the -I:l3livn Clerk has certainly enhanced his reputa- tion in this department of work.</p>
        <p>-The Indian famine came at a very awkward time for everybody, but as such scourges cannot be ex- Pected to corvider mere man, the latter has to be Prepared. In India, unfortunately he was not,and even bitter experience does not appear to teach „1718dom to the administrators of that vast country -rainino raged fiercely, and this country was called n,l)on for help as on previous occasions. Nor did i e cry go unheard, the multitude of other ,clairus notwithstanding. A noble response has ?e'en made to the appeal of the suffer- ing ilindoos. Lancashire in particular has :lone its duty manfully, and the fact that £134,000 nes been raised in the county is one eminently creditable to Lancashire open heartedness and ?tripathy. The sttm is, indeed, only £'26,000 less itnan that raised in 1897, when there was no war !und draining the pockets of the people. London ,181 e this matter, as in many others, behind the ur,onnty Palatine, for the Mansion House fund is 200,000 below the level of 1897. Well done Lan- cashire.</p>
        <p>We have had several little lectures of late on gul subject of " treating" soldiers when they re- rn home from the war, but none have touched (re matter in a more practical way that has the Lancet." This medical journal urges upoa every- corning into contact with these returned rarriors, the plain fact that knowing a man as a acod or the pissession of admiration for a man hrl has done his duty is no reason for treating 0 'lite an overpius of alcoholic drinks. It points witt tfis,t for any man, and more especially for one 0° has just recovered from a serious illness, to filled with a selection of alcoholic drinks, and Loose, too, in many instances, not of the best quality, is conducive neither to his health tor to his chance of success in civil employment. A mixture, as is very nos- eible, of beer, rum, and "whisky-and-soda," is calculated, says this medical authority, "seriously to disturb the progress of convalescence,in addition to the fact that if a man on sick leave is treated to over-indulgence his resulting state of incapacity Would not raise him in the eyes of a possible 7,ni.Ployer." After such an excellent piece of advice 44 .to be hoped that anyone who " treats " a 9,olclier returned from the front will not dos q under e impression that he is doing him a kindness or ulePlaying his friendliness in the best possible kanner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Nothing has occurred of late—not even the Pekin Lelief expedition, or the winding up of affairs in etath Africa,—that has caused more talk tnan the traordinary samples ot weather produced all over re country within the past fortnight, lii its des- ' brective effects, as well as in other ways, the mar- 2ellous capabilities of the English climate,have been metZonstrated in the passage across F.nlaud of a f.yclonic storm which is acknowledged by meteor:, ,c44lCal experts to have been quite unique. in the :44enths when such coaditions of weather are most oDected, i might not, have attracted so much atten- -I,l°ll, but be d spoiling Aug uet bank-holiday for Ztlndreds of thousands of toilers, coming in tho very Zight of a summer which has been remarkable for neat and an abundance of sunshine together ;Ivith plentiful crops of fruit, it has caused an -I.onnt of damage which it must he very difficult seu estimate. Apple, pear, and even plum treec have strippel of halt their fruit, and in many cases early varieties almost tit for the market, pra..::tic- islS. no fruit. has with,toorl the violent shaking. t,,4e whole of England and Wales has been affected; ,ee wind everywhere reaching the force of a fresh :Jr strong gale. Happily, in our climate, there is Zver reason to despair, though many will have 7en brought to the verge of it within the past few -</p>
        <p>. There is,if not a fortune something near it await- the electricai expert vrio can discover an in- llibje against the "sparking" of the cabns, there gas mains and electric cables run along side tY side each ()her, as they do in some of our cl.wns, there is aiways the danger th,it the sparkng the cables may ignite any gas that ma). have :Ccomulated in the cavities of the conduits. In tvtle else which ha 9 just been •reported a gas main latween two electric circuits,aiad all three were 101ved in a mischief which caused much alarm no 10,s of life. It is mentioned here as an illin- tt.l.tion of a street danger to which our towns and kileties th,-. happen to be electrically lighted will come increasingly expose, unless the discovery rondo of an unfailing remedy, or the local ;;IltJiorities devise c,ome means tor keeping the gas fin the electric mains at a- considerab!e distance Ir(Ito each other. . It is feared that the hope of the ilVter is slender, because gas and electricity will be th,, ci"3sr, compittion probably, as long as the °IQ-beds hold cut.</p>
        <p>t,,,14.0v7 that attention is being everywhere directed the lists cf voters appearing on or near the church 171 chapel doors and otter usual places,it may not r: out of Disc.: here to remark that there are very tititi,lll' lodgers fully entitled to the vote whose a„ es are not oa the r,spective lists. That is to (;`,:,,Y! they have only to pat in their claim in the "inary way in or.ler to become the proud pos- th"OrB of a vote. Bat the truth is that many of do not know it, and forget to make any in- until the time for putting in their claim, the tett'. of Auga.gtrhasi-gana by. Now is the time for tilloding such.</p>
        <p>DrlieferPnce was recently made in this letter to a I,,,i?Posal that teacners of music shall be registered. to,;11 a view to discouraging those thousands of teurs l'wbo, en the strength of;an elementary tlin°.wledge of pianoforte plo,ying,presume to torture kuelr fellow creatures by setting tip schools of •,14kie. A Bill has been introduced into Parliament "LlB thltig4ffid of the tasion,by Dir. Sidebothanr,</p>
        <p>which pays due regard to the suggestion of those musical associations which are representative of the profession, and it is understood to only remain now for some association representative of the public, after suffering so long, to press the measure firmly and unceasingly upon the attention of the L-gis- lature. It is about nine years since a Select Com- mittee of the House of tommons was appointed to report upon the question of a general registration of teachers ; and its conclusion was that while it was not essential that teachers of music, and of possibly one or two other subjects, should be placed on the register, the wishes of those entitled to speak on their behalf should be consulted. Of Course, there is something to be said on the other side, favourable to poor teachers; and, what, more- over, about learners? The next-door neighbour could say much as regards the latter. It can scarcely be said that all has been done when imperfect or ' even perfect teachers have been punished, to sup- press the pianoforte torture.</p>
        <p>Lessons from the Assizes ought never to be ignored; those placed before us by her Majesty's judges above all. Mr. Justice Wills, for instance, teaches that betting, rather than drunkenness, is the greatest curse of the nation. If it be greater than drunkenness, it may well be asked what is to become ,of the nation? Mr. Justice Grantham appears to differ from certain other legal "lights," inasmuch as he believes in the efficacy of labour as a preventative of crime. At Swansea he found a much shorter calendar than usual, and straightway puts it down to the diversion of so many people's thoughts from mischief to events in South Africa, and the more general employment of others owing to the absence at the front of so many reservists. There is nothing peculiarly novel in the suggestion that idleness is an incentive to crime. That "Satan finds some mischief still for idle bands to do," we were taught from our youth up. But the truth came from a poet, and now that we have it also from a judge, it is useless for idlers, pre,umably, to appeal. By a parity of reasoning, it is made to ap- pear that wages and constant employment are the antidotes to crime. Anyhow, the problem is inter- esting from a sociologoical point of view, and if its solution is productive of gooi to the community Mr. Justice Grantham is entitled to the gratitude of the public for a well-timed reminder. .</p>
        <p>- --- From the report of the S-lect Committee on Pire Brigades, it appears that while the larger towns of the provinces are invariably well provided for, the small towns, urban and rural districts, are in an unenviable position. Special instances are recorded and the remedy which the committee suggests for an existing and admitted evil is the constitution of the Rural District Councils as the fire authorities for dealing with fires in villages, although provision may be made to enable any Parish Council,having regard to the size and population of the parish, to be constituted a separate fire authority. Most small towns and villages are better found in all necessary appliances than they were before urban and parish councils sprang into existence, but there is still ample room for improvement in some cases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>-„,•-. PARENTAL NEGLECT.</head>
        <p>-„,•-. PARENTAL NEGLECT.</p>
        <p>Shocking Case at Earlestown,</p>
        <p>At the Newtoa Petty Sessians on Thursday before Mr T. J. Gillespie and other magistrates, Jamee Gormley and Sarah Gormley, of It )biUs•row, Earlestown, were summoned for having on July 25th and divers other dates during the past six months neglected their children, John, William, Fanny, Mary Ellen, and James in a manner lik dy to caase them unnecessary suffering and injury to health. Mr J W Knowles prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C. and stated that on Wednesday, the 25th July, defendant's house was visited by the Inspector for the Socie'y. "that he said was that the children did not appear to be starved, but he found practically no furniture the hous In the front bedroom up- stairs he found 4 heap of rags, and in the hick betlrootrt a couple of straw mattresses. The children bad ,oily half the normal amount of clothing, but the chief thing complained about was the amount of fleas and lice in the house, which was of a most extraordinary character. The Inspector and the doctor's clothing had to be brushed after they came out of the house, and he. (Mr. Knowles) was assured that whatever statement he made With regard to the quantity of lice &amp;1., could not be an exaggeration. The houie was simply swarming, although it was cleaned through before de- fenlants catered into osciipation of it. The house stank, the children stank, the whole place was a" mace ot smell" and most objectionable and injurious to health. This w is simply the state of things which caused typhoid ' and enteric fever which ravaged the country. Be asked the magistrates to inflict a substantial penalty. Tne man was a callier,and his wages amounted to something over £1 a wt ek. The woman was out gossiping when the Inspector visited the house and she did not attend to the children who looked as if they had not been washed ids weeks although defendant said she had washed them the week bef ira (Laughter.) Defendants had simply let the children live like wild animals wi:h the exception that the wild animal had a habit of licking its young and keeping them clan, and the children were not Atte ided to in this respect That was the only distinc- tion. Defendants ought to be made an example of both for their own good and for the goad of the public. P. C. Cla:ka was called ani stated that he visited the house in compa•sy with Inspector Hackett on the 25;h July. The house was in a meat filthy state, ttur was no furniture, and the stench was most horrible. The children were swarming with lice, tnd witness got covered with them There were thousands of fleas and lice of all varletiss. The stench was so great as Co make anyone ill. In answer to the male defendant witness sail that an improvement had been made since his visit on the 25th lJuly. The house was cleaner but one of the girls was still covered with lice. Dr. Gidge stated that he accompanied the Inspector I and the last witness. The fi'thy condition of the house 'was indescribable. There was no furniture except two broken chairs, a crib and two dirty mattresses which were covered with vermin. Witness got covered with fleas.</p>
        <p>Inspector Hackett norroboratel and stAted that ehad never seen si.minv ft-?as in all his exp D.feirlAnt's pioairwl for leniency aril itato I th it the house had been cleanel•</p>
        <p>--iii—an—sw---rio•th7Ch-armin, P.C. Clarke said that when he visited he house on the previous city he found the rooms had been scrubbed a.-4d did not smell si much, The eldest girl was till covered with lice. Considering that the defendants htd no st trved the children. and that an improvement had been effected in the condition of the house, the B met d led to send thi mt] gaol for 14 days and h0p.,1 !hit they would make a n-w start in life when th esrne out, and endeavour to keep their chi'dren resn-,rable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>MANCHESTER SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>MANCHESTER SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>Half-yearly Meeting.</p>
        <p>The half-yearly meeting of the Manchester Ship Canal Company waa held on Thursday m..rning, Mr Bythell presiding. The notic, convening th- meeting having been read, a shareholder askld why the circular contain- ing the report was not issued to the various shareholders in sufficient time fc,r them to read it before they came to the meeting to criticise matters Th 9 Chairman s iid the report was publish,l in the n S 'turd ay last—five days before the m. e'rirug— tnd he had to</p>
        <p>explain to them that the ret,ioe thee did not post the report and statement was owing t ) the lac,: that they ha] some 35,000 or 36,000 sharehoiders and the expenditure would be very eonseterable. -Moving the adoption of the report, the Ch airmail •aid it was cheering to be able to state the while the shipping trade of the district had been falling off, the traffic on the Ship Canal had shown a considerable expansion, and statistics of the trade of Liverpool showed that there was a decrease: This is, no doubt, largely owing to the very large num- ber of foreign steamers having been engaged by the, Government for transport service. The peculiar position of the cotton market had els) a very considerable in- fluence, th re being a great lilting off in the ehipments. 'Fhe scarcity of cotton combined with the dimin- ished exports of Manchester goods end yarn', caused by the Indian famine, and the present disastrous state of affairs in China, was very bad for the trade in Lanca- shire at the present time. Notwithstanding all there circumstances, the quantity of merchandise dealt with in their port during the last half-year was 230,000 tons larger than during the previous half-year, and the re- ceipts showed an increase of £17,160. Manchester bad been an absolutely free port for vessels, but from the beginning of January next dues would be levied, but they would be on a moderate scale. Oceupiers of appropriated berths would also from January have ta pay some rent. From the beginning of next year there would be no more free towage. Assistance would be provided at a very moderato charge, giving tenders to understand that they must begin to impost their penelty charges, and regula- tions had been institute I in order to have a more rapid removal of cargo from the quay. The increased cost of coal from July 1 last involved an Pxpenditura of £5,000 per annem. There was a great future before the under- taking. The motion was seconded and adopted.</p>
      </div>
