      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRI , AUGUST 10, 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRI , AUGUST 10, 1900.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN GAS COMPANY</p>
        <p>At the annual oae,uing,Nll presiding, a dividend of ten per cent Was declared. Mr. W. Handley, J P., was re-e1ec,,,41 a director, and Mr. C. J. Cunningham was re-appoiiited auditor.</p>
        <p>1() ht) SUCCESS OF RUNCORN DOG FANCIERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Davies (Bradley, Br.ige-street), secured two third prizes with his pomeranion dogs at the recent Birkenhead show. Mr LI Briscoe, Church-street, secured a third prize in the Ainidale class.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>SUCCESS OF A WIDNES ATHLETE</p>
        <p>At the-liii:k—en-iie-ad Football Club Sports on Satur- day, G Wright, Farnworth Harriers, came second in the mile flat race with 109 yards start. Wright won his place by inches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>BANK HOLIDAY.</head>
        <p>BANK HOLIDAY.</p>
        <p>Rain fell almost without intermission through the day and in the evening there was a severe thunderstorm Holiday making was at a discount and the railway company suffered accordingly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>LOCAL MUSICAL SUCCESS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL MUSICAL SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>Miss Hilda Waterworth has successfully passed the advanced grade (pianoforte playing) of the Incorporated Society of Musicians EXAMinition held recently at Liverpool. Miss Dorothy Cunning- ham also passed in Grade 11. of the same examina- tion.</p>
        <p>MBILSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Runcorn branch an ex- cursion to London Bridge and Grappenhall, along the Bridgewater Canal took place on Monday. The heavy rains spoiled the pleasure of the excursion. Fortunately the two light:3rs which conveyed the party were covered in; and tea had to be provi led on one of these instead of being partaken of a/ fresco amid the rural beauties of Grappenhalt.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE GO TO CAMP</p>
        <p>'the Runcorn companies of the Otiurcii Lads' Brigade left the town on Monday for Blackpool for a week's encampment at Soutii chore. Arrange- ments had been made for the boy: to go into camp on Saturday, but an intimation was received that the camp ground was flooded. The weather on Monday was of the most unpropitious character.</p>
        <p>VOLUNTEER CYCLISTS FOR THE RUNCORN COMPANIES</p>
        <p>Permission has been given for the formation of two cyclist; sections, of ten each, in connection with the Runcorn Detachment of the 2nd V.B.C.R. We understand a good number of Ole present mem- bers of the detachment have given in their names, but the choice, of course, is no limited to these, and outsiders are inirited to send in their names to Captain George F Ashton, commanding ths Runcorn Companies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>COOLING HIS BRAIN.</head>
        <p>COOLING HIS BRAIN.</p>
        <p>A. Runcorn boatman has discavered a new cure for wife beaters. One day this week he entered the bar ofa well-known hotel in proximity to the Bridgewater Canal. lie seemed consumed with in- ward merriment. " What is the matter" inquired the smart lady of the house with woman's Liatural curiosity. "Oh nowt" replied the man. " only I throwed a mon int' cut (canal) and pulled him eawt agean.'' " Whatever was that for " exclaimed his interrogator with amazement. " Well" answered he " the mon were thrashing his wife, s I thowt a minute int' cut would cool his brain o bit." HONOUR FOR A RUNCORN FREEMASON.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the Pr ,vineial Grand Lodge OtCheshire held at Stockport on Wednesday Lord Egerton, P.G.M., presiding Bro. Charles W Phillips (of Daresbury) Pa,t master of Ellesmere Lodge, Runcorn, was invested as Director of Ceremonies. A formal invitation, to hold the meeting of the P.G. lodge at Runeorn next year was considered but the brethren from West Kirby and Liscard were so insistent that the meeting should be . held there that eventu,-,lly toe Grand Master yielded and the next meetinq will therefore be held iii West Kirby. RAILWAY ACCIDENT NE AR RUNCIRN</p>
        <p>An 'accident, haPpily not of a serious ct.mracter, occurred.to the 3 25 p.m. express train from Liver- pool to Bristol When between Ditton and Run- corn, on the stiff incline there, the crank of the , engine broke, bringing the train to a sudden dead stop, but no possengers complained either of alarm or injury. Within nail an hour another engine was procured, and the train taken baek to Dit; in, awl sent on the down line to Runcorn Mr. Prescot, stationmaster, Runcorn, and \lr. Bailey, wation- raster, Ditton, arranged single line working with commendable promptitude, but the acci 'eat cluled the trains to and from Liverpoll to be run nearly an hour late. The engine was immovable until half- past seven, when a breakdown g "rig, wuic was on the scene at half-past five,bad tewp )rarily rep Aired the damage so that the engine could be dragged into a siding at Ditton. Double lini w theu re- sumed, and traffic took its normal course. The cause of the accident has not transpired</p>
        <p>TERRIBLE FATALITY AT WIDNES. On Saturday evening a stillman engaged at the Globe Chemical Works, Widnes, named Andrew Grey,wno resided at the White Star Hotel, West Baiik-street, met his death under very distressing circumstances. The deceased had been engaged on the works since six o'clock in the morning and had been seen several times during the day by the under manager, Joseph Penny. About 4 p.m. a lad named Charles Law ton went into the yard, and thinking tiara w is some- thing wrong with the deceased he called the atten- tion of Olan Carolan, a foreman. The latter imme- diately went in search of the deceased, and found him with his legs and part of his body in a well containing nitre chloride of acid. He oulle I him out and summoned the under manager, who turned the hose pipe on the deceased. in order to destroy as far as possible the effects of the acid. Grey after- wards walked home with assistance He was heard to exclaim "Hy God, lam don." Dr Creighton flutchinson's assistant, was so iu attendance, and did all he could for the devased, but death took place about six o'clock. The inquest ou the body was held on Wednesday and a verdict of accidental death returned. The jury reco.ntnended that the well should be railed. RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE</p>
        <p>A meeting of the above association was hsld on Saturday evtsninz in the Green way-road School, at which there was a good attendance of members. Mr. Eiward Key, seri., late of Ranc.irn, wa- invited by the president (Mr. Thomas Whyte), to take the chair. Re made a few remarks with regard to the testimonial recently presented to 'dr. Thomas Heesom, who, he said, had done so much for the order since its introduction. FAr many years he had been deeply interested in the movement, and had done all in his power to extend it Nlessrs: Alfred Noden, Daniel Bisbrown, Samuel Tomlinson and Thomas J, Holt also spoke in eulogistic terms of Mr Heesom's services.—Ar Heesom fee'ingly re spended, D Bisbrovvn then referred to the death ot Mr James Monres,who bati,up tottte time his decease occupied an important office This shlwel that the members had great confidence in him, an-I he (the speaker) felt that as he wes an offi.:er if their Division, and also of the Cheshire Gr,n I Division, it would be fitting to pass the following resdution, which he begged to move :—" That. the officers and members of the 'Life Boat Crew' Division a,!know- ledge the loss the order has qa,tairtqcl by the leith of Bro James Moores, Worthy Associat of the Division, and would place on record their appre- ciation of his services to the cause of temperance and would express their sympittl.ty with the family in their bereavement."—This was serionded by the Chairman Mr Edward Key, and warmly supported by Messrs Thomas Cooke, Thomas J Holt. Alfred Noden, Thomas lleesom, Thomas Tt Whyte, and Samuel Tomlinson. RUNCORN CO OPERATIVR '"OCTETY.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting was held on Taeday night under the presidency of Nfr W E Dudley. Tie re- port stated :--Oar trade continu•ts to make unpre- cedented progress, and records are only made by us to be broken. The half-year under review shows a total turnover of £70083 6s 34, a standing testimony to the popularity of co-operation in this district. This represents an increase of £14,601 13s 84 over the corresponding period laNt year, and £5,728 2s 7.1 over last. hall-year. When full pro- vision has beeemade for interest upon capital and the depreciation of our several properties,we have a disposal nett profit of £10,766 13s 21 out of which we purpose to pay a dividend of 31 in the X. This will leave a balance of £266 134 21, and for your pleasure we recommend that £l5O be placed to the credit of depreciation of fixtures account .standing under the head of the central premi:es. These fixtures will go Out of use by the transference of our business to the new premises in Church-street, Runcorn, but of course all aro saleable or capable of future use at, same other point. Your premises and rolling stock have had the usual careful and praper maintenance during the past half-year, and stand in your balances sheet as a very valuable and sound asset. We are always able to record a steady in- crease in membership, which number 139 this six mouthy. The share capital you will perceive haa gone up £2,471 19s 11d, and sound investments for your hard-earned savings have our constant care and consideration. Your loyalty is fully demon- strated by the uniformity of the increases in all our departmental accounts. (Jur new premises in Church-street, Runcorn, are now 'fast approaching completion. and we expect on an early date to be doing a still further increased trade, with greater advantage and comfort to all concerned, be premises will prove a credit to the town, and a monument to co-operation. The report was adopted. The retiring directors .were rapappointed.---- -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>honsEs- KILLED BY LIGHTNING.</head>
        <p>honsEs- KILLED BY LIGHTNING.</p>
        <p>During the storm on Monuay night Mr Alexander Once, of Halton Lodge Farm, had two mares killed by lightning. One mare was in foal. The horses were valued at about .Cl2O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>By the lamented death of Mr. James Handley, a gap !ms been left in the public life of Cheshire, which, it is agreed on all hands, it will be difficult to adequately fill. It is a true .saying that the less work a man has to perform and the lees time he appears to have for work; and Mr. Handley,always working, always appeared to have time to accept and fulfil new duties. Multifarious as were his pri- vate concerns, attended to with great exactitude and thoroughness, he nevertheless de.vote,d much time to the public weal o is nativ• county of Cheshire. end of his adopte.: town of Garston. Gifted with a talent for figures and finance, calm of. judgment., energetic and peNevering, ever desirous to do the right thing, h undertook no posvion of administration, trust or honour that he ;tid not fill with cre.dik to himself and profin to thos- whom he served. Ile had an almost infinite capacity of tak- ing pains.</p>
        <p>It was a striking testimoAN to his chAracter and ability to be chosen as Chirmin of 01,1 lotiap,insa- tion Board at North wice, under the Sab,idences Act, a,ti-office requiring great tact, ski i judg- mem, in which,as Mr Flettner testifiA Runc,)rn Court on Tu-sday, he earned the adtutradon or his colleagues. As County Councillor. Eis Urban Councillor, as a trustee of the river Wert---er and as a tuagistnte and in nacti,-r.us other Qffi'!es he im- pressed all who came in contact with him with his intellectual skill, his sine rir,y, and his opright judgment. It is not generally known thlt tor some years he held • a seat on the defunct Board of Im- provement Commissioners. This was in the early 'seventies, andalttiough then only a youTig man his abilities were recognised an las Chairman of the Finance Committee he accomplished work of method and organisation which is bearing fruit to- day. His. endeavoured in i- sphere to leave the world bert,ir than he found if, and succeeded, and in the elocrvnt words of Mr. Norlian he has " passed in•so toe world of silence followed by the euediction of all his fe!inw-men."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>m...mm.410.111M0i11n THE LATE Vria JAMES HANDLEY.</head>
        <p>m...mm.410.111M0i11n THE LATE Vria JAMES HANDLEY.</p>
        <p>The Funeral.</p>
        <p>The funeral of the late Mr. JaCaeS Handley, J.P., C.C., of lioliy Bank, Garsten, .shose death we re- ported last w eek, took placa on Saturday and de- znoastratel the great respec. in which the deceased gentleman was held in Garst ,n, Runcorn and neigh- nourhood. Nlr Handley was a native of Runcern. He was one of four sons of the late Mr. Thomas Handley who in the first halt of the last century was a boot manufacturer an 1 in rchaet in the town In religion he was a Congregationalist and pos- sessed strong and sound views; whilst palitically he was a staunch Liberal, always able alit aniline. to give a reason for hi. opinion and Sir John Brunner postessed no truer or firmer supporter. When Mr. James had received to education considered requisite for a commercial career, he enterea into partnership with the late Mr. Gibson, as ir nynonger imnd gneral furnisher. in Fountein-squ ire, Rune.Orn. thi the re- tirement o Mr. Gibson, tee firm took the title of Messrs Handley and Beek, and until the time of his death the deceased maintained an interest in that business. Mr. Handley, in the course of his life, acquired a wonderful knowledge of French, and could in every respect be termed e most accom- plished linguist. His appetote for travel was insati- able and to his friends it was well-known he would rush from one end of the country to the other on the slightest call of business. He visited Switze-- land. France, and other co.itinental ceen.-ries and acquired a fund of knowledge that eminently fitted him fir the financier he ultimately came to be Indeed his ability as a financier was acknowledged and he guided many enterprises to a successful issue. He engaged extensively in quarrying at Weston Point and when the private owners there formed themselves into a co npanv, so oe years ago Mr Handley was made one of the directors, HA VJV also a (lirector of the Runcora Gas Company. Frei - chain Gas Company, Aldershot Gas Company, and was also an active shareholder in he great concern of Pares „Banking Company Limited, as well as a Trustee of the Runcorn Savings Bank. He was also interested in rainy commercial enterprises his knowlelge of trading and commerce causing his advice te he often reqaisitiened and highly valued. He took a deco interest in public questioe. hut the only locil office (excepting his c)nnectien with the Saving. Bauk), so far as public bodies in Runcorn were concterned,was as a member of the now defunct Burial Board, and which body by the way honoured him with its chairmanship. During the tenure of this office Mr.Hanaley was responsible for putting the finances upon a sounder basis. At a Cheshire County Council Election in 1899 Mr Handley con- tested Runcorn South against, the late Nlr Philip Speakman but was unsuccessful. Subsequently, though, on the retirement of Mr Councillor Taylor from the representation of Runcorn North, Mr Handley was nominated fe. the position and elected without. opposition H. was a mernberof the Standing Joint:Committee and of several ether important committees ef the ICounty Council where his work was greatly valued. He was also a member of the River Weaver Truat. On Friday, a meeting of the Trnstees was held to deal with some important business initiated by mfr fiandley.and the trustees unanimously passed a vote of condolence with the widow and family At the same tame they placed on record their appre- ciation of the late Mr ilandley's character and abili- ties and of tee valuable services he had rendered to the Trust. About ten. years ago he was made a Justice of the Peace for the County of Cheshire and about tour years later he was placed upon the Com- mission of the Peace also for the larger and more imp)rtant, Ceunty Palatine of Lancaster. His knowledge of Magisterial Law was great. and he sat regularly at the Petty SeSSi,llB at Runcorn and Garston. For the last 14 years or more he resided ,at liclly Bank, Garstoe, and avail the forloation of District Councils in 1894 was elected a member of the first Urban District. Council at Garstnn, a posi- tion he fittingly discharged up to his ,lemise. BY his death the inhabiteets of both Runcorn and Gar 'ton lose a man of much public spirit, great knowledge, iategrity and unblemished henoer. The ac at rit,s were performed at Run 'orn ne.,netervs The coffin it was arranged, should be taken to Runcorn. by the train leaving Allerton at 1.50 a-id the nrocessionJ trom Holly Wink to the railway station was of consider- able length, and included Inspector WIC-a-A and a detachment of police, members of the Urban Council of GarSton, numerous residents and scholars of the Congregational Sunday Shool, in addition to the chief mourners. Amongst those present were: Messrs C Battigate, Btttiga*e, AE D Ivies R Sinlr. Jones. k Drew, T Talbot, J Pckhall, J Tailor, R Hold ing, T Hodirkinson, James Harris, F Rope, John Elven and J Pitt Kethlev ; representing Gtrs'on Urhin Coun- cil there were J Rigby (chairman), Couocillors Pt ir Mos. C Stubbs, J Rawlinson, F D Cox (clerk), F Roden (surveyor). The followinr memb,rs of dscetsed's Sunday school class were present: MeFsre 11 Hughes.S McCullock, Warburton, J Meredith, `J., Wilkins„l Craig- , a Rinser, Pys and T Howarth. Most of these tra- velled on to Runcorn. On arrival at the latter town the mournful cortege was formed in the following order : Police, Maeis rates. County Councillors, Runcorn Urlsan Council, Canton Urban Council, Technical Instruction Committee. tradesmen and fri,n Is, hearse, coaches. The procession included Super- intendent Pearson, ex-Superintendent S:oim, In soector Hicks, Inspector McKcand : Messrs F iNlrmani J.P. G L DtVieFi. IP., A R Norman, J.P., Dr Bowman, T Ward, J.P., (iNortliwich), Mr F Johnson (clerk to Runcorn justices), Me E Imisun, (1.0., Messrs J 13 Sal- D Hisbrewn, James Pritchard. F a ese Wi'son, F A (Atka. Tlbote, J Willing, Dr Robinson, J El • Frith (Runcorn Gas Co). J M Frith. Grsorge 8 Frith (Frodsharn Gas Co ), R W E swards (Aldershot Gts and Water Co.), Jonathan Florsfield, James Collier (Weston Qutrries), A Lighthurn, W Fiche', Ctptain Riglayf(Dannottar Castle), Captain W Rowlands, James Lea, R Rushton, George Cooke (Weston Point Towing Co.), Ernest S Inman (representative River Weaver Trustees),A E Fletcher(Northwich),J Buttnn,L Rosenberg (Weston Point), C Leach, W 13 Chad coft":k:fl John Pritchard, Jesse Rhodes, William Bite, E Key, J B 'Chadwick, James Beck, John Beck, Joseph Littler, John</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <p>Littler, Joseph Ormson, S Watson, J M Williams (chair- ma.: Runcorn Board of Guardians), G Bellamy, George Summerfleid, Thomas Goke, C Dutton, John Heyes, J Bothwell (Frodsham), S Pope, W Shaw (Seamen's Mis- sionary), E fayl, r„Percy Buckley, D H Jones and C J Cunningham, W Carroll (representing Mes-rs. P. Speakman and Sins, Liverpool). The occupants of the coaches were the Rev. T Cole, Congregational minister, Gaist-in, Dr. Humphreys, , medical a.tteudant Messrs. F Beck, El Bell, C Hodgkin- son, George F Handley (son) Rev S B Handley and Mr. W Handley (brothers) J Ihst.ock (son-in-law), Messrs. Thomaa Handley (son) El W Handley, T B Havdley, C ' F HandlPy, and George Forester (nephews), H Bostock (Stafford) James Shaw (ex-superint,andent Northern Division London and North Western Railway Company), R C Gray (Edinburgh) and John Dormand. There were many signs of mourning en route t 3 the cemetery. The burial service in the chapel and at tne grave side was conducted by the Rev T Cole, who aelivered an impressive address. By request there were no flowers. At the cemetery the cortege was met by Messrs J W Deakin, chairman ot Nortbwich Urban Council, and F A Cowley, clerk, And E T Wood sur- veyor to N3rthwich Compensatkn Bard. The bearers were Messrs F and J Hewitt, J Lightfoot, and J Smith (emploj es of Messrs Handley and Beck). Rev. T. Cole's Oration.</p>
        <p>After the service in the chapel the Rev. T. Cole gave an oration over the body of tee departed. After referr- ing to the swidennrss of Mr. Handley's death,the minis ter said ail tie Ii h site weld go out to the stricken wi ox and the eneriwing sons and daughters and rela- dves iii glen!. An 1 yet serrossfut though they were and ea; urai thoaeh it W%3 to give expression to their grin', therm wiB else another feeling in them which strove tor atteranc 1. " I have heard many people speek of Mr. Handley as woe of 'nature's gentlemen ' "said a young meia on the day of his death, "but" he continued "I prefer to call him one of $ tie men.'" They accepted that nut only as a better description but also as a descriptiou mere in harmony with Christian faith. A good man was owe of Gee's best gifts to the world. Hence, hewever saddened and pmplexed they might be at the dispensation of God through the removal of their friend, if tb,,re was one thing more than another that he (Mr. Cole) would wish to do to-day it was this—to express their fervent gratitude to God for the men Mr. Handley proved himself to be as seen in his life end wo(k. H . was seen to be one of "God's men" in tne discharge of his business duties daily during the esrlier portion of his life, and still more so in cennec • tisn with his inutrifirieus public activities during recent years, "Whatever thy head fineeth to do, do it with thy might." mat text must often have been in Mr. Elandtey's mind. At all events his strenuous and busy life made him a living exemplification of it. But that was not all. "Woatsoever ye do in word or deed do it in the name of t,IP Lord Jesus Christ." This was a still greater principle—the spirit in which a man did his worn being of far 4r,iater importance than the nature or quantity of toe work done. Mr. Handl.-.37 was always mindful of this. He was a man of strict ieteerity and uprightness arid withall be was often able by the exer- cise of great tai:h to win his way whsre others won d have failed. H.• would make no foolish claim on M. Handley's behaif, but he did say that at bottom Mr. Handley was essentially a religious min— a man who lived under his great taskmaster's eye—and that was the great thing. Moreover his religion was marked by great sanctity. Too hones-, to be ever bandying religious phrases on his lips, the spirit of the Christian faith still wrvaded all he said and did. He believed in the institution of public worship and he was profoundly convinced that separated from spiritual fellowship a man suffered an irreparable loss. Onerous a: were his duties he was rarely absent from his place in Church on Sunday In the choir and in the Sunday school he was a most devoted helper. His class--he senior class of young men—would miss him sorely. In- cli.ed they would all miss him. As the secretary of the Church during the whole of his (Mr. C)le's) pastorate an 1 for some years previ 'us he brought all his business aptitude to bear up on his work. To every good word he could utter all the officers and members would say "Amen." Much also ought to be said of his generosity in the Church and out of it. All sorts of philanthropic 1 societies had WS sympathy and as far as it lay in his power to help them he gave liberally 1 ti their import. And then how much could ' be said about him in the home, in the bosom of his family. It could never be too frequently repeated that the home as we know it, was the outgrowth ot the spirit of the B'ble Verieruver Bible toad gone is had pure fied and exalted family life : It was given te hire (Mr. C 01,..) as to few to see how much the home was to Mr. Handley and how blessed he was in his family life. He must desist from saying too much about this. It was toe sacred. Ho mentioned it here partly because it was fitting at such a time as this, that they should rem m bar how inn h they ale eeved God for their hem-a, and partly b :cause the sorrow of the widow and family reminded them of their claim to sympathy, and prayers of them all. " The Lord gave and the Lord tekete away, blessed be the name of the Lord." It Was easy to a ter the first, clause of these va wets ; it was not easy at this ailment to alter the secend. Tne strickee acid iw and Limit'. must b per- fectly conscious of this. But they must pray for them and it might be that as teey cannot fail to realise the divine goodness in the bestowment of the life that was so dear to tte.m,they would yet learn that:such gtoodness must "order all things well." Our capacities wee not all of the same order and the work of life was varisd. But day by day in the world, in the crutch aol in tie home, God was divine u 3 all opportunities of noble service. Le, us not b -hot ourselves as to what consti- tutes new life We live in dseds act years ; in thoughts not breaths ; In felines not in figures on a dial. We should count true file by heart throbs,—he most lives Who thinea most fees the noblest act the best</p>
        <p>se,z pacl day all the opportunities God grants us for hum, 4..rvice, to accept each day as God's gift for the dev,lopmen,. of character, to think and speak and act as in the sigiit ot One to whom we must give an account -f our stewardship—that was life. That was Mr. Handley's view and to all •Rho accepted it there came the glorio is ins iratl3n of " a hope that malieth not ashamed." "0 death where is thy sting? 0 grave where is thy victory ? Thri sting of death is sin and the strength of sin is the law. But thanks be to G.id WhO giveth us the victory through our L'ird Jesus Christ."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Magisterial References.</head>
        <p>Magisterial References.</p>
        <p>At R reeorn Pet.v S )u on T ieselay, Mr. F. J. Nerinan (chairman) said, bef3re proceeding with the ordinary busii.eQs of th , Court it was only fitting that seitable reference should be male to the irreparable loss they had sustainel in tilt deeth of Mr James Handley. Tee family of Handley was closely itrerwov,m into the history of that locali*.y and he need hardly s teat Mr Jdrael bad fully maintainA the best traditions of that f-irn (M No man) should not trust him- self to speak of his personal friendship with Mr. floyiley bet should . content himself by stating that it hal been life long and einbrek-'n and in his °pillion tit town bad never produced a safer 2u dolor ~neryoung man to follow than their late friend. He (Mr. No man) had never known a man se WU of energy and yet wthall so devoid ot that objectionable aggressivenees which sometime made energy a question- able qual.ty. Flandlee was alwAya at work. Now, the trestai cremcillor of a private friend in whose best interests he gr,oill I • go to any amount of trouble ; now the moving spirit in BOW great commercial an dertaking ; now the public servant pro- cheat, of his flowers for the goneraL good. Toat court knew him chiefly in his magisterial capacity, and no man ever botter qualified himself for that great trust. His condu it of the puhic husiness,the soundness of his ju,-Igment commanded the r spect of all, while the more p..csrate duties of th °ffie. his great ability and accuracY inspired the admiration of bit Cileagues. Th.,v would all Jon with tim (Mr Nmnan) in expressing their deep sympathy with tilt b.reavel o tee. He (Mr Nat.. ma) A:wa .9 felt on t hes occasions how pdnfully im- po-.eet .748 anything short or the Divine consoletien,but atte- ell it wood he a gree comfort ie) Mr. Handley' wife an ; faerly to realise that his life's work had or-en s ) well done and that he had passed into the land of siinince !Paving behind him only gracious memories in I the minds of his fellow men. • Mr. Burton oe tetalf of th, selicitcrs practicine at the Coen, xpressed hew fully and completely they approved of ev-ry word that had fallen frcm Mr. Norman in reference to their friend Mr. Handley, for he was the friend of al!. He slould Also like to join in the ex- preesion of sympelty with the wdow and Emily. Mr A. E. Fletcher (Northwich) said he would like to add his word to thole which had fallen so well from Mr. Norman's lips and from the lips of Mr. Burton, of the appvciation they all had of Mr. Handley. They in Northwich had shown their appreciation of Mr Handley and his sterling qualities and of his honest, upright judgment, by electine him at Chairman of the Com- pensaion Board, which wis appointed to minister nice questions of valuation and other quostions under the compensation of sutsidence act, and this was looked upon bota by the County Council and by his colleagues as being a position few which ha was eminently adapted. Mr. Handley had a cool, clear judgment which was thoroughly reliable and he also brought to boar upon the subjects placed before him the utmost possible energy and a desire to get at the truth. He was sure that his colleagues on the Board would feel his loss very much, indeed they hardly ' know where to look for a Demon to replace him. Mr. Handley was a man respected by everyone who knew him and to When: his life would ever be a pleasant memory for Mr Handley was one who heartily sought to leave the world better than he found it and did his duty in every station of life in wbich Ite waa placed. Mr J W Knowles of Widnes endorsed what had been said, and said he had always found Mr Handley a genial kind hearted gentleman, who was held in the highest estimation.</p>
        <p>Mr F Johnson (magistrates's clerk) also endorsed what had been said of the war Mr Handley performed his magisterial duties and mentioned that not one single decision of his had ever been questioned nor ant- appeal made against his decisions in any way. The officials of the Court felt they had lost a sincere and kind friend. Superintendant Pearson observed that the police bad • -</p>
        <p>ievery confidence in Mr Handley who was all they could I desire. He was a man and a gentleman. The business ot the Court was then proceeded with. Garston Condolence.</p>
        <p>The monthly meting of the Garstou Urban District Council was held on Wednesday night at the Public Offices, Grassendale, Mr. J. Rigby in the chair.—Before proceeding with the ordinary business, the Chairman referred to the great loss the Council had sustained ny the death of Mr James Handley, J.P. He felt that he had lost an able and valued colleague, and he thought the Council had lost a member whom it would be difficult to replace. He moved that a letter of condolence be sent to Mrs Handley and family in their bereavement—Mr C. Stopford seconded, and the motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>LICENSING PROSECUTIONS AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>LICENSING PROSECU- TIONS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>SAILORS IN THEIR CUPS. A Man Stabbed.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Petty Sessions on Tuesday, before Messrs. A. R. Norman and W. Handley', Clara Marsh, keeper of the Sailors Home beerhouse, Regent-street, was sum- moned for permitting drunkenness on her premises. Mr. A. E. Fletcher, prosecuted for the police, and Mr. J. W. Knowles defended. Mr. Fletcher in opening, said on Sunday niglit the 22nd of July, a Dane named Otto Schmidt, carpenter on board the Celtic Mo.larch, was found in Dukes field, in a state of mania from drink. He was a maniac for the time being and had stabbed a youn; man named Findlow. So dangerous was his con- dition that several men had secured him with ropes and he was locked up. It was often alleged against the police that they ought to fad out where persons tamed loose on the streets in a dangerously drunken condition, ob- tained their drink from. The ruagistrates would be asked that morning to solve the problem,where Schmidt got his drink so as to reduce him to the dangerous state of mind he was in. ,</p>
        <p>Otto Schmidt said he was earp,nter on board the Celtic Monarch, which arrived at lidacorn about noon on the 22nd July. Mr. Fox, tailor, boarded the ship and he and several sailors came ashore with Mr. Fox and went to his shop. They stayed at his shop for an hour and a half ta two hours. They did not have any- thing to drink at the shop, but Mr. Fox took them to the Sailors Home between four and five o'clock. They had some beer. They stayed in the house a good while; he could not say how long. He had no idea how much beer he had. He went into the ya:cl and when he re— turned into the room he remembered nothing more,until the following morning when he lound himself in prison coo a charge of stabbing a man. He did not know how mach money he bad when apprehended but he had a guld pi,ce—be could not tell whether a sovereign or half a sovereign—which he had received from Mr Fox before he want in Mai Sailors Home.-13y Mr. Knowles: He could not tell whether he had drink anywhere else than toe Sailors Home or not.—Re-examined by Mr. Fletcher: fie did not go into any puolic house except the Sailors hne. It was getting dark when be left that house.</p>
        <p>P.C. Rigby described Schmidt's condition when he apprehended him for stabbing Findlow as very drunk. Schmi:it had 7s lid upon him. NPxt evening be called at the Sailors Home in company with PC. Bowyer and Mrs. Marsh told them that Mr. Fox and her son Willie brought five sailors to the house about half past four and the carpenter stayed until after nine o'clock. During that time he was drinking beer. He appeared to be sober when he left her house; out when a man had been on a long voyage a drop of drink soon got hold ot him. The night following witness again visited the Sailors Home and told Mrs Marsh she would be reported for permitting drunkenness and she then said she had made a mistake in telling him that Schmidt had stayed in her house until nine o'clock and she called her son Willie who said he left at halt past six. She also called Jack Gibson and asked him what time " Ch'ps " left and added " Didn't he leave at half past six." Gibs )n replied : "No it was nine o'clock when he left."</p>
        <p>P.C. BJwyer and P.C. Carrington corroborated these statelncnt3.</p>
        <p>P.C. Wilson depus.A to seeing Schmidt staggering drunk in High-street at 9.45 on the Sunday night. He went down Regent-street in the direction of the Sailors Home.—By Mr Knowles: He did not lock him up 13-cause he was viet and not interfering with anyone. Peter Johnson, Hi:chmou;h's Cmir-, said he went in the Sailors Home- at-9 p.m.on the Sunday in question and Schmidt was there with several other sailors. He was not drunk. but "half and half." Half an hour later he saw Schmidt standing in the street with some dailors. Joseph Gesler, Norwegian, able seaman on the Celtic Monarch, deposed to going to Mr. Fox's shop and then entering the Sailors Hems between four and five. He went out at 9 o'clock and left Schmidt there. Schmidt an:l somebody else were quarrelling when he left. Wito, ss made several contradictory statements and in cross examination said Schmidt went nut with Gibson before nine o'clock. Schmidt .a as not drunk. George Findlow repnsted evidence already reported as to how Schmidt attacked him when taking him to the doeks Schmidt was very drunk.</p>
        <p>T. West, mate on the steamer Helen also deposed Schmidt was very drunk:. . . _</p>
        <p>M. Knowles for the defence pointed out discrepancies in the ev‘denee and said .7,c1-10-licit'B evidence was not fit ta hang a do-1, on. He would prove that Schmidt lett the house with Gibson and was away or two hours and when he returned had no drink in the house. He, how- ever, sabmitted that on the evidence of the prosecution there W 39 no case for him to answer.</p>
        <p>rho inistrates retired and were absent ten minutes. Mr, Norm4n said they did not think there was suffi- ci, t evidence to convict and they had decided to dis- miss the c.ise.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Marsh was then charged with selling liquor to a drunken person named Joseph Gesler on the 27th July. P S. Gregory deposed that on Friday the 27th July he was standing in Devonshire Square between 11 and 12 o'clock noon, and he saw Gester and another little stiff man come out of the Sailors Home. G sler was drunk and caught the other man by the arm and they passed him in the square. He followed them and saw them , enter the Holshead Harbour. At that time a person came and told him a child was in the Bridgewater Canal and he found the child was dead. After getting par- ticulars of the accident for the inquest at 12 30 he was again in Devonshire Square and me t• P.C. McLeod. He saw Gesler coming over Doctors Bridge from the direction of Greenway Read. Gesler went down an entry which led to the back door ot Mr. Fox. He did not ray long and then went down High Street. At two o'clock he foend G. Filer at the Sailors Home. He was drunk He had before him two glasses one fun of beer and one half full. He had a plate from wnico h- was eating meat. Another sailor was lying on the sofa and he got up and drank his beer and went out. McL• od peinted out to the son ef Mrs Marsh that Gesler was dunk and he replied "On yes,' know he is stopping here." P.C. MoLeon told him he had no right to serve him with neer in that state and the son replied " He has Mot been ,erved." McLe.od said "Oh yes he has, he lag got two of beer before him." The sou sail "Oh neer ; th- servant must have served him " While they were talking Gesler emptied the full glass. The son :added li•• was in the yard when Gesler entered and he heard him shouting for beer. The servant now entered and anrni'ten that she had served Gesler with a glass oh beer w .on he came in. McLeod ask q.l "How do you acceunt -tr the sf,cond glass ?"she repiied " Oh I gave him alit with his dinner." At that moment Mrs. Marsh appeared aed said Gcsl-r had had no beer tin re ; he had been ramhisn2, out all morning. The son said "Oh yes, Lily has inrved hi n." Gesler asked witness and McLeod to have a drink, and Mrs. Marsh took hold ef his arm and led him upstairs, telling him he would leel better :,fter he had had a lie down. On returning Mrs Mareli maid " That's the way l!nr. Gregory Ido with them when they have had a drop if drink." He had Gesler under observation since the 22ed and he had seen him drunk about the street every day, except Friday and Saturday last. P.C. McLeod gave corroborative testimony. He des- cribel Gesler as staggering drunk and incoherent in his speech. Hannah Tatlock, wife of James Tatlock, licensee of Holyhead Harbow. Inn, High-street, depesed to Geslcr and another sailor entering the house between eleven and twelve o'clock on the 27th. Gesler asked far a bottle of stout and the other man for a bottle of lemon- ade. She refused to serve stout and each had lemonade. At half-past twelve Gesler came again and she refused to serve him. He was drunk. Sarah Beardmae, wife of Robert Beardinan, Man at the Wheel beerhouse, Church street, said (kegler came to her house;between 12 aul 1.39, and she would not ser* e him because he was drunk.</p>
        <p>Annie Warder, wife of Thomas Warder, living at the Lord R:dney. Church-iitreet, said the prisoner entered the house about two o'clock. She thought he had bad enough aod refused to serv., him. He cline agaih, and again she refused.—Cross-examined : Gesler staggered a little, but he was quite polite.</p>
        <p>. . Mr. Knowles for Me defence, saw he could not deny that Gosler had had some drink, but pointed out that Gosler wss lodging at the hz)ule and he was only sup- plied witii a !lase of beer to his dinner. He asked the magistrates to decide that this was not unreasonable and was what would be done in any first class hotel in the land. It was not a case in which a conviction should be recorded, and be was quite willing to pay costs if the caFe were dismissed.</p>
        <p>- Lily 110111.c:so:I, single woman servant at the Sailors Bone, said when Gtsler came in he asked for his dinner and she gave him a glass of beer with it. He stoad her a bottle of lemonade, and that was the other glass the constables saw• He walked straight and was very quiet.—Cross-examined witness said Gesler went out of the house before any of them were up that morning. She denied that Mrs. Marsh helped him up to bed ; he walked up himself. He did not get up again until next morning. John Marsh son of the defendant, said he was brush- ing the yard when Geater came in. He had been out all</p>
        <p>Imorning. He walked all right and in his opinion he wag not drunk. He had boarded at the imuse skce July 220 d. Cross-examined. Be denied that II acknowledged to the plide officers that Gesler was drunk. There was no doubt he had had some drink. The B -neh alter brief retirement, fi-ied the defend- ant 40s -.IA c)sts £3 121 61—The lic!!re-e would n .t be gnd.</p>
        <p>Gesier was then charged with being drunk Cu the premisea. He replied that he had never been drunk since he came to Runcorn —P.S. Gregory and P.C. McLeod gave evidence that he was very drunk —Deiendant was fined 5s and ct.sts 104 6d or 14 days. Otto Sc.;lll.oitit was now charged on remand with maliciously wounding George Fincilow by stabbing bina on Sunday night the 22nd of July under circumstances fully reported. Mr. Fletcher said the police wet%) dis- posed to ilynk the man was mad with drink at the time, and Mr. Burton, solicitor, for the prisoner had a proposition to make.—Mr. Burton put in discharges covering fit teen years, each one of which was marked " V.G " very good and Captain David ',Wig of the Celtic Mon arch, would testify that his conduct had been most exemplary during the thirteen month i he had been with the suip on a voyage to the Cape, New South Wales, San Francisco and back to England. His attack on Findlow appeared to have been a mad impulse. HO suggested that justice would be met if the prisoner com- pensated Final° w and paid the doctor's fees and other expenses, and iae urged this course bnause the prisoner was a for igner —Cap:Ain Lewis swor.), said he Lover had a quieter man on his ship and be would take hima bark it t.): was ci,!arel of this charge —Fadlow agreed to accla', c.)rnpeusation and the prison-T vas discharged Mr. F. C. B. &amp;Tweet', Danish vice Cir.sai, was present on the ori..oner's,. :kali. After term era arranged Mr: fiasvlt )a, sup,rliti;endent of cus:-,!;) I, handed over the .</p>
        <p>Potato Disease in Cheshire.</p>
        <p>The he .vy rains ot the past 1-2 w days have flooded many potato fields in Cheshire, and potato disease has sg, in. Th a early sorts hAve been got free fro :1 disease, and marketed in ;Aoal. condition. The diseme has attacked the later ,Jrt, which in many plac F, it iS fl-tred will rot ilstead of ripen entailing heavy !a.m. Cheshire Salt Subsidences.</p>
        <p>At a tne-..tin4 ot the Northwich Lit ban Council it was repor ed that a serious subsidem'e of sixty yards circumference had occurred in Verdin Park, the cavity extending beneath the plank piling twerry- eight feet deep. An urgency c Immitte9 was appointed to deal with the matter. The Cheshire Coutiky Council wrote offering to contribute .C5OO towards restoring high-street, North wich, to the original level. This street has sunk three feet six inches in less than eight years. The County Coun- cil's offer was accepted. All the prlperty in the street will have to be raised, and heavy claims will be made upon the &amp;it Compensation Board. • Buckley-street Choir</p>
        <p>The members of duckley-street ceoir held their annual picnic on Monday last, to Alderley Edge. On arrival lunch Iva* partaken of at Mr T Bowers' Public Hall Restaurant. Owing to the inclemency of the weather a programme was arranged consist- ing of choruses by the choir, solos by the Misses Kean and a recitation by Miss L Fairhurst.Mr 'Crow accompanied. Teti concert took place in a room kindly lent by the host. About fiv- o'clock tea was partaken of. After tea speeches were made by Messrs. Jennings and Wibberley, tiianking the host and hosties for the very excellent manner in which they had provided the meals and e vote of thanks was carriA with musical h ,nours A vote of thank-- was also tendered to Mr Ryder for his labours in m ikiiig•tne picnic asaccses. 'dome was reached About 11 o'clock after a very enjoyable though we' day. Sir J. T. Brunner, Bart, and Tied Houses, Tile foilowi.ig re the operative ciaeses of the bill for the abli ..):1 of tie I houses' briught in by Mr . Broadhur t, Sir Jihn Brunuer, Mr Tomas Bayley, 'Mr Herbert Lewis, and Mr Hazell : —(1) From and after the pa —ing of this Act any c. venant or agree- ment binding the holder of a license tor the sale of ale, bser, or spirituous liquors for consumption on the premises, to purchase such ale, beer, or spiritu- ous liquors, or any part thereof, from any specified person or peon- only shall be deemei to be an un- reasonatOe coeen,int in restraint of trade, and shall be void. Pr.)vi led always, that thiii section shall not apply to 90 V license grantad bt fore the passing, of this Act unless and until application shall be made for a renewal thereof.(2)Upen any application for the grant or renewal of a license for the sale of ale, beer, or spirituous liquors, to be consumed on the premises. tie licensing authorities shall require the applican f satisfy them that n, is not under- any sueh co:enant or agreement as aforesaid and that in the case of an application for the renewal of a licen-e that his been granted or renewed subse- quently to t;le passing of this Act. that he has not been under any such covenant since the granting or last renewal of the license, as the ease may be, and unless the ~sid licensing authority is so satisfied, it shall not grant; or renew such license.</p>
