        <head>HEALTH AND STRENGTH.</head>
        <p>HEALTH AND STRENGTH.</p>
        <p>Nature's best gifts are those that contribute towards health and strength. The active principle of the best remedial agents (sarsapsrilla,burdock, gentian,lavender, saffron, dandelion, and quinine) constitute Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, the most reliable tonic, purely vegetable.oHealth has many handmails, and all depend on strength of nerve and mwole. We exact too much from these in tho daily competitive struggle for success in life, and we have to suffer in consequence—indiges- tion, weakness, nervousness, sleeplessness, liver dis- orders, chest affections, and numerous other complaints. Now all these are successfully combatted with the sus- taining assistance of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, the best remedy of the age. It is safe, sure, and per- manent in its effect. If you find that your system is unable to bear the oppressive heat, and you are almost unable to follow your daily vocation, immediately strengthen your constitution by taking a course of Gwilym Evan's Quinine Bitters. They make tne weak strong, and give new heart and ''go" to the weary toiler. They revive the spirits and strengthen the nerves. By giving tone to the whole constitution they ward off fevers and other ailments. No family zhould be without a bottle. An occasional dose taken when a man has felt slightly out of sorts, has prevented many a long illness. We assert the above confi- dently. Man is much more subject to the influence of his surroundings than is generally supoosed. The ill effects upon the general health of living in close ill- 'ventilated, and badly-lit homes, offices, or workshops, are, of course, well-known to everyone who has studied hygiene; while the life giving effects of sunlight and fresh air are equally well known. The same may be said of the results of the various climatic changes. The injuri- ous effects of a prevailing east wind are evident to all observant persons: and the depressing influence of an approaching thunderstorm on acme constitutions has also been observed by mas people. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters may be termed an insurance policy against" catchinr, cold." for it gives stamina and a healthy vitality to resist climatic changes and is a reliable safeguard against colds, chills, and coughs, which frequently happen through the weakness of the constitution being unable to resist the effects of the changeableness of the weather. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters never disappoints when given a fair trial, and no medicine has had so many imitators, but the purchaser has the remedy against such deceit in his own hands. Note particularly the name" Gwilym Evans" on the label, on the stamp, andon the bottle (a threefold precaution),without which none are genuine. Refuse all others then you will have the satisfaction of benefitting by the unrivalled virtues of this great tonic. Gwilrm Evans' Quinine Bitters is sold in bottles 29. 9d. and 43. 6d. each by chemists and patent medicine venders, or will be sent direct by the proprietors, without any extra charge to any address in the United Kingdom. .</p>
        <p>Sole Proprietors:—Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales.</p>
        <p>Monks at 11.30 the previous nic!ht. They were both under the influence of drink. The man WAS copking his supper and the woman and three children were lying on a dirty mattress upstairs. The Chief Constable proved 10 previous appearances against the man and 9 against he woman. He also informed the Bench that the prisoners' little child was running about on the cold flags of the cells in its bare feet that morning.—The chairman: Pigs have more consideration.—The Bench decided to send the femaleprisoner to gaol for three months and the man for one month with hard labiur.</p>
        <p>THREE MONTHS FOR NEGLECT OF CHILDREN-A BAD CASE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Tu-Adm.', before the Mayor and other magistrates, John Dunn, Howard- street, Sankey Bridges, was summoned for neglecting his children, John, Mary. Valentine, and Richard, in a manner likely to cause them unnecessary suffering and injury to health—Mr. A Browne prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C. and stated that the defendant was a peddler and if he would work could earn 30s. a week. tie wonld not work regularly and was continually drink- ing. His wife, through his illtreatment, was becoming half-witted, the illtreatment consisting of not providing sufficient money. Last week he gave her 7s. with which to keep the whole family and pay the rent. The mother of the defendant's wife had constantly provided food for the children and had taken them in when they bad been turned out by the defendant at midnight and early in the morning. Had it not been for her help they would have been starved. The man had had every chance of r.formine. Inspector Hackett and P.C. Woods, had warned him time after time. The Inspector had visited him at least 30 times and be had been under the influ- ence of drink on 20 occasions.—Defendant's wife gave evidence bearing out the above statement and stated that the defendant insisted upon having good food him- self. She and her children had not had any flesh meat for a fortnight. Last Sunday they had potatoes and a little salt and on Monday they had bread and butter to eat. Defendant was constantly drinking and her mother had given food and clothing to the children.—Mrs. Ferber. who resides at 53,Howley-lane and is the mother of defendant's wife informed the Bench that her daugh- ter and children had often come to her house at mid- night, defendant having come home " mad drunk "and turned them out.—lnspector Hackett said he had visted the house 30 recorded and several unrecorded times. He Ind repeatedly warned the defendant whom he had found in a state of drunkenness no less than 20 times. The house and children were kept clean. The children were poorly nourished on( little girl being emaciated. Last Thursday when he visited the house defendant was "beastly drunk." Wit- ness had seen food consisting of eggs and bacon and new potatoes which defendant had refused to eat. He admitted having thrown food away many a time. The food which he (the Inspector) saw wag perfectly good P.O !Woods said defendant's wifelhad made a gvat many complaints to bim. Defendant went drintdng with other men and there was no doubt that the children had not sufficient food.—Defendant's wife was recalled and stated that her husband had been convicted in Scotland of neglect of children,and served 40 days' imprisaumentl —The Chief Constable said defendant had been before the court six times, principally for school attendance cases.—The magi-trates committed defendant to gaol for bree months with hard labour. APPLE STEALERS WELL PUNISHED.</p>
        <p>Two young forge labourers named Frank Miller and Aaron Smith were heavily fined at the Warrington Borough Police Court on Thursday for apple stealing. They pleaded guilty. The apples stolen were valued at three shillings.—From the statement of the Chief Con- stable it appears that the prisoners worked during the previous night at Messrs.Monks' forge. About 5 o'clock on Thursday morning, Mr. Frank Grimshaw, of Gate Wharf Farm. heard the dogs harking, and on looking through the window he saw the prisoners helping them- selves pretty freely to the apples in the orchard. His father and he went out and Mr Grimshaw secured Miller whose shirt front was filled with apples as were also his pockets. Frank Grimshaw went to look for a policeman and succeeded in finding P.C. Carter, btf: in the meantime P.C. Woods arrived on the scene and took Miller into custody. The other prisoner got away but WaS Tienttlally arrested by P.C. Carter. Whilst running away he threc some apple 3 t_9 Str, ground and others were found in his pockets. Mr Grim shaw informed the Chief Constable that depredations of this kind were almost incessant. The vaiu.. of the apples was small when comparel with the damage done to cultivated fields by persons in going to the orchard.— Mr. Frank Grimshaw gave evidence and said a pathway had been made through the wheat field.—The Chairman (Alderman Fairclough) said the magistrates were deter- mined to put down this kind of thing and the prisoners Would be fined 20s and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST A TRACIION ENGINE DRIVER.-THE</p>
        <p>MAYOR GIVES EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court, on Friday, before Mr Birchall (in the chair), and R •T Fairclough, James Stuthers, of Statham, was summoned for using a locomotive engine; which did not, so far as practicable consume its own smoke, in St. James'-street on July 3rd.—Evidence was given by the Mayor, who stated that on the date in question he was going home from business and was passing along St James-street. He naiticed the traction engine driven bv the defendant and the whole street from the old grave yard to the end of Edelsten's workshop was filled with black smoke and emus. He did not think he had ever seen a chimney in Warrington of that capacity emitting so much smoke. If the offence had not been an outrageous one he should not have asked the Chief Constable to take proceedings. —Tte Chief Constable said that under the Act the locomotives should be so constructed as to consume their own smoke. The maximum penalty for the offence was £5 —Defendant V 745 tined 103 and costs (21 0; 61 in all).</p>
        <p>, AN &amp;TRUSTING POINT. 4t:the Warrington Police Court, on Friday, before the Meyor and other magistrates, Sarah Collins, of Corona- tion Buildings,waa summoned by Kate Sauce for making we of obscene language in Coronation Buildings—Mr. Browne presectred and the defendant was represented by Mr. Steel—Mr. Browne state I that the complainant was standing at the door of her house when the defena dant made um of mest horrible laneuegc towards her— Mr. Steel took a technical objection to the summons and be thought that Mr. White (the maeietrates' clerk) would agree with him that the case could not proceed. The proceedings were taken under the Towrs Police Clauses Act and the offence must have been committed in a public street, whereas Coronation Buildings were the private property of the Gas Depart- ment of the Corporation.—Mr. Browne It is an open vard.—Mr. Steel; It is not an oven yard at all. It is private property and the Corporation have a right to prevent the public going in at all. The Act clearly de- fines thatrthe offence must take place in a street, and a street includes any road, square, court, alley, and thoroughfare, or puelic passage and means a place to which the public have a right of access.—The Mayor expressed the opinion that Coronation Buildings was a public place.—Mr. Steel read alt r which he I- al received from the Borough Surveyor stating that the approach from Coronation Buildings to Bank-street was private and there was no right of way for the public over the same—Mr. Browne still contended that it was a public place stating that p,ople had teen summoned for being drunk and die - orderly there. Mr. Steel : I shall ask for an adjourn- ment in order to produce evidence that it is private property—The Mayor is it lighted and watched ? The Chief Constable: It is watched by all means—The Mayor : Is it lighted by the Corporation. The Chief Constable made a reply in the affirmative—The Mayor: If the aistrict is lighted, watched, and repaired by the Corporation there is nothing in your contention Mr. Steel.—The defendant in atswer to Mr. Stef I said there was no lamp and the place was not paved.—The Chief Constable despatched an cfficer to visit the place and on his return Mr. Talbot informed the Bench that he found there was a lamp on the wall which lighted a portion of the yard. The yard wal only partly paved and there were two gates, one to admit vehicles and the ether to admit residents. He was also informed that the lamp was lit by a servant of the gas depart- ment, and not by the Corporation lamplighter.—Mr. Steel: My submis,ien is in cone, quence of inquiries I have made at the Town Halle—The cage was ultimately adjourned for a week in order to give Mr, Steel an opportunity of producing evidence. INHUMAN PARENTS.—A WRETCHED HOME. On Wednesday at the Warrington Police Court, be- fore Mr. Birched( and other magistrates,John McLoughlin and Mary McLoughlie, of Morris's Court, were charged on a warrant with having on August Ist and other dates neglected their three children, Mary Ellen, Margaret, and Agnes, in a manner likely to name them unnecessary suffering and injury to health.—Mr Browne who appeared for the N.5.P.0.C., stated that prisoners' child Margaret, aged three years, was taken to the Infirmary suffering from a cut on the head, which was caused by falling off a table. Dr. Thorpe noticed the awful condition of the child and reported the matter to Inspector Hackett.who,on Tuesday m, ening visited the u.e in company with P.C. Whittaker. He examined the children and found Mary was do' hod in a frock and one skirt, and was filthy dirty.—Margaret was verminrus, and was bitten with lice, and Agnes was poorly nourished, badly clothed, verminous and bitten about the head with lice. The woman had evidently just come out of a drunken orgie. Two of the children were taken to the Workhouse and Mrs.Fernside,the portrrss would describe their condition. Prisoners had been before the magistrates in times gone by and had been di9rherged in order that they might have an oppor- tunity of affecting an improvement, but in February last the woman was sent to gaol for a month. The case against the men was adjournei and ultimately with- drawn as he made some small attempt to reform. The Inspector had had to keep his constant attention on the bones'. The man worked at the forge and was in re- eeipt of fairly od wages,driek being entirely the cause 15T th 4 trzlect.—De. Ziorpe was called and said that on Monday the child Margaret was brought to the Infirm- ary suffering from a small en', on the head. She was not properly .clothed, having no shoes and stockines and had difficulty in breatt log. Another child of the prisoners was at present in the Infirmary suffer- ing from hip disease brought about by neglect and when the mother had ceme to tbe Infirmary to see him she had frequen•ly been under the influence of drink. A neigh- bour brought the child Margaret to the Infirmary as Mrs. McLoughlin was stated to be drunk. Inspector Hackett informed the magistrates that the case had been under his observation since February. At that time the male priecrer wP s allowed an cpportunity of amending and he did something towards improving the condition of the children whilst his .wife was in gaol. In consequence of information he received he visited the house nn Tuesday morning at 11 30 and the female prisoner had just got up. The house was in a filthy state and there was no food, fire or coal. Two of the children came downstairs in a shocking state. They wete badly clothed, filthy dirty and vermin- ous. One of them had her head bandaged. He asked the prisoner where the other children 'sere and she said she bad not any others living with her. As he was go. log cut of the house the girl Mary came in. The mother hid her in the coal hole and witness went back and ex- amined the girl. She was in a worse condition than the wheys her hair being matted with nits and alive with lice. The house was un healthy and not fit to live in. The baby twelve Months nlt was badly bitten with lice. The woman admitted that she and her husband were drunk in the house when the child Margaret fell off the table and cut her head. It was a rnoct wretched home.—P.O. Whit- taker corroborated.—Mrs Fernside spoke to examining the children when they were admitted into the woik- booms and deocribs4 their filthy condition.—EG:tarnett asid be appisbestled the ptioastes is oompsay</p>
      </div>
