      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>MINOR CASES. Warringtonj3;lough Court.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES. Warringtonj3;lough Court.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY—Before the Mayor (In the chair). Messrs. Birehall and R. T. Fairelough. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>AS9LULT. —Sarah Gleaye, 54, Scott-street, was fined ss. and costs for assaulting Margaret Drinkwater on the 28th of July. A TROUBLESOMIt NutGlinoun.—James Morriaon. 277. Knutsford-road, was summoned for making use of threats towards James Tonge, 279, Knutsfor Road, on the 2 st July. There was a cross summons against Tonge for tlaeatening Morrison.—Mr. Browne appeared on behalf of Tonge and Morrison was represented by Mr. Fowler— Ar. Browne said that Mr. Tongs was an inspector of signals and lived next door to Morrison, the gardens of the houses adjoining. On the date in question Mr. 'rouge was in his garden in company with a young man named Warburton when Morrison came out and used most horrible language in the hearing o: Constable Cooper and others?l He then commenced to accuse him of robbinA the Railway Company and also said that Mr. Tonge was a churehwa:den and went round with the plate at St. JalligeS Charch and robbed it whilst he was taking it round. He also asserted that the buildings in the yard were stolen property an t some belonged to him as he was a shareholder in the Railway Company. Ffe then threatened that if he came within a yard of Mr Tonge he would thrash him and Mr. Tonga was afraid that he wobld do him some bodily harm.—Evidence was given in support of this statement. the witnesses including P.C. Cooper[who said Morrison and his family were a complete nuisance to the neighbonrho )d.—For the defence, Mr. Fowler stated that the young man Warburton commenced the affair. It was further alleged that Tonge called Morrison a fool and said what he would do for him. He invited him to corns into the yard and Morrison did say that it he went into the yard he would Rive him a good hiding.—The bench dismissed the case against Tonga and bound Morrison over to keep the peace, himself in £lO and in one surety of £lO. He was also ordered to pay the costs and advocate's fee (E 2 is 6d in all.) SLTURDA.Y.—Before Messrs .C.handley (in the chair), Birchall</p>
        <p>and Hayward.</p>
        <p>NOT FIT TO LIVE WlT.—MiCh%el Cragoll of Dutton-street was charged on a warrant with assaulting his wife, Mary Cragon, on the 28th July.—Complainant stated that her husband came home in a state of intoxication and commenael to break all the farniture. lie then used abusive language towards her and struck her on the heal and about the body with his fist. Prisoner had given her so many black eyes as to injure her eyesight, and he was not fit to live with— Prisoner said he was quite innocent of the charge —The Chief Constable proved a number of previous convictions avaint the man, a id the magistrates bound him over to keep the peace for six months and ordered him to pay the costs. MoNDAY• —Before Messrs Monks (in the ch sir) and Birch ill. _ _</p>
        <p>CARRIED TO THE POLICE STATlON.—Stephen Pannell, a tanner, living in West-street, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-street. on Saturday night. P.C. Fish proved the case. He informed the bench that he was asked to eject the prisoner from a shop where lie had been asking for ice cream. Toe prisoner, who was drunk,lay down Inside the shop. With the assistance of Sergeant Barnett and P.C. Edwards the prisoner was got into the street, but he had to be ^arried to the Bridewell.—Fined Stephen Burns, an ironworker, charged by Sergeant Hvans with having been drunk and disorderly in Hose and Crown. street. on Saturday night Was similarly fined. FORGIVING WivEs.—John White, a general labourer, living at 70, Sharp-street, was charged on warrant with assaulting his wife.—The complainant stepped into the box but she appeared to be very reluctant to give evidence against her husband. Pressed by the Clerk she stated that the prisoner " only gave her a kick, but it did not hurt her."—The Clerk : You are not telling the magistrates the tali you told when you applied for the warrant.—Th, Chief Constable : I want to know at which end of the story these women are liars.— Complainant : I shall have to give up my home if my husleand is sent to gaol, and I have no one to keep me.— !dr. Talbot informed the maOstrates that the prisoner had been dealt with twice for assaulting his wife ; altogether he had been brought before the Court 20 times. These men took a 'vantage of the fact that if they were sent to prison the wives suffered.—Prisoner: Let me off this time and I will be a teetotaler all my life.—The Chief Constable : Yes, until you get outside the court. (Laughter.) The defend- ant was fined 20s the alternative being 14 days' imprison- ment. . .</p>
        <p>TUESDAY—Before the Mayor (In the chair), Alderman Fran- comb, Messrs. Birchall and Hayward. THE STONE THROWING NUISANCE. —Half a dozen boys, named Thomas Coleman, 24, 'Arwood-street, Thomas Wilkin- son, 21. Tanners-lane. George Williams, 18, Bowes-strect, Joseph Lythgoe, 20, Portland street. Peter Carroll, 2, Port- land-street and William Brown, 71. Dallam-lane, were summoned for throwing stonet in Dallam-lane on the 26th Juiv.—P.C. Clough stated that he wai on duty in plain clothes between Dallam-lane 13ridge and Hope Foundry, and saw the defendants come from an adjoining field and com- mence throwing macadam at the Foundry, railway waggons. and telegraph posts. It was very dangerous to persons who might be passing. Witness had been on plain elothes duty specially on account of curnplaints which had been received of gas lamps, and private property being damaged A great deal of glass had been broken at Hope Foundrv.—T tle Chief Constable said he had received a letter Trom the Town Clerk on behalf of the Gat Committee com- plaining of the damage done by stone throwing in different parts of the town.—A fine of be was imposed.—Harvey Phillips, 4, Worseley-street aud Harry Smith, 11, Allcard- street, were summoned for a similar offence.—P.C. Clough proved the case, and defendants who said they were only " bowling" with the stones, were fined ss. TROUBLESOME CONDUCT —Margaret Kelly, Hollow-yard, Was summoned for making use of obscene and profane</p>
