      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>LATE MR. WM. JEPSON.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. WM. JEPSON.</p>
        <p>Funeral at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>On Monday afternoon the lss solemn tribute of respect was paid to the memery of Mr. William Jepson. eine of the nest known figures in Runcorn. The deceased who had reached the advanced age of BO years, died at his residence in Wellington-street on Tnursdav at 10 p.m. For many years he was in the employ of Mr. Samuel Taylor, and was highly esteemed by the boat people of the town with whom he had intimate business relations. AI Baptist in religious belief ho was a member of the church at Hill Cliffemear Warrington for between 45 and 50 years, for the last 20 of which he was a deacon and a trustee. For a period of 20 years ha walked the miles from Runcorn to Hill Cliffe every Sunday in all weathers a remarkable proof of his attachment. For a number of years he wa.e superintendent of the Sunday school there. During the last few years however, finding age made thojeurney to Hill Cliffe inareasingly difficult he trans- ferred his allegiance to Latchford ani remained a mem- ber there till his death, although he did not entirely sever his connection with the first named. Of late years h-iwever, he has regularly attended S. John's Presby- terian Church where he was held in high esteem for his sterling Christian coaracter. Ills attachment to the Sabbath school was ietense. He regularly attended until the end being present in St. John's school on his 80th birthday.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jepsoa's life has been essentially conructed with the Church of Chris'o-ill on a few occaaions he has been a speaker at political meetings, his views being dis- tinctly radical. Those who k,tew him will remember him became of his goodness cimbined with the quaint ob- servations which were often very humorous. The funeral procession left the hous.3 at about 3 o'clock, toe first part of the service being lat I I in St. John's Presby- terian Courch. Before the hearse were the Rev J F Matthews, Latchford ; Rev T 8 Morris, Runcorn ; Rev J S Hughes, Hill Clifie, and many memthrs of S. John's, his Sunday school class twine strongly represented. There were also many well-known people present to show th-ir respect to the deceased. The mourners were : First coach, P W Harrop and T Outhwaite (son,-in-law), W J Harrop, J Harrop and F Outhwa t 3 (griothoua) ; second coach, S Driokwater, T Drinkwater (ate pscni), air A Riley (representing his late employers), Mr:Albinson (brother-in-law) ; 3rd coach, W Outhwaite„B Outhwaite (step-grandchildren), A Wnyte, T Dob:,on and Wilbraham.</p>
        <p>As the procession entered the church the Dead March in maul was played by the organist Mr. J. Richardson, after which Or Revs, T S Morris and J S Hughes read Suitible portions of Scripture,Thc., 10;', Mattewl who conducted the EPrvice In it brief addre.a paid a high tribute:to the personal character of the deceased, saying his life had been lived in the beauty of holiness, and around him as he moved ho carried a fragrance and beauty that were the essence of his goodness. He was gAod man and had gone to his reward_ The service was resumPd at the graveside. Rev. J. Matthews again clnduclog th . proceedings, It is worthy of ncte that about: 20 persons of bo'h sexes made the journey from Latchford and Hill Cliffs in spite of the inclement weatoer to pay toe last possible tribute to one who was by all held in the grates :i admiration and ei. teem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>RUNCORN FLAT vvRECKED NEAR LLANDUDNO. -- Three Men Rescued.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FLAT vvRECKED NEAR LLANDUDNO. -- Three Men Rescued.</p>
        <p>A gale of almost unparalleled severity pas2ed over the North Wales coast on Nionday,and the full fury of it was experienced at Llandudno. Rain continued to fall almost intermittently throughout the night, though the fury of the wind abated somewhat between three and four o'clock on Tues- day morning. J. Williams, second coxswain, and John flughes,a boatman,noticed a wreck in the bay and several man clinging to the rigging. They immediately put out in a small boat, not waiting for the lifeboat to be launched, and succeeded in effecting a rescuelt was found that there were three men in the rigging,and they were almost exhausted. They had charge of the coal flat Evelena, bound from Runcorn to Dinorwic. For several hours they had been at the mercy of the waves,and had drifted into Me bay, where the boat sank after having struck the rock, near the Little Orme. One of the men was rescued in a semi-conscious condition. They were all taken to Wil- liam's restaurant, and after resuscitation reported their loss to Mr. Dunphy, secretary to the Shipwrecked Fishermen and Mariners' Society. The wreek is being visited by many poople, the rigging being visible above the water —the names of the crew are :—Alfreel Wil- liamson (captain), Samuel Bowyer and Henry Sutton (mates), and the owner is Mr. Kitcrese, Widnes, where the men reside. The storm was the most severe in their long experience, the lightning being most terrifying. They left New Ferry, near Liverpool, at eight o'olock on Monday morning. Thew lost their small boat at sea; the main gaff was broken, and the mizrmsail torn in twain. The vessel was helpless from midnight to 5 a.m. on Tuesday, when the crew were rescued. She sprang a leak near Puffin Sound, and the crew had been four hours in the rigging expecting momentarily to be washed off and drowned. An effort is being made to recover the cargo of coal when the tide is out, and it is hoped to be able to rec3ver and repair the vessel.</p>
        <p>Burtonwood Dahlia Queen Festival Postponed. The annual Dahlia Queen Febtival at Burtonwood which should have been held on Monday, was to the great disappointment of the large number of juveniles and others, postponed owing to the un- popular weather that prevailed throughout the day. London and North-Western Railway Extensions at Warrington. _ .</p>
        <p>The report of the directors— of the London and North-Western Railway, will be submitted to the proprietors at the half-yearly general meeting on the 14th August, 1900. It is recommended that dividends at the following rates be declared: 4 per cent per annum on guaranteed stock, £302008; 4 per cent per annum on preference stock, £461,612 ; 6 per cent per annum on consolidated stock, £1,274,525(as against 61- per cent in the correspond- ing period) ; amounting to £2,038,145; leaving a balance of £72,328 to be carried forward,as against £94,105 in the corresponding half-year. The amount received for passengers, merchandise, minerals, was £6,409,641. The increase in revenue receipts this halt-year is £166,653 and the increase in expenditure is £268,912 fis com- pared with the corresponding half-year of 1899. rhe engineer's report, dated the 6th August, 1900, states that at li;uston the works in connection with the improvement of the approach lines into the Station are complete and in use. At Crewe Station the extension works have made good progress, and 34 miles of additional lines and sidings are occupied for traffic purposes. At Warrington the foundations have been put in for the new viaduct over the river Mersey, forming part of the intended widening of the Grand Junction Railway. At Preston Station (jointly with the Lancashire and Yorkshire Com- pany) the new viaduct over the river Ribble is complete, and a portion of the new arrangements to facilitate the working of through traffic has been brought into use.</p>
        <p>ENGLISH PATENTS.—The 101 lowing pitints amonggt others were applied for, and publish(d in the "Official Journal «1 Patents," during the week ending July nth, 1900.—Nos. 13.223, A Hill, Moro le Bridge, for " An improvement in golf clubs," 23rd July, 13,224 ; W Sutton, Stockport, for" An improved machine for stretchinE the felt in the formation of hat brims." 13295, r Booth and J E Marcroft, Rochdale, for " An improved cooler for butter and other substances," 24:h July. 13297, J T Hailwood, Rochdale, for" Improved method of moulding and loc,easing the efficienny of nnn-conductine covering's to pipea, tunes, boilers. 24th July. July. 13392, 13 Higgins for "improvement in photographic printing-out frames," 25% July. 13432, Meldrum Bros., Limited, and F Clayton for "improve- ment in and connected with mechanical furntss stokers, Ste.," 26th July. 13493, J Hargreaves Widnes, for" improvements in treating and obtain;ne metallic chlorides arid oxides," 27th July, 1900. 13574, F C Rogers, Stockport, for "an Improved hair-pin," 28th July.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>Horse and Jockey Bowling Club, Warrington</p>
        <p>_ A picnic to Gateacre, in cminection with the above club, took place on Saturday, when 40 members were conveyed thither in brakes, kindly supplied by the land- lord of the hotel, Mr, Rushworth. On arriving at Gate- acre, a side game was played, one side being captained by the Pres:dent of the club. Councilbr Wilkinson, and the other side by Mr. Marshall. There was some good bowling and the , ame created much interest. The result was an easy win for the President's side, who succeeded in compiling 185, against 132. Full scores appended : MR. MARSHALL'S SIDE. MR WILKINSON'S SIDE. Marshall (capt.) Hackett T Broadhurst 1 D Daniels T Littlefair 2 B Radcliffe ll W Mee</p>
