        <p>THE CASTNER-KELLNER ALKALI COMPANY, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>Amalgamation of Aluminium Company.</p>
        <p>An extraordinary general meeting of the members of the above-named company was held on Wednesday, at the Cannon-street Hotel, London, for the purpose of con. sidering a pr-vosal for amalgamation with the Aluminium Company, Limited. Mr. W. Mather, M.P., presided, and stated that since the completion of the Castner-Kellner Company's works at Weston Point and the successful transaction of the induitry th,y had established, the directors had from time to time considered the importance for the future development of their uodee.aking of adding to it other branches of chemical manufacture, and had endeavoured to discover what might profitably be joined to the manufacture of caustic soda and bleaching powder, which were their present products. The ques- tion of joining with the Aluminium Company, of Old- bury, near Birmingham, a rosP, the development of that company rendering necessary the acquisition of cheap electric power, and the directors of this company were satisfied that it would be a desirable thing for both com- panies if they could be amalgamated. There was a neutral band between the two companies, for the Aluminium Company were the vendors to this company of (Ale patents un iflt which they were carrying on their operetions, and the former company took a considerable quantity of the latter's caustic soda for the manufacture of the sodium which was their special pro- duct ; that both concerns could be more econo- mically carried on at Weston Point. It was proposed that the amalgamation should! be eff.-cted by the purchase of the assets and undertakings of the Aluminium Company other than the 66,000 shares they had in the Coitner-Kellner Company. The capital of the Aluminium Company was £65,000 ordinary capital, 'and tiPy had a debenture debt of £90,000, bearing in- terest at 4 per cent. The agreement to be submitted for approval provided that the consideration for the purchase should be the allotment to the Aluminium Company of 130 000 shares of £1 each fully paid, and the purchasing company should take over the debenture debt of £90,000 toy giving their debenture holders a similar amount of debenture stock to rank pan i passu with the £150,000 debenture stock already held by the Castner-Kellner Company, bearing interest at the rate of q per cent per annum. According to the last balance-sheet of the Aluminium Company, th-ir aqiete stood at £309,683, but from this must be deducted £66,000, the par value of 66,000 of the shares of the Ctstner-K diner C impany. which they could not purchase, making the ass-ts £243,683. The profits of the Aluminium Company had been for the year ending June 30th, 1898, • £10,473 ; far 1899, £23,288 ; and for the nine months ending March 31st last, £16,376. Taking the profits of the past twelve months at the rate shown in the balance-sheet for nine months at about £24,000, and deducting from that the I dividends they had received from the gastner-Kellner Company's shares, welch would not be received by the latter company, the profits for the twelve months would I be about £20,000. Assumiag that the Ctstner- Kellner Company continued to pay dividend at the rate ot 8 per cent per annum, there would be an extra chrrge put upon their profits of 4i per cent per annum on £90,000, the addi- tional amount of debenture steck to be allotted to the Aluminium Company, £4,050 ; 8 per cent. on £130,000 share capital to be allotted to them. £lo,4oo—total, £14,450. Against this. the Castner-Kellner Company 'hone i to receive the profits already mentioned of £20,000. Referring, in conclusion, to the recent litiga- tion concerniag the alleged infringement by the Rhoden Company of one of this company's patentsithe Chairman said the judgment of the House of Lords, given against them on a strictly technical point, would make no difference in their postion, and they would continue to manufacture as heretofore.</p>
        <p>On the motion of the Chairman, s-conded by Mr. F Hardcastle, re?olution was adopted ratifying the pro- visional agreement for am Agamation ; and a further r'qohrtion, moved by the Chairman, and seconded by Mr. Smith, authoriaPd the directors to borrow an addi- tinnal sum of £120,000, making' the total amount which the bard might r.)w £270,000. It was also resolved in accordanc,3 with a propoqal, supported amongst others by Mr. Burton, Mr. Justice, and Sir Henry Rosoce, tha, the capital of the company be increased from £300,000 to £450,000, divided into shares of £1 each ; that c,rtain alterations la- made in the articles of a.sociation ; and that the direetors should be entitled for their remuneration to £2,000 per annum, unless otherwise determined by the company in general meet, ing.</p>
        <p>'the Chairman pointed out that the last proposal was prospective, being dependent on the amalgamation and toe approval of the joint etareholders at a future meet- ing. Ho added that in his opinion there would probably be a considerable increase of profits and greater economy in working, to the benefit of the shareholders In both compauies, if the amalgamation vrre carried out.</p>
        <p>Music in the Park.</p>
        <p>Mr T J Down's Military Band, will play the following selection of music, (weather permitting), on Thursday evening, commencing at 7 o'clock. March. "The Hunter." Faust; overture, "Si yards Roi," Adam; valse "Casino Tanze," Gung'l ; nautical selection," A life on the Ocean," Binding; polonaise, " Masken." Faust; priests' war march, from "Athalie," Mendelsshon ; galop, " Lustige Faust; "God save the Queen." Mr T J Down will be the conductor.</p>
