      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <p>THE STRANGE AFFAIR NEAR BANK QUAY BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Prisoner Acquitted.</p>
        <p>At the Liverp )ol ASSiVII as Thursday before Mr. Justice Channell, Albert Clarke (24), labourer, was in- dicted for having, at Wrarington, on May 4, felmoiously obbeJ Henry Charles Hope. of the sum of 84 91 in money, a bunch of keys, a purse, and a pocketknife, using personal violence.</p>
        <p>Mr. Foard coaducted th 3 pio3ecution, and Mr. Rigby Swift defended.</p>
        <p>Mr. Foard said that the prosecutor was a musician, and was going home about half past eleven o'clock at night, and in passing along Sankey-gtreet he saw the prisoner, who had no cap on. Some conversation passed between them, and tney walked along the street together. When they came opposite to some steps the prisoner seized the prosecutor by the wrist, pulled him down with great force, knelt upon him, and made a very disgusting observation, afterwards forcibly taking the money and the articles namel in the charge from him. Soon after this the prosecutor went to the police station and made a charge in consequence of which the police went to the prisoner's house. Prisoner said, when he saw the police-sergeant. "I know what you have come for," at the sa=e time producing the sum of 4s in money, a knife, keys and other articles taken from the prosecutor. When charged, the prisonor made an infamous counter-charge against the proaecutor adding that the latter gave him the articles. Counsel pointed out the improbability of a bunch of keys being given for this purpose. The prosecutor supported counsel's statement. Cross-examieed by Mr. Rigby swift, the prosecutor said that ho had been a member of the police force of Liverpool twenty years ago, but had been dismissed owing to his being at that time addicted to drink. He had since cured himself of that habit. He denied emphatically that he was a drunkard. A police-sergeant at Warrington deposed that when the prosecutor went to the police station his clothes were dirty, his hat was dinged and broken, his face was bleeding slightly, and he appeared to be 'very much exhausted. He was sober.</p>
        <p>Another policeman said that when the proaecutor earrived at the station he noticed signs of drink in him. _ _</p>
        <p>For the defence, Mr Swift called the prisoner. who in evidence repeated the charge be had made against the prosecutor. The jury retired to consider their verdict and on their return the foreman announced that they had agreed to a verdict of "Not guilty." The prisoner was accordingly discharged.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT OF THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Week Ending Saturday, July 28th, 1900. During the week the following births and deaths were registered : Births. Deaths.</p>
        <p>Warrington Registration District 48</p>
        <p>The excess of births ever deaths was 27. The Birth Rate was equal to 384 per 1,000. The death•rate from all causes was equal to 16•8 per 1,000, against 156 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 13.8 the corresponding period of last year. The death- rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 2.4 per 1,000. against 2.4 the average rate of the previous four weeks, and against 2 4 the corresponding period of 1899. The ages of mortality were- -0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 85 hp. Total. 7 3 1 1 8 1 21 The deaths included :-1 from measles 2 from diarr- hces, 1 from phthisis, 4 from lung disease, 3 from heart disease, 1 from injuries, 9 from all other causes. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—lcarlet fever, two cases ; enteric fever, one case, erysipelas, one case. Total number of patients in hospital week ending Saturday. 28th July. 8. MprreonoLoov—The mean temperature wu 88 Fah., and therefore 2 D. lower than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 81F., was recorded on the 24tu inst, and the minimum temperature 45 F.. on the 27th inst. The Rainfall nrasur3d .27 inch. The general direc- tion was S.W.</p>
        <p>JOHN H. GOANALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>A Newsagent's Failure.</p>
        <p>A receiving order has been made in the Warring- ton County Court, on the debtor's own petition, against Joseph William Cavanagh, of 52, Horse- market-st., Warrington, newsagent and stationer. Discouraging Prospects for Cheshire Farmers.</p>
