      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Big Blaze at Daresbury.</head>
        <p>A Big Blaze at Daresbury.</p>
        <p>At 3.20 p.m. on Saturday information was re- ceived by telegram at the Warrington Fire Station that a fire had broken out at the farm of Mr. Bill- ington, Daresburv. The deputy superintendent, with a full complement of firemen, with the steam engine" Major" at once turned out. Upon their arrival they found the ViTilderspool Fire Brigade with their steam fire engine had just arrived. The Warrington brigade were informed that their ser- vices would not be required. The fire had origin- ated in a Dutchbarn, eight bays long, and adjoin- ing the farm building. Luckily a strong wind was blowing from the buildings and a large quantity of willing aesistantg prevented the fire spreading by throwing buckets ef water on the flames. The barn contained two stacks of hay, three half bags of straw, about twelve tons of pressed straw, and a large quantity of farm implements all of which were totally destroyed. The loss, however, is covered by insurance. Owing to the scarcity of water the Wilderspool Brigade were placed at a great distance from the fire and had to run out such long lines of hose that some time elapsed be- fore water could he thrown upon the flames. The cause of the fire is unknown. The amount of dam- age has not vet been ascertained but it is consider- able. The Warrington Brigade after resting their horses returned home.</p>
