      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPH</p>
        <p>"Miss AUTUMN told me her age was twenty., lour." "I always said that girl wasn't up to late. _</p>
        <p>AN egg just on the point of being hatched can't kelp being chicken-hearted. WOULD a man who was colour-blind know blind. man's buff. A CLOCK should never be placed at the head of the stairs, because it is so apt to run down. THE Most musical conip.ny in England is thei P. a.n' O.</p>
        <p>.-.,, , . i :..i '4 1,</p>
        <p>- . THE man who frequently takes the chair is the broker's man.</p>
        <p>EvEN though the manufacturing of sausages may seem to be a profitable business, the butcher does not always make both ends meet. "ISN'T it strange? Minnie despises Mr. Wil- kins, while her mother thinks he is the pleasantest parson in the world." "That's easily accounted for. The first time he met them together he took them for sisters."</p>
        <p>A GREAT many girls say " No " at first; but like the photogaphers, they know how to retouch their negatives.</p>
        <p>SIRS. CRIMSONBEAK-" If you say you never made a mistake in your life you state what is not a fact." Mr. Crimsonbeak : "Well, you needn't throw my marrying you in my face so much." "HE says he has been out with a theatrical .compAuy that had many unusual and sensational experiences." Yes; I am told that all the mem- bers received their salaries regularly." • TREVIiLLYN : "Why do you Irishmen always be singing 48t Patrick's Day in the Morning 'V' Corrigan : "Because, av we have good luck, ut's too Cull for utterance we arc in the evenin', be jabbers 1" CHOLLIE : " Welly, Miss Gayleigh, if you we- fuse me, I'll cut nie throat, dontcherknow." Miss Gayleigh " Well, if you don't do it your- self pretty quick, your collar will save you the trouble."</p>
        <p>"I SUPPOSE you acquired a good deal of useful informatioo while you were on a farm last sum- mer?" " Yes ; I noticed that while black hens lay white eggs it never happens that white hens lay black eggs." MoTHER : "What Eire you examining so closely, Johnnie?" Boy (at the window): "A hair, mamma. Teacher said in Sunday-school that the hairs of our head are all numbered, and I'm look- ing for the number on this one." MOTHER, : "How's this, Tommy? You've been fighting Billy once again, when I forbade it." Tommy: "Didn't yer tell me to return good for evil, Ma? Well, he gave me a bad lickin' yester- day an' I gave him a good one to-day." VlsiToat: "What would you like to be when you grow up?" Johnnie: "I think I'd like to be a soldier." "But then you might get killed." " Who would kill me?" "The enemy, of course." "Then I believe I'd rather be the enemy."</p>
        <p>MRE. DARLEY (newspaper in hand) : "One of the leading Czechs rejoices in the name af Czwrez:.k.' Mr. Darley : "I don't believe it." "But here is is name in the paper " "Oh, that may be his naine all right, but I can't believe that he rejoices it it." THEY MIGHT WANT IT.—Miss Upton: "Ma, Miss Flighty and Mr. Saphead are to be married to-day. Shall I take some rice to throw after them?" Practical Mother: "No, my dear. Wait until they have run through what little money they have, and then give it to them." HOOT, TOOT !—We know old Dr. Johnson's description of oats as food for horses in England and for men in Scotland. Something similar was Raid the othr day by a would-be caustic wit who described the wad Ii.00t" as ,sti exclamation used by Scotchmen and owls. EXPL(WN:E,— Mistress : Get dinner to-day 9n the gasoline stove, Bridget.—Bridget : Plaze, roam, I did thry, but th' stove wint out.—Mistress: Try then.—Bridget: Yis, mum, but it's not come back y it. It wint out t'rough the roof. EVIDENCE OF INSANITY.—The Farmer: "From what I can make out, them folks that collect postage stamps must be crazy." His Wife: " Why ? " The Farmer: "Why, they'll give more for stamps that has been used than for stamps that hasn't !" "To GIVE THE COLD SHOULDER."—This saying originaled in a practice once common in France, and during the Norman days in England also. When a guest had outstayed his welcome, instead of the haunch of mutton or venison usually served at dinner, a cold shoulder of mutton was placed before him as a hint that he had better go. NOT VERY REMARKABLE.-Smith : "I was reading in the paper this morning about a Texas man who was struck by lightning while he was swearing. Remarkable occurrence wasn't it 7" Brown; "Oh, I don't know. If lightning hap- pened to strike a Texas man when he wasn't swearing, it would be much more remarkable." SELF-BETRAYED.—" I heard one of those little Dutch bands this morning," Ashbury Peppers began. "Are you sure you do not mean a Ger- man band?" asked the sarcastic and critical boarder: "I think it was Dutch," replied Asbury Peppers. "I could distinctly hear the big trombone shouting, Oom Paul! Oom Paul, Oom 1' " MATTER OF TASTE.—Saici a member of a house- hold economic association to a lady of society: "The city water is so full of animalculae, I wonder you dare to drink it as ic is. We always boil ours." "Dear me!" returned Mrs. Mundane • "What a distressing thought ! I'd rather be an aquarium than a graveyard; so I drink mine raw.</p>
        <p>A Wm: COURSE. —The lecturer on health had finished his discourse, and invited his auditors to ask any questions they chose concerning points that might seem to need clearing up, when a lean, skinny man rose up and asked: "Professor, what do you do when you can't sleep at night?" "I usually stay awake," replied the lecturer, "although, of course, everybody should feel at liberty to do oteerwise if he chooses. Are there. any other questions?" A SITUATION WAZi TED. —Pat, intent on emi% grating, as ho was out of work, stops before a newsvendor's chop, reads a placard with "Situa- tion in Egypt" upon it." "Sure, I've come about that situation you're advertising." "What situa- tion do you wean ?" Pat (pointing to the poster): "It's the wonn in Agypt l'm after." " Pooh ! That's on the state of aff,lirs " " Sorra a ha'porth I care whose estate it's on. Bedad, 11l take it!"</p>
        <p>AFTER. he had waited in the cold for 10 minutes the door was opened on the chain and a woman's face appeared at the aperture. "Good morning, madam," began the vendor, in his suavest tones, "I have here a little article of universal utility. It is called the Marvellous Mice Exterminator. and the price is--" " No use," interrupted the woman, firmly. "We have no marvellous mice in this house—only t.)e ordinary kind." Then the door was shut and the vendor was once more alone.</p>
        <p>REFLECTIONS OF A BACHELOR.—When a girl blushes she-- By Jove! There's that kid cry- lug again! Every married woman thinks-- By khe way, Maria, did you sew on those buttons? NVhen you see a widow-- Johnny, put away tat drum, this minute 1 When a woman tells her hp •:band-- George, if you don't keep quiet 5 1.1 have to go to bed. Dress is to a woman w:-.t-- Serves you right, Willy. It's a won- der you didn't break your neck. Bright eyes and rosy cheeks-- Great Scott! A man might as well try to write in a boiler factory ! AN ENDLESS TRAIN.—" Yes," said the talkative travediug man, " -lonia of those Southern roads are frightfully crocked. I remember once starting out to make a little trip of thirty miles Oi• so on the P.D.S. road. I started early in the morning. The hours went by; noon came. At the middle of the afternoon we were still going. The road seemed more crooked than usual. "We noticed that there seemed tc oe something strange in the mono- tony of the scenery along the route. Our train was one of those "accommodation " freight trains, with about forty cars. Well, sir, we didn't know what was up. "Finally it dawned upon some of the more observant of us that we were continually pasaing and repassing the same places. And that was just what was the matter." "Why, how could that be?" "Well, sir, some of us went to the rear of the train and found that the front of the engine had somehow or other, in some of our curves, got coupled into the rear of our last car. The engine man was asleep, and we had been going in a circle all day."</p>
