      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>EXAMINER PRI; NY, AUGUST 10, 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER PRI; NY, AUGUST 10, 1900.</p>
        <p>one day's food, so I don't think I do amiss.—Hospital I Ship, Orcana, Durban, July 12th. I now take the oppor- tunity of writing, after swallowing half a chicken and a basin of rice. We are constantly visited by tie ladies cf Durban. They are very kind. They always thing something—handkorchiels, fruit, cake;, books, a hirts, &amp;c. We are lying in harbour. They say sht, sails for England on the 17th of this month and arrives there on August 14th. She is a splendid ship, fitted up with beds electric light and kind nurses. We get beautiful bread and butter and tPa, a good custard in the afternoon and 'a glass of port wine. lam glad to get out of this- It has been a trying time for us.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>MILITARY NOTES.</head>
        <p>MILITARY NOTES.</p>
        <p>Second Lieutenant Garrett of the 3rd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment with headquarters at Warrington, has, been given a commission in the regular army and has become Second Lieutenant in the Ist Battalion South Lancashire Regiment in South Africa. Second Lieutenant Garrett who was appointed to the Militia Battalion above mentioned last January was previously a Cheshire Volunteer officer. He was appointed Second Lieutenant in the 2nd Volunteer Battalion Cheshire Regiment in Niay 1895 and promoted Lieutenant in August. 1896, serving with the corps until he joined the 3rd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment. Second-Lieutenants Witherington and Wheeler of the sth battalion Liverpool Regiment with head- quarters at Warrington have been promoted to the rank of lieutenant in the corps. The first named officer joined the battalion in July of last year while Lieutenant Wheeler • entered the service last December.</p>
        <p>Second-Lieutenant Ackland of the 6th battalion Liverpool Regiment with headquarters at Warring- ton has been given a commission in the regular service joining the 3rd battalion Liverpool Regi- ment-. Second-Lieutenant Docker 3rd battalion Royal Lancaster Regiment and Second-Lieutenant Lynn of the 4th battalion of the same corps have also been appointed second-lieutenants in the let battalion Liverpool Regiment. A Volunteer officer has been appointed to a com- mission as second Lieut. in the South tancashire Regiment. This is Lieut. Dickson of the 2nd (Angus) Volunteer battalion Royal Highlanders the old 2nd Forfarshire Rifles who was appointed ti his first commission in N,)vember 1892, and was pro- moted Lieut. in November 1894: He has passed in tactics and also holds a P.S. certificate.</p>
        <p>Corporal Simpson of the South Africa Light Horse has been given a commission as second Lieut. in the Ist battalion Liverpool Regiment, as a reward for his services in the Boer War. Lieutenant Lloyd of the 4th Battalion Yorkshire Regiment, has been promoted Captain in the 3rd Battalion Cheshire Regiment. He joined the Militia last December.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant Lake has been promoted Captain in the 4th Volunteer Battalion Cheshire Regiment. He joined that corps in May 1896. Captain Haines, Cheshire Regiment, has been appointed governor of Gosport Military Prison. Commenting upon the fact that the Ist Volunteer Battalion South Lancashire Regiment have received permission to raise a company of mounted infantry, a writer in a Manchester contemporary says : '"seeing that the battalion has also received official sanction for two other new companies, Colonel Ridgway and his staff have certainly got plenty of of work before them. But they always have done things weli at Warrington, ever since the good old days of tba Bluebacks,' and 1 have little doubt but that they will manage this also."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>SCENE IN THE HOUSE OF COMMONS. Attack on Mr. Chamberlain.</head>
        <p>SCENE IN THE HOUSE OF COMMONS. Attack on Mr. Chamberlain.</p>
        <p>In the course of debate on the Transvaal corre3pond- once in the House of Commons on Wednesday evening, Mr. Lloyd George (Carnarvon, L.) complained that Mr. CbamberlAin had deliberately kept back the alleged treasonable letters from the House, knowing that if they bad been produced many pertinent queitions woul.: have been asked. N.) cther Member of the House would have acted in such a way. (Radical cheers.) Then the proceedings before the Army Contracts Committee ehowed that in the matter of tenders the firm of Messrs, Kynoch had been favoured Whatever families and parts of the country had suffered from the war, there was one family and one part of the country which had profited. (Opposition cheers, and universal cries of "Order.") lithe Colonial Secretary had been in the position ot critic, they kciew ho ..g he would have acted in tuch a case. (Opposition cheers and laughter.) Mr. Chamberlain : As the hon. member says, this is a question of innuendoes. We perfectly understand that. (Ministerial cheers). And perhaps that will justify me in asking the indulgence of the [louse to say a few words. The deport of the Committee I have not seen, nor have I seen the rk ports of the eximination while it was going on, and, I therefore, cannot answer anything that the bon. gentleman has said with reference to what went on before that Committee e• but the innuendo means, of coarse, that the Colonial Secretary i in sool2 way or another connected with till matter.</p>
        <p>An Hon. Member: Your brother. Mr. Chamberlain : NOW, I Wi3b to say I have. no ' interest, direct or indirect. Mr. Lloyd-George: I never said so. (Ministerial cries of "Order.") (The hon. mPmber remaining standing.) The Speaker : Order, Order. Unless the right hon. gentleman choeses to give way, he is in possession of ' the House, and the hon. member must resume his seat. Mr. Lloyd-George The right hon. gentlem In is speaking a second time by the indulgence of the House," and Ido not object to that, nor do the hon. members near me, but wnen a misrepresentation of what I said is made, I think I am entitled to protest. I never even suvgested— The Speaker: That is not a paint of order. If any member is in possession of the Hou,e he is not bound to give way because some other member wiahed to correct a misrepresentation. There is no point of order. Mr, Lloyd George : Then I must ask leave later on to speak. Mr. Chamberlain : I have no interest, direct or indirect. in Kynochs, or in any ottler firm making ammunition or war !material. I have never interfered I directly or indirectly with the distribution of these con- tracts, and I have never sp-ken fo•sr.y rile a': the War Office about them, and I had no more to do with them than the hon. mPmber hims3lf. (MihisteriA cheers.) The hen gentleman interrupted me just now, and said what, of ceurse,is public property,that my brother is chlirman of Kynochs. Mr; Mac Neill : Your family. Mr. Chamberlain: I have never diaaugsed she matter with my brother- I have no more whatever to Cl) with his private concerns than he has to di with my public concerns, and it is a gross abu e—(loud Ministerial cheers and Opposition cries of " Order ")—to attack a public man through his relativs, for whom he is not re- sponsible. (Loud Ministerial cheers.) . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Lire asked leave to withdraw his amendment. The amendment was withdrawn,and the third reading of the Appropriation Bill agreed to. The Speaker left the chair, and the sitting was sus- pended at 1.35. At two o'clock the Bitting was resumed, and five minutes lat,..r the Back Rod appeared with a summons to the Speaker and the Members of this House to attend in the House of Peers to hear the Royal Assent riven to certain measures, and to hear the reading of the Queen's Speech. The Close of the Session.</p>
        <p>On returning from the other House, the Speaker, tak- irw the seat of the Clerk at the table, read, in accord- ance with the trsu II practice, a ceps:, of the Spr rch from the Qienn. The Ministers and Members then filed past; the Speaker, shaking hands and saving' Gooller ," and at half-past two the Speaker had disappeared, leaving the Chamber in the possession of the attendants and a few sightseers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>Considering it was a holiday Sanda.y. there was .1 fair attendance at the service in connecti.,,a with the above society on Sunday las% Mr. Da wnea presided and gave a brief address on " Friendship." The Rev. W R Parry of Peasley Cresi, who waA the snooker, delivered an interestin, address on the influence of one life upon anothor, b t-ing his rem irk on the Mqnderlu, incident related in II Kinus, chap. 13, 20-21. Mr Heolev le 1 the devotional part of the service. Mis 3 Elcerslev sang with gor,rl effect, " The Promised Land," and " I know that my Releemer liveth."</p>
        <p>1.0.G.T. Temperance Mission Lodge. The usual wsekiv meeting of toe above lodge was held in the Brick-street schoolroom on Wednes- day. Bro J W Hotwrts, C.T., presided over a fair attendance of members and visitors from the " On- ward" Lodge, Warrington. Sister Sheath asked the lodge to apply for a Juvenile Temperance Charter. It was deci.led to do so. Bros J Sheath, Foberts, and O'B:ien' and Sisters Nurse and Sheath were appointed as Juvenile Executive Committee. Bro Wright, san ' Kitty Wells"; Sister IS L Sheath re- cited "How Horatius kept the bridge" ; and sister Davies sang "Queen of the Earth." Bro O'Brien moved sn Bro J Sheath seconded a vote of thanks to the visitor=. The resolution was carried and the edge was closed. PUBLIC BRNBFIT UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BES I' BOOTi IN LANCIASEiIitH.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>POLICE CASES.</p>
        <p>At the Newton Police ti-nirt, on Tuesday morning before Mr 17 J ilkspie, IV Leigh was charged with being drank and disol.derly ia EArle3town on Mon- day and was fined 53 and co-ti. Albert Crook,lVigan, and Michael Uollin wire e.Ach charged with being drank and disorderly and were fined 2s 61 and costs each.</p>
        <p>VIADUCT INSTI TUTE TECHNIC A.L INSTRUCTION CLASSES.</p>
        <p>The following re,ults of examinations in the Sub- jects named, in cmin,ction with the above classes have cmiaa to hand :—Applied Mechanics ;—A R Hill, Ist E. W Antrobu4, Joseph Bate, Joseph Crouchley, H Williamson, 2nd E, Buildng Clnstruc- tion :—G Rezadall, 2nd A, W Duilgeou, and P Mullen, 2nd E. this completes the returns for the 1900 examinations.</p>
        <p>BANK HOLIDAY AT EARLESTO WN AND NEWTON</p>
        <p>A more miserable day could not nave been imajned, than that Nviic.i prevailed on Monday. Fortunately. the day was not observed by the various works, as a holiday with the sole exception of Nlessrs McUorquodale and i;ompany's Works, at Newton, and thus the disappointment because of the weather, was not se keenly felt. All the trades- men ciosed their shops, an but for this, there was no indication whatever, that the great August bank holiday was in existence. A goodly number left the town for Liverpool and Manchester, and other popular places, but on their return home their appearance did not indicate . that they had had a very enjoyable day. GOOD TWMPLARS.</p>
        <p>M3pe of Earlestown Lodge meeting was held in Wargrave Hall, on Monday, Bro Woods, L.D., pre- siding, one being restored to membership. Bro Clarke gave the report of the programme commit- tee. On motion the report was adopted and it was decided to invite the Wesley Hall Lodge and to accept the invitation . to visit the Alpha Lodge, Lei2h. Bro Rev J A Lee, D.0.T., wrote accepting the invitation to preach the anniversary sertuon3, in the morning in the Wesh3yan Chapel ; the after- noon and evening in the Co-operative hail ; invita- tion to attend the re-opening of the Samaritan Lodge, Haydock. The remainder of the evening was spent in various games. ST. JOHN's CHURCH SUNDAY SCHOOL,</p>
        <p>The children connec,ed with the above school, and the Mission School had their annual treat and field day on Saturday. Fortunately the day was fine, and the chiluren were consequently enabled to enjoy themselves in the open air. They assembled at the church at one o'clock, where a short service was held. A procession was afterwards formed and headed by the Viaduct Works band, and the Liverpool Farm School band, they proceeded through the principal streets of the town,something like 1,100 scholars forming the procession. After parading the town Oe children proceeded to the old Newton race course, where in the paddock. and grandstand enclosure they eajoyed themselves in various ways until 5.30_ at which time tea was served. After tea each scholar was presented with a bag of nuts and sweets, and the remainder of the evening was very pleasantly spent. The Viaduct band enlivened the proceedings with selections of music and dancing was indulged in by the elder scholars and teachers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS PROBATE CASE</p>
        <p>Oa Wednesday alterncon, in the Probate Division of the High Courts of Justice, the action Massey v. Ozden and others came on before Mr Justice Gor-ll Barer&amp; The plaintiff was Mrs Ellen Massey, of St Helens, and the defendants Ann Ogden, John Sherlock, and Henry Massey. The action ha l reference to the testamentary dispositions of the late Mrs Elizabeth Massey. Counsel for the plaintiff said that it was at first proposed to set up a will of February, 1890, but the parties to the suit had come to terms, and probate would be asked of an earlier will made in the year 1886.—Mr Gerald Massey, solicitor, S. Helena, d, posed to having witues,ed the execution of the Will of 1886 by the testatrix ; upon which His Lo:dship granted probate of the document in question. HEALTH OF ST HELENS.</p>
        <p>The medical officer of St. Helens, (Dr F Drew Harris) reportel to a meeting of the Health Committee on Wed- nesday a serious increase in the death-rate. The health statistics for the past fortnight showed that while the hirt h-rate for the borough had increaced from 31.6 per 1,000 to 41.9 compared with a rate of 33 6 for the corres- noodinq period last year. the death rateooad gone up from 234 to 26.1 the rate for the preceding fortnight having been 214. Of the deaths, ei2ht were due to tubercular diqease, twenty to diarrbcea (the same num- ber as in the corresponding period last year), three to whooping-cough, and two t measles. No fewer than twenty-three cases of scarlet fever load been reported during the fortnight, against fourteen for the previous fortnight and seven for the corresponding period last year. ROBBING A CHURCH Box.</p>
        <p>Thq St. Helens magistrates had before them on Mon- day morning a juvenile of twelve named William Tucker of 26, Hanover-street. He was charged with robbing a collection box at the Sacred Heart Church of 24, the monies of the Rector. On Sunday afternoon the boy wa seen in the church, an young man named Wiliam Ripley caught him with his hands in a collection 10-dr, which had been broken open. On being seized the boy held up a two-shilling piece, and said he had put it in the box in mistake for a penny,and was trying to get it back When he was handed over to ClonstabtA htwever, he admitted stealing the money. Prisoner's father told the bench his son was a bad lad, and that he had consulted Chief Constable Wood with a view to get- tine him sent to a reformatory. The boy•bad beeti the habit of meeting children sent on errandssand taking coppers off them. He pretended to wrap the money up in paper, but gave the paper back and puz the money in his nocket. The magistrates told the father he ought to be able to control so young a boy, and imposed a fine of ss, at the same time ordering the prisoner twelve strokes with the birch rod TRAVELLING WITHOUT A TICKET AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>A middle-aged woman named Ellen Taylor, of 246, Cas,le-hill, Elindley, was charged.before the St Helene magistrates on Tuesday with travelling beyond the dis- tance allowed by her tichet on the railway.' Detective Fisher, of the London aril North Western Railway Com- pany, said the woman tried to pass the barrier at Wigan Station on Monday night, saying she wanted to meet snme friends from S. Helens. She was refused admis- lion to the platform, and she then went and took a ti7.ket for Bryn, paying 3-11 for it, and boarded a train. The detective, however, was suspicious,and got into the name compartment, with the result that he found the pri,oner went through to St. Helens, and after loitering' about the o!atform there passed the ticket taken with the at t.ment that she had not come by train, but had been to en,: some friends off. Fisher then spoke to her, %And she persisted in her story until he asked her for the ticket, but insisted that a gentleman had given it to her. At last she admitted having come from Wigao and offAred to pay the difference. Vie detective's version of what took place at St. Helens was borne out by two porte:s. Prisoner pleaded not guilty, and said it was quite a mistake. She came to St. Helens to see her sister. It was the first time she had been before the magistrates in her life —A fine of 103 and coats was in- flicted, with the alternative of fourteen. days' imprison- ment.</p>
        <p>Sr. i3ELENS CONGRhGA.TIO NAL SUNDAY SCHOOL.--• UUFURLING THE NEW BANNER.</p>
        <p>An interesting ccrelon7 was performed at the St. Helens Congregational Sunday School on Mo nday after- when a magnificent new banner, which cost £25, was unfurled by Mrs. ularke, wife of the Rev. F. W. Clarke, 8.A., the pagtor. The cerernny had been . arranged to take place in the schoolyard prior to the scho'ars parading for their annual field day, but owi.,:g , the banner !was unfurled in ttohethie,irTeisearsasbelme b layv-eraotohme which was crowded wqh scholars and friends. Amongst those present were the 13.,v I W Clarke, 8.A., Mrs Clarke, Miss Clark, Messrs C Sharpies, J.P., S Glover, and W Webster (superinten- dents). the Rev. C. E Robinson. So. Mr. S. G'oYer ex- plained the reason they were met, and said it was to see their no w school banner unfurled,:and to show that they preferred to " process " under the banner of the Brook Street Sunday school. (Applause.) They had bei looking forward to having a new banner for some years, and a number of friends had been work og ht rd to raise the moncy to pay for it. Tney had now eot it, and he hoped they were all prepared to stand behind it ad see that the banner was paid for.—Mr. Sharpies sail th t he had walked with the old banner for at least twenty-five years. During the last four August Monday treats a number of their young friends had worked at the school stall to get the motley for the banner, and it was a very grrat disappointment for them not to be able to ger to the field that day. He had great pleasure in knig Mrs. Clarke to unfurl the hstiner.—Amid trp- inendous anplausr,Mrs. Clarke unfurled the banner,and M,,s E'sie Hewitt,presented Mrs. Clarke with a splendid b, ugnet of flowers on behalf of the Sunday school. Th 3 bin Der, which is a beautiful one of blue and gold silk, was gre.;tly admired by all presen', and is certainly a beautiful sample of Messes. Tutills' (Lond)n) wr.,rkman- ship On the one side are the words "St. Helens Con- gregational Sunday School.. Founded 1806,' and on tree reverse side is an open Bible, with the texts "Search the Scriptures" and "Thy word is a lamp unto my feet." —A vote of thanks to Mrs. Clarke terminated the function.</p>
        <p>When n.kinfz for Coeoa,finsist on having CADBURYS Bold only ic Packets and Tins as other Cocoas are often substi- tuted fcr the liake of extra profit.—Advt (1) PR1:1411EIt CYCLSS pries £9 17s 6.1.—J. S. GARLAND, Cycle Factor, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>It certainly seemi a little out of place to be talking of the Noi them Union Cup Ties before the season h is really commenced, but cJasiderahle interest will be taken in the list of draws for the Northilrn Union Cup which will be found in another column. In the first round to be played on March 21d, Warrington are drawn to play Leeds at Leeds. It is to early to speak of the merits of either team, but judging from past performances, the match will ho a close coratist and if Warrington show an improveinent in form this season they should certainly pull through this round. If they succeed in doing so, the next essay does not seem to be of such a formidable character, for :they will have the privilege of meeting Heelcmi.indwike or Seaton on their own ground. Pre- suming that they win this round they will oppose Liver- sedge, Nom:intim, Le. di Parish Church or Radcliffe. In the tourth round they will also have choice of grounds, which is a great advantage and should give them gold opportunity of mixing a bold bid for the cup tnia year. The ad important point is to win at Leeds and this the Warrington lads can no doubt accomplkh if they will only make up their minds to do so.</p>
        <p>The first practicl of the Warrington players will take place to-day (Saturday), a stx o'clock prompt. It is to he hoped that a good start will be made.</p>
        <p>A writer in a Manchester contemporary, commenting upon the draw for the Northern Union Cup Tie says : " Tne draw for the cup has not been as favourable to Lancashire clubs as was the case List season, when Swintoa,Salford, and Benughtnio Rangers were all drawn at home. This year they will all be away in the first round, and Swinton, the hAders, have a very powerful nut to crack in meetinz Bradford at Park Avenue. If they get through this they will run well up, for the in,st serious opponentb they will meet up to the fourth round wiil be Wilrefielii Trinity, alLhough they will have to play away cacti time. Tae runners-op, Salford, have been well drawn in former years, but the luck is against them this time, and Hull Kingston Rovers will have no easy task."</p>
        <p>Through with this, however, tho 1)13 Ar Barns conting- ent should run to the fourth round, their most serious opposition up to this stags b3ing Barrow or Brighouse Rangers. It will thus be seer' that if the two finalists of last year emerge successfully from the initial stage they may again play a leading part. Broughton Ringers go to Tyldesley,and should win this round as well as the second. Then in the third they are at home probibly against Rcendale Hornets or Oldham, so the Wheater's Field contingent will have to be smarter than they were last season if they are to figure prominently.</p>
        <p>Oldham and Roch lale Hornets should clash in the semnd round. Runcorn are perhaps botter drawn than auy other club in this district, and it seems as if they will henefi;.; as much from the cup ties this season as they did last. In any event they wilt start big favourites for the trophy, as the opening rounds should cause them little trouble. Commencing at home against Wigan, they next lace Leigh or Alverthorpe, also at Runcorn, and still continuing on theii own ground, face Halifax in the third round. rney will have a tussle after this.as they will clash aqainst St_ Helene, Batley, Huddersfield, or Flull,but up to tbis their march should be a triumphant one.</p>
        <p>If Widnes have anything like a team they should do well, for they are not badly drawn, and three rounds should go in their favour. Warrington are fairly placed for, if they m.i.nage to defeat Leeds away, they are then at home f it. what should be a victory over Eleckmond- wike, and in the third round they should ern ‘rge sue- cessFully. The; will hav choice of ground in the fourth match, which will gee them facing a strong team. bui; if the Wilderspool fifteen is anything like a decent one they should figure better than they have previously done.</p>
        <p>Ro :Male El orn:tts, 149igh, Barrow, Tyldesley,Lancaster and Morecambe are the only Lancashire clubs at home in the first rou: d, whilst ol the other hand Salford, War- rington, Wigan, Radcliffe, Broughton Rangers, Swinton, Whitchurch, St. Helens, Warmth, Oldham, and Widnes, are all away. The moss reinarkatle thing about the draw is that with two or three exceptions all the chief Yorkshire clubs are at home. Despite this they will have to show considerable improvement upon last sea- son if they are going to take the cup out of Lancashire.</p>
        <p>The disagreement between the Oldham Football Club Committee and SAM Willicns, the well-knewn three- quarter player, has at length been adjusted, and Williams signed on on Wednesday evening. Some others of the best of the old players among the back division have not yet signed for the coming season, but it is anticipated that they will be inclined to arrange the matters in dispute, now that Williams has done so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Cricket.</head>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>The largest gate which has assembled on the Arpley enclosure this slam, witnessed the encounter between Warringt ,n and Leyland on Stturday. The match was for the benefit of The wlis, the popular local professional. who has done invaluable service for the team. There was a disappointment in store for the spectators. Mold, the well known Lancashire player,who bad been announced to do duty for Warrington, failing to put in an appear- ance.</p>
        <p>Llls.nd came clown with an exceptionally strong e'even which included Sharp and Hibbert of Lancashire County fame, and H. D. Staining, of Cambridge University. Warrington placed an even weaker team than that which played against Bolton the previous week, in the field,Callon and Thompson being absentees. Against such a powerful eleven, Warrington could not have been expected to shine,and the result of the match was a victory for the visitors by 100 runs. Thewlis's innings realized 17 runs.</p>
        <p>Trio best batting digplays given by the Llyland side were those contributed by .1 Sharp and W 'Ebben, and the first named was heartily applauded for a beautifully played 65. included in which were two 6'a—splendid hits —and seven 4's. W Hibbert hit six 4's ; his innings being a stylish exhibition.</p>
        <p>H. D. Scanning, the youngest member of the family who have done so much for county-cricket showed ex- ceptional form for his 19, and bids fair to distinguish himself. The Leyland azgrega.te realised 165.</p>
        <p>Wa.rington started th, ir task with E. A. Connor and B. Siicock, but from the onset the locals never looked like savinc..i the match. Thew.is b ttted stealily for hi innings of 17, whilst G. Clarke made big 16 in merry style, but so deadly prf-,lrd the deliveries of Sharp, who took six wickets tor 32 runs, hitting the pegs on every instance, th it the _innings closed tor 65, or 103 runs in arrears.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Cycling.</head>
        <p>Cycling.</p>
        <p>A noteworthy feat was performed the other afternoon by a young man namel Laurence Lowe, of Headlingley, Leeds, who rode on a bicycle from that place, through Todrnorden, Rochdale, and Manchester to Stockton Heath in the face of a nGrth-west gale and heavy shower of rain. He covered the distance, a little over 68 miles, in about eight hoars. After a warm bath and a cop of steaming hot coffee, it is said, he appeared to be none the worse for hii exploit.</p>
        <p>Tho members of. the Orchid Cycling Club had a pleasant afternoon run on Saturday. Thor assembled at the residenco of Mr. R. A. Naylor and at 2.30 a start was made. The cyclists were accompanied by their esteemed patron and president, Mr R A Naylor and Miss Naylor ; Mr and Ms John Naylor and family, Beeston Tower; and Mr J Naylor, jun., Exet -r College, Ox- ford. The party proceeded through KnutGford along the Holmes Chapel-road to their destination, Woodside Farm, Cranage, where a subitantial tea was provided, arid thoroughly appreciated. Before tea rt delightful ramble through the woods was much enjoyed and afterwards games were indulged in. The return journey was safely accomplished after one of the most eni.yable runs of the season.</p>
        <p>The introducion into the House of Gammons of the National Cyclists' Union Bill, for the redress of certain grievances which cyclists suffer, has been watched with interest by local cyclists. The St. Helens Cyclists' Club has in fact categorically askel the local M P.'s to sup- ' port the hill, which, it will be remembered, has three main objec's rn compel the railway companies to afford greater facilities for the conveyance of cycles by rail as passengeis bag2al.fe ; to make the companies responsible for damage by negligence ; and to substi- tute for the present unreasonably high scale of charges a mere registration fee of 31 for distances under fifty miles. and GI for any distance over that Ii nit. The bill has been introluced by Sir Howard Vincent and hacked by Mr. Herbert Gladstone, and the St. Helens c7elists have been careful to point out to their M.P.'s that the measure is a strictly non-party one. Replies to the clnh's communications have been received from Colon,l Richard Pilkingt.on,M..P. for the Newton Division and from Mr. fi Set )a-Karr,the member for St. Helens boroach. rho former has written briefly acknowledging the letter sent hirn,and saying that the till has his csre fui attention. Mr. Seton-Karr, who is himself a cyclist and a member of the Sr. Helens C.C., has replied as follows :—"I entirely sympathise with the object of the bill, and will ei•r:ainly support it if it COM9B on ; hit I very much fear it will no now come on this session ' owing to the pressure of Government business. Perhaps we can get it in another session."</p>
        <p>How some rner; come so near t) and yet mi4s a fortune often furnishes interesting reading. There is a bicycle exhibited in a Gloucegter cycle maker'A office which is claimed to be the original of the modern safety. It has small wheels about the S'Z9 of the present-day bike, and the rear or driving wheel is geared to 45fn., the weight of the machine bqng 8013 F., and it is 24 years of age. The inventor, George &amp;ergo% was a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>LATEST NEWS.</head>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>CHINA.</p>
        <p>Heavy Russian Losses.</p>
        <p>The Press Association telegraphs (Tientsin, August 5) :—The allied troops advanced towards Peitsang yesterday. The British, Japanese, and Americans moved along the right bank of the river, while the Russians, Germans, and French operated on the other side in the direction of the railway. The whole force bivouacked for the night. At half-past three this morning the engagement commenced. After several hours of severe fighting the Chinese were driven from their entrencnments.</p>
        <p>The Russians lost 500 killed and the British fifty. Germans and Japanese a 159 lost heavily, but their exact casualties and those of the French are un- known. The road to Pekin is supposed to be open.</p>
        <p>Funeral of the Late King of Italy. Exciting Incident.</p>
        <p>ROME, THURSDAY 7 A.M.</p>
        <p>The train:lbearing the remain s ofjthe late King Humbert arrived at 6.30 this morning. The body, which was accompanied among others by the Duke of Aosta,the Count of Turin,Prince Victor Napoleon and the Duke of Oporto. was received at the station by the King, the Italian and foreign Princes, the members of the various foreign missions, the diplomatic body, and the chief authorities. An exciting incident occurred d aring the progress of the cortege. In the Rue Nazi3nale there was a sudden movement among tlae crowd, apparently caused by some sort of panic. Several people were badly crushed and injured. This occurred just as the carriage of the King was passing. The first thoughc when the officers heard the or ies of the in- jured was that the King was in danger. The Duo d'Aosta and the Count of Turin immediately drew their swords and the cuirassiers formed a square round the King's carriage. YESTERDAY'S CRICKET.</p>
        <p>Rain interfered with all the County cricket matches yesterday the only match which was commenced being Kent v. Surrey,and this was abandonedlwhen Kent were 17 for no wickets. The following ma'clses were also abandoned :—Lancashire v E isex,Warwick- shire v Yorkshire, Somersetshire v Sussex, Glouces- tershire v Middlesex, Leicestershire v Hampshire, Worcestershire v Derbyshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>Supposed Capture of a Garrison.</p>
        <p>The following telegram from Lord Roberts has been received by the Secretary for war :</p>
        <p>PRETORIA, Bth AUGUST.</p>
        <p>Kitchener received information early ycsterday morning from a British prisoner who escaped from the Boer camp that De ;Wet's waggons had crossed the Vaal.</p>
        <p>Shortly afterwards the sound of guns was heard, which I think must have been Methuen, as I had directed him to take up a p9sition between Potchef- stroom and Lindeque, where he should have been able to intercept the.enemy, , who crossed the river at De Wet's Drift.</p>
        <p>Kitchener is crossing the Va at wish the cavalry and wounted infantry. Hunter reports that 4,140 133ers were made prisoners in the Bethiehen-i and Harri smith districts. The great majerity are now on their way to Cape town, for despatch to Ceylon. Of the three guns wilioh were ea ptured two be- longed to "II" Battery, R.R.A. Over 4,000 horses and pales were captured.</p>
        <p>Ten waggon loads of ammunition were destroyed at Naauwpoort, 180,000:rounds in the Calcdon Valley and 15,000 rounds at Fouriesburg. The garrison at Elands River has, I fear, been captured. It consists of about 140 bushmdn, 80 Rhodesians, and the same number of Rhode3ian Volunteers. '</p>
        <p>I hoped that Carrington would have been in time to withdraw the Eands River Garrison, but it seems that Commandant Thlarev, on hearing t,f lan Hamilton's approach to Rustenburg, must have hurried off westward awl surrounded the little garrison before Carrington could arrive there. In a telegram just received Methuen reports that he engaged a part of D 3 Wet's force yesterday morn- ing near Venters Kroom. _ _</p>
        <p>He drove the enemy off a eu eceseion of hills, which they held with great obstinacy.</p>
        <p>He says that Scots Fusi:iers and Welsh Fusiliers behaved splendidly. Our casualties were : Killed, seven men ; woundel, Imperial Yalmanry, Lieutenant Gurney, severely; Welsh Fuailiers, Captain Gough slightly, Second-Lieutenant Bayley severely, Cap Cain Barte- lot slightly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Boers Only Eight Miles from</head>
        <p>Boers Only Eight Miles from</p>
        <p>Pretoria.</p>
        <p>A Laffan's telegram reporti thst the Boers are only eight miles north of Pretoria, and nightly ven- ture close to our outposts.</p>
        <p>A Runcorn Volunteer Captured.</p>
        <p>Among the list of caPualties with the Fredrickstad railway accident on the 30tti July, published on Friday, appeared the name of SPrgeant Jeacolk, who was re- ported as having been talr,m prisoner. Tne last news which was received Of Sergeant Jeacnck by his Runcorn friends was that he was doing pike duty at Johannes burg.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Another Local Volunteer Homeward</head>
        <p>Another Local Volunteer Homeward</p>
        <p>Bound.</p>
        <p>The following are extracts of letters received from Private J. Loundes, of the Warrington Volunteer Com- pany in South Africa, who is now on his way home as an invalid i—Mooi Myer Hospital, July 6th. Dear father and mother,—No doubt it will seem a long time since you received a letter from me. At first we had scarcely any time for a wash, never mind writing. Ws left Jenona's linp on May 13th at 6.30 p.m.; marched about nine miles to a place called Woodcote Farm. bivouacked for the rest of the night and again scatted without breakfast at six next meraieg, We marched about 16 miles that day, crossing Sunday River. We leaded our magazines, as we had to attack the Boers at Weasel's Nek. It was a forced march and several dropped out of line through the intense heat. When we got to Wessel's Nek, however, the Boers had gone. We stayed there for the night. The regiment marched away next morning, lervine us hehinci to escort the naval guns, so we had another night's rest. While we were there we found two orange trees. We had a "heno." We then marched to Wasch Bank Farm, and stayed there for the night. We were lup again at 1.30 a m. and marched to Glencoe, staying there two hours, then off aeain to flatting'a Spruit, 'h n to Dienhauser, halted for dinner and marched to a o,e° called lugagarne. Here we found that the big bridge had been blown down, so they kept Us there three da}:, making a temporary bridge across the river. We after- wards marched to Newcastleestayed there one night, and marched into the Transvaal, crossing Baffalo River to Ihrecht, on one aide of Majuba Hill. wi,ere I took fever. [hey kept me at Newcastle Hospital for a week, then I went to Ladysmith General Hospital, where I eee a little better. Subsequently I was sent to Mnoi River Hospital where I am now. Don't be alarmed. for the fever has left me and lam getting all right. It is a e rand hospital, they are feeding me up like a "fighting rock." I need it after 41 eal s on nothing but milk. They are feeding me on st?wed chicken, two eggs, one tin of condensed milk, one bottle of soda water one pot el Liebig's beef tea, and bread and butter. This is</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>ANNUAL HOLIDAY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>THE ANNUAL HOLIDAY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>For the first time, the whoo!e of the works in Earls- town will close next week, for what will in future be considered the annual summer holiday. It will be re- membered that some months ago an agitation was started amongst the workmen for the institution of an annual general holid ,v, because of the discontinuance of the race meeting at Newton, which for many years was considered and observed as a holiday by all the works. Deputations waited upon the various works' managers, with the result that it was decided ta close all the works for four days, commencing on the evening of August 14th. From this arrangement the Vulcan Foundry and Messrs. Slee and Co. have departed, with the result that their respective works will be closed from to-night (Friday) until nex Monday week the 20th inst. The Viaduct Works and Messrs. T. and T. Vicars will abide by the arrangement originally arrived at and close on Tuesday night next for the remainder of the week. There is certain to be a large exodus from the town, and in preparation for the holidays, going away clubs have been started at some ot the works, and it is said that many of the men will have quite a respe3table sum to draw. The Eirlestown Co-operative Society have also held their (partedy meeting a fortnight earlier.in order that dividend might be paid out before the holidays, which will undoubtedly prove a great boon to scores of people. The holiday is looked forward to with pleasur- able anticipation, and if the weather be of a favourable character, there is certain to be a very large exodus of people. Although th's year there is a lack of unanimity, as regards the length of the holiday, it is confidently ex- pected by the man that next year all the works will c!ose for a full week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>WARRINGTON CORPORATION GAS</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CORPORATION GAS</p>
        <p>DEPARTMENT.</p>
        <p>Annual Report.</p>
        <p>Mr. William S. Haddock in submitting his twelfth annual report for the year ending March 31st last, says :---(` The gas buildings and manufacturing plant have been kept in a good state of repair. The average price of coal and cannel for the present year is nearly 50 per cent of an advance upon last year's prices. The increased receipts from residual products owing to better prices and the large in- crease in our output of gas have enabled us to carry on the works with profit. In the contracts entered into for next year's supply of coal and cannel the prices are again advanced 50 per cent upon this year's price or rather over double the price we paid for last year's supply. By postponing the renewals and repairs of build- ings and plant where not absolutely necessary, to gt,ther with increased receipts from residuals it will probably enable us to carry on the works for the coming year without loss. An early reduction in the price of gas was promised last year, but owing to the present inflated prices for raw material it is not advisable t.) make any alterations at present The increased make of gas is 32,034,300 cubic feet or 10.6 per cent upon last year's production." Fr om a return showing the makes and con gumption of gas, it appears that the largest quan- tity sent out on a single day was on December Ist when 1,757,000 cubic feet were distributed. The largest quantity sent out in a single week was 10,491,700 cubic feet during the week ending December 24, and the smallest 3,132,800 during the week ending July 17. The smallest consumption of gag during a single day was on June 11 when only 287,100 cubic feet were sent out.</p>
        <p>The average price of coal and cannel during the year was 103 511 per ton as against 7s lid per ton in the previous year. The average illuminating power of the gap: is given at 18.41 candles. The wages cost per 1,000 cubic feet of ens made was 9.985 d as compared with 9.7421 in 1898-9. The total expenditure. including wages, during the year was ..E51,327 lie 104 and the estimated receipts £67.938 8s 41, showing a handsome profit of £16,610 63 64.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-10_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>.....e91,,...• SCHOLARSHIPS Ft)R WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>.....e91,,...• SCHOLARSHIPS Ft)R WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>ELEMENTARY SCHOLARS</p>
        <p>The Museum, Library, Arts and Technic II Instruction Commictee have approved of the following sehems for the Coa.rles Middleton scholarships, and have decided that two juhior and one senior Charles Middleton scholarships be offered this year. Senior Scholarship. sonior scholarship shall be of tho amount of £9 for the first year. £l2 for the secohd, and £l5 for the third year, with day feos at the Warrington School of Art, v.z., £3 each year in addition. Canoidates must have been educated at a public elementary school in Warringron, and be under 17 years of age at first appo:ntment. Scholars shall bo appointed for one year only, but may be re-appointed for a second and for a third year. at the discre'ion of the trustees. The ccholarship shall be awarded on the result of the Art Ex iminations of the Board of E location, and the scholar shall satisfy the Trustees, before app,dorment,as to the arrangement pr iposed to be m %de for attending elacses7in history, literarure, and languages, and shall attend the School of Art three days weekly during the session (September to July).</p>
        <p>Junior Scholarships. A Julior Scholarahip vlail be of the amount of £4 for the fist year, £5 for the s ,cond, and £6 for the third, with the Evening Class fees at the Warrington School of Art each year, in addition. Scholars shall be boys who have been in regular at'en- dance in a public elementary school in Warrington for not less than twelve months at first appointment, and who have passel the sixth st indard as i..rescribed by the Code of the Board of Education. The scholarship shall be awarded on the result of a special examination held at the School of Art. Scholars shall attend three evenings weekly at the School of Art during the srssion (September to June), and also attend re4ularly an approved day school. Scholars shall be appointed for one year only, but may be re-appointed, at the discretion of the Trwitees, for a sec',nd and for a third year, if still under 16 years of age. _</p>
        <p>_ 'I he SgCRETARY alp() reported to tin Committee that a gift of £5 per annum for three years had been offered by Mr. F W Monks for a local scholarship in Art for girl, It was resolved that the he irty thanks of the Com- mittee be given to Ms. Monks for his kind offer.</p>
        <p>The Field Club.</p>
        <p>By the kindness of the owners, the members were permitted on Saturday to visit Rixton NIOAS and Rixton Firs under the leadership of Mr E E Lowe The afternoon was bright and fine, and the ramble most enjoyable, though as there was a strong westerly breeze there were not many insects on the wing, and consequently, from a collector's point of view, the conditions were not exactly ideal. Nevertheless some good insects were taken, includ- ing cnrcia imbuta,</p>
