        <p>Glouces,er shoemaker. To-day he is 76 years of age, a poor old man who has little prospect of ending his days even in certif)rt. It is the cruel doing of fate that the man who first conceived an idea which has benefitee' mankind, arid out of which millions of pounds have been made, should thus end his days. Foot Racing. At the Marton sports, on Monday, T. Devlin, of War- rington (185 yards), came in first in the two miles handicap race. Holmes, of the Sefton Harriers, was second. The race was run through a drenching thunder- storm by seven competitors. EARLtSTOWN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY. The 159th quarterly meeting of the above society was held in the Co-operative Hall on Tuesday evening. Mr. D. Forrester presided, and there was a poor attendance of members. The report and balance sheet showed the cash receipts for the quarter to be £9.578 14s 7d, being an increase over list quarter of £4O 7s 11d. and £1,047 193 51 over the corresponding period last year. The nett profits realised were £1,375 19; 33 to which it was pro- pased to add £39 7,3 31 from Dividend Reserve Fund, and pay a dividend of 3s in the £to members, The reduced profit was said to be mainly due to a general advance in the cost of household commodities while, in several instances, there had been no corresponding in- crease in retail prices. During the quarter 48 new mem berg hid been admitted, and 27 had withdrawn, leaving 1,573 on the register of whom 1,131 Dad brought in checks. Ti accounts were adopted, Messrs. W. Clare and G. Goff were re-elected without opposition to the Committee of Management, and Messrs. Parr and Mullins were nominated for election next quarter. Mr. P Goodie' was re-appointed auditor. Mr. W. Gilmour submitted his report as members' delegate to the quarterly meeting of the Co-operative Wnolesate S;ciety which was adopted, and he was r appointed for another quarter. The question of the annual tea party was brought up, but the voting for and against, teiog equal it was left with toe Committee t) decide. The Chairman having stated that there were numer— ous requests for the erection of cottages by the Society, particulars as to the sites were submitted, but there being a eiffereace of opinion as to the Cia9El of house mostly needed, it was decided :o leave the question over for three months. This was all the business.</p>
